/* chat-kb-bg.js — generated by tools/chat/build_kb.py from tools/chat/background/*.md. Do not edit by hand.
   The Professor’s background notes: related reading that is not a page on this site.
   site-chat.js loads this only when the site’s own pages don’t answer a question. */
window.TOL_CHAT_BG = {"v":1,"docs":[{"id":"anatomy-of-an-apology","t":"Anatomy of an apology","a":["parts of an apology","how to apologize","good apology","what makes an apology"],"x":["Apologies that work tend to include a clear “I’m sorry”, naming what you did, taking responsibility (without “but”), and saying what you’ll do differently or how you’ll make it right. An explanation can help, as long as it doesn’t turn into an excuse.","Short and sincere usually beats long and elaborate. The other person needs to hear that you understand how it landed."],"p":"The situation advice for repairing after a rough moment includes ready-to-use repair lines. The Library’s apology entry goes deeper.","go":["/library/conflict.html#apology-research","What makes an apology work (Library)"],"see":["non-apologies","apology-at-work","self-forgiveness"],"ev":"A study by Roy Lewicki and colleagues (2016) looked at six parts of an apology and found acknowledging responsibility and offering to repair mattered most. Other research varies in which parts matter most.","ex":"“I’m sorry I snapped at you. That was my stress, not your fault. Next time I’ll say I need a minute instead.”","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"non-apologies","t":"Non-apologies","a":["sorry you feel that way","non apology","fake apology","sorry but"],"x":["Some apologies sound like apologies but shift the blame: “I’m sorry you feel that way”, “I’m sorry if you were offended”, “I’m sorry, but you started it”. They often make things worse, because the other person feels unheard a second time.","A real apology is about what you did, not about how they reacted."],"p":"WP-09’s fact, feeling and ask can help: name the fact of what you did.","go":["/library/conflict.html#apology-research","What makes an apology work (Library)"],"see":["anatomy-of-an-apology","over-apologizing"],"ev":"Communication researchers and etiquette writers widely describe these patterns. They tend to increase frustration rather than repair.","ex":"Swap “I’m sorry you felt hurt” for “I’m sorry I said that. It was hurtful.”","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"over-apologizing","t":"Over-apologizing","a":["saying sorry too much","apologizing constantly","sorry for everything"],"x":["Some people apologize for everything: taking up space, asking questions, having needs. Over time, it can make real apologies mean less, and it can reflect a habit of making yourself small.","Swapping some “sorries” for thank-yous (“thanks for your patience”) keeps the warmth without the self-blame."],"p":"Know your own wiring helps you notice patterns you’ve learned, which are among the things that can change.","go":["/know-yourself.html","Know your own wiring"],"see":["non-apologies","self-talk"],"ev":"Many people report apologizing reflexively. It’s commonly discussed in communication coaching; research on its effects is limited.","ex":"Swap “Sorry I’m late!” for “Thanks for waiting!”","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"apologizing-to-kids","t":"Apologizing to kids","a":["apologizing to children","parents saying sorry","sorry to my kid"],"x":["When parents apologize to children, they teach that everyone makes mistakes and that repair is part of love. It doesn’t weaken authority. It shows how to take responsibility.","Keep it simple and age-appropriate, and follow through on what you say you’ll do differently."],"p":"Repair matters more than never slipping. The family road includes a plan for heated moments (WP-11).","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#family","Where families start"],"see":["anatomy-of-an-apology","emotion-coaching"],"ev":"Parenting experts widely recommend that parents apologize when they get it wrong, modeling repair for children.","ex":"“I’m sorry I yelled. You didn’t deserve that. I was tired, and I’m going to try to take a breath next time.”","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"apology-at-work","t":"Apology at work","a":["apologizing at work","professional apology","sorry to a colleague"],"x":["At work, a good apology is prompt, specific and focused on fixing things: what happened, that you own it, and what you’re doing next. It doesn’t need to be emotional.","Pair it with a plan to prevent a repeat, so the apology builds trust rather than just ending the awkwardness."],"p":"The coworker road suggests keeping repair quick and professional.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#coworkers","Where coworkers and teams start"],"see":["anatomy-of-an-apology","receiving-criticism-at-work"],"ev":"Research on workplace apologies suggests timely, specific apologies help repair trust. Over-apologizing can undermine confidence.","ex":"“I’m sorry I missed the deadline. Here’s what happened and what I’m doing to make sure it doesn’t happen again.”","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"receiving-an-apology","t":"Receiving an apology","a":["accepting an apology","responding to sorry","how to respond to an apology"],"x":["When someone apologizes, you don’t have to pretend everything is fine right away. Acknowledging the apology (“thank you for saying that”) respects their effort, even if you still need time.","If it was a small thing and you’re ready, a warm acceptance helps both of you move on."],"p":"The Library’s forgiveness entry explains how forgiveness differs from forgetting or excusing.","go":["/library/conflict.html#forgiveness","Forgiveness (Library)"],"see":["anatomy-of-an-apology","self-forgiveness"],"ev":"Research on forgiveness suggests accepting an apology is a process; people may need time. It’s okay not to be ready right away.","ex":"“Thank you for saying that. I need a little time, but I really appreciate it.”","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"self-forgiveness","t":"Self-forgiveness","a":["forgiving yourself","self forgiveness","letting go of mistakes"],"x":["After a mistake, some people stay stuck in guilt long after making amends. Self-forgiveness means accepting responsibility and then letting yourself move forward.","It isn’t pretending it didn’t happen. It’s choosing to learn from it rather than punish yourself indefinitely."],"p":"The Library’s self-compassion entry goes deeper. The program’s whole approach is about fixing the setup, not punishing the person, including yourself.","go":["/library/connection.html#self-compassion","Self-compassion (Library)"],"see":["anatomy-of-an-apology","self-talk"],"ev":"Research on self-forgiveness suggests it works best when combined with taking responsibility, rather than simply letting yourself off the hook.","ex":"“I got that wrong, I’ve made it right as best I can, and I’m allowed to move on.”","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"apology-styles","t":"Apology styles","a":["apology languages","five languages of apology","different kinds of apology"],"x":["People may value different parts of an apology: some most want to hear regret, others want responsibility taken, others want to see things made right or changed.","Asking what matters most to someone, rather than guessing, helps your apologies land."],"p":"Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive: an apology is a message too, and it lands differently for different people.","go":["/library/conflict.html#apology-research","What makes an apology work (Library)"],"see":["anatomy-of-an-apology","love-languages-caveats"],"ev":"Gary Chapman and Jennifer Thomas described “five languages of apology” in The Five Languages of Apology (2006). It’s a popular framework with little research testing it.","ex":"Ask: “When I’ve upset you, what helps most: hearing I’m sorry, hearing I understand, or seeing me fix it?”","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"specific-thanks","t":"Specific thanks","a":["specific thank you","how to thank someone","meaningful thanks"],"x":["Specific thanks names what the person did, the effort it took, and what it meant to you. It’s more meaningful than a quick “thanks!” because it shows you noticed.","It takes a few more seconds and often has a much bigger effect."],"p":"Turning Toward’s daily idea is often one specific thank-you. The daily check-in includes one thing you appreciated.","go":["/turning-toward.html","Turning Toward"],"see":["thanking-for-invisible-work","gratitude-letters"],"ev":"Research on gratitude in relationships (for example, Sara Algoe’s work) suggests thanks that notice the other person’s effort and care are especially meaningful.","ex":"“Thank you for sorting out the car insurance. I know it took ages on the phone, and it’s a weight off my mind.”","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"gratitude-letters","t":"Gratitude letters","a":["gratitude letter","gratitude visit","thank you letter"],"x":["A gratitude letter describes, in detail, what someone did for you and how it affected your life. Delivering it can be moving for both people.","It works well for friends, family, teachers or colleagues, and for partners in long relationships where thanks has faded."],"p":"The Library’s gratitude entry covers the research. Pillar V: thanks keeps things steady.","go":["/library/connection.html#gratitude","Gratitude (Library)"],"see":["specific-thanks","three-good-things"],"ev":"Martin Seligman and colleagues (2005) found writing and delivering a gratitude letter boosted happiness for a short time. Later studies generally find small, positive effects.","ex":"Write a letter to someone who helped you, and read it to them or send it.","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"three-good-things","t":"Three good things","a":["three good things","gratitude journal","counting blessings"],"x":["Writing down three good things each day trains attention toward what’s going well, which is easy to miss in busy lives.","The effects are modest but real for many people. Doing it together, as a family or couple, can be a sweet ritual."],"p":"Noticing what’s working is part of the daily check-in: one thing you appreciated.","go":["/library/connection.html#gratitude","Gratitude (Library)"],"see":["gratitude-letters","specific-thanks"],"ev":"Seligman and colleagues (2005) found writing down three good things each day had positive effects on wellbeing. Research on gratitude journaling (for example, Emmons and McCullough, 2003) shows modest benefits.","ex":"Before bed, write down three small good things from today, and why each happened.","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"gratitude-without-toxic-positivity","t":"Gratitude without toxic positivity","a":["toxic positivity","forced gratitude","be grateful"],"x":["Gratitude helps when it’s chosen, not imposed. Telling someone to “just be grateful” when they’re struggling can feel dismissive.","It’s possible to be grateful and struggling at the same time. Both deserve room."],"p":"The Library’s validation entry explains why acknowledging feelings matters.","go":["/library/communication.html#validation-in-conversation","Validation (Library)"],"see":["specific-thanks","validation-before-solutions"],"ev":"“Toxic positivity” is a popular term rather than a research concept, but research on invalidation suggests dismissing negative feelings can make people feel worse.","ex":"Instead of “At least you have a job,” try “That sounds really hard. I’m sorry.”","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"validation-before-solutions","t":"Validation before solutions","a":["validate first","acknowledge before fixing"],"x":["Before offering solutions, acknowledging the feeling (“that sounds frustrating”) helps people feel heard. Solutions offered too early can feel like dismissal.","Validation isn’t agreement. It’s recognizing that the feeling makes sense from where they stand."],"p":"The Library’s validation entry and the Check-ins guide’s “hear it back” step both build on this.","go":["/library/communication.html#validation-in-conversation","Validation (Library)"],"see":["listening-mode-or-fixing-mode","gratitude-without-toxic-positivity"],"ev":"Research on validation suggests acknowledging someone’s feelings helps them calm down and feel understood.","ex":"“That makes sense. Anyone would be frustrated.” Then, if they want: “Want to think about what to do?”","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"love-languages-caveats","t":"Love languages caveats","a":["love languages","five love languages","love language"],"x":["The love languages idea (words, time, gifts, acts of service, touch) is a helpful prompt for talking about what makes people feel cared for. The research, though, suggests most people value several, and matching languages doesn’t predict happiness much.","Use it as a conversation starter, not a label."],"p":"The Library’s love languages entry covers the research in more detail.","go":["/library/relationships.html#love-languages","Love languages (Library)"],"see":["apology-styles","specific-thanks"],"ev":"Gary Chapman’s The Five Love Languages (1992) is very popular. Research testing it has found limited support for the idea of one main “language” per person.","ex":"Instead of one “language”, ask: “What made you feel most cared for this week?”","f":"apologies-and-gratitude"},{"id":"self-serving-bias","t":"Self-serving bias","a":["self serving bias","credit for wins blame for losses"],"x":["We tend to take credit when things go well and point to circumstances when they don’t. In shared lives, this means each person often sees their own contributions as bigger and their own slips as more excusable.","It’s normal, not a character flaw. Knowing about it makes it easier to give others the same benefit of the doubt you give yourself."],"p":"The Library’s entry on why we each think we do more is closely related. Writing things down in WP-01 helps correct for it.","go":["/library/fairness.html#egocentric-bias","Why we each think we do more (Library)"],"see":["owning-your-part","fundamental-attribution-error-at-home"],"ev":"One of the best-documented findings in social psychology: people tend to credit themselves for successes and blame circumstances for failures.","ex":"“When the dinner went well, I thought it was my planning. When it flopped, I blamed the oven. Hmm.”","f":"biases"},{"id":"fundamental-attribution-error-at-home","t":"Fundamental attribution error at home","a":["character versus circumstances","they’re just lazy","blaming personality"],"x":["We tend to explain other people’s behavior by their character (“she’s lazy”) and our own by circumstances (“I was exhausted”). At home, this turns setup problems into character judgments.","Asking “what about the situation might explain this?” often reveals a fixable cause, like no clear owner, bad timing or overload."],"p":"This is why the program says: fix the setup, not the person (Pillar II).","go":["/library/thinking.html#fundamental-attribution-error","Fundamental attribution error (Library)"],"see":["self-serving-bias","assume-positive-intent"],"ev":"A classic finding in social psychology (Lee Ross named it in 1977), though how strong and universal it is has been debated.","ex":"Instead of “He’s so careless,” ask: “What in his day might have made this slip?”","f":"biases"},{"id":"sunk-cost-thinking","t":"Sunk cost thinking","a":["sunk cost","sunk cost fallacy","we’ve already put so much in"],"x":["Sunk cost thinking means sticking with something because of what you’ve already put in (time, money, effort), even when it no longer makes sense.","In households, it can keep a failing system in place (“but we spent a whole Sunday making that chart”). The better question is: from here, what works best?"],"p":"The monthly look-back (WP-04) asks you to fix the setup, not keep patching it. Changing a setup that isn’t working is a win.","go":["/library/thinking.html","Thinking traps (Library)"],"see":["status-quo-bias","reversible-and-irreversible-decisions"],"ev":"The sunk cost effect is well documented in decision research (for example, Arkes and Blumer, 1985), though people vary in how strongly they show it.","ex":"“We’ve done the chore chart for months, but it’s clearly not working. Let’s change it anyway.”","f":"biases"},{"id":"status-quo-bias","t":"Status quo bias","a":["status quo","sticking with how things are","resistance to change"],"x":["We tend to prefer things as they are, even when a change might be better. Existing arrangements feel safe and familiar, and any change feels like giving something up.","In shared homes, it keeps old splits in place long after life has changed: a new job, a baby, an illness."],"p":"WP-03 says to change agreements on purpose at the weekly closing, not by drift. Pillar V is about noticing these quiet pulls.","go":["/five-pillars.html#quiet-incentives","Pillar V: Notice the quiet incentives"],"see":["defaults-at-home","sunk-cost-thinking"],"ev":"Status quo bias (Samuelson and Zeckhauser, 1988) is well documented: people tend to prefer the current state of affairs, partly because changes feel like losses.","ex":"“We’ve always split it this way, but let’s check whether it still fits our lives now.”","f":"biases"},{"id":"loss-aversion","t":"Loss aversion","a":["loss aversion","losses loom larger","fear of losing"],"x":["Losses often feel bigger than equal gains. Giving up a job you like, or some free time, can feel heavier than the benefit you get in exchange.","In negotiation at home, it helps to acknowledge what each person is giving up, not just what they’re gaining."],"p":"When trading jobs in WP-03, say what each person is letting go of, and thank them for it.","go":["/library/thinking.html","Thinking traps (Library)"],"see":["framing-gains-and-losses","status-quo-bias"],"ev":"A core idea in prospect theory (Kahneman and Tversky, 1979). Its size and generality have been debated in recent research, but the basic pattern is widely observed.","ex":"Giving up “my” Saturday morning can feel bigger than gaining a free Sunday afternoon, even if it’s the same time.","f":"biases"},{"id":"hindsight-bias","t":"Hindsight bias","a":["hindsight","I knew it all along","should have seen it coming"],"x":["After something goes wrong, it often seems obvious in hindsight: “you should have known”. But at the time, the information may not have been clear at all.","Hindsight bias makes us harsher on others and ourselves. Asking “what did we actually know at the time?” is fairer."],"p":"Blameless reviews (in the Library’s teams section) are designed to counter hindsight when looking back at what went wrong.","go":["/library/thinking.html","Thinking traps (Library)"],"see":["outcome-bias","self-forgiveness"],"ev":"Hindsight bias is well documented (Baruch Fischhoff’s research in the 1970s): once we know an outcome, we think it was more predictable than it was.","ex":"“It’s easy to say we should have left earlier now that we know about the traffic.”","f":"biases"},{"id":"outcome-bias","t":"Outcome bias","a":["judging by results","outcome bias","good decision bad outcome"],"x":["We tend to judge a decision as good or bad based on how it turned out, rather than on whether it was sensible given what was known. A good decision can have a bad outcome through bad luck, and vice versa.","In shared decisions, separating the quality of the choice from the result keeps blame out of it."],"p":"The Library’s blameless reviews entry shows how teams look at the process rather than the person.","go":["/library/teams.html#blameless-reviews","Blameless reviews (Library)"],"see":["hindsight-bias","reversible-and-irreversible-decisions"],"ev":"Research (for example, Baron and Hershey, 1988) shows people judge decisions by their outcomes even when the outcome was partly luck.","ex":"“Booking the cheap flight was reasonable. The strike wasn’t anyone’s fault.”","f":"biases"},{"id":"recency-bias","t":"Recency bias","a":["recency effect","last week colors everything","recent memory"],"x":["What happened recently feels more important than what happened a while ago. One rough week can overshadow a good month, and one good gesture can overshadow weeks of imbalance.","Looking at a longer stretch, ideally written down, gives a fairer picture."],"p":"Chapter II says it plainly: look at the month, not last night. The monthly look-back (WP-04) uses four weeks of logs.","go":["/book/chapter-2.html","Chapter II: Is the split working?"],"see":["memory-drift-after-an-argument","hindsight-bias"],"ev":"Research on memory shows recent events are easier to recall and often get extra weight in judgments.","ex":"One bad week of chores can make it feel like “you never help”, even after a good month.","f":"biases"},{"id":"memory-drift-after-an-argument","t":"Memory drift after an argument","a":["remembering it differently","conflicting memories","we remember it differently"],"x":["Two people often remember the same argument quite differently, and both can be sincere. Memory rebuilds events each time we recall them, shaped by feelings and later conversations.","Arguing about whose memory is correct rarely helps. Focusing on what each person felt, and what to do next time, usually works better."],"p":"This is why WP-01 says to write down the week before it turns into a story.","go":["/library/thinking.html#memory-reconstruction","Memory reconstruction (Library)"],"see":["recency-bias","written-recaps-after-a-talk"],"ev":"Memory research shows memories are reconstructed each time we recall them, and emotional events are especially prone to shifting details.","ex":"“I think we remember that night differently. Instead of arguing about who’s right, can we agree what we want next time?”","f":"biases"},{"id":"just-world-thinking","t":"Just-world thinking","a":["just world belief","they must have deserved it","everything happens for a reason"],"x":["Many of us like to believe the world is fair, so people get what they deserve. It’s comforting, but it can make us blame people for problems that aren’t their fault, including overload they didn’t choose.","Remembering that a lot of life is circumstance helps us respond with more compassion."],"p":"Pillar II reminds you to look at the setup. The Library’s thinking-traps entries go deeper.","go":["/library/thinking.html","Thinking traps (Library)"],"see":["fundamental-attribution-error-at-home","assume-positive-intent"],"ev":"The “belief in a just world” was described by Melvin Lerner in the 1960s and 70s. It can lead people to blame others for misfortunes outside their control.","ex":"“Maybe she’s behind at work not because she’s disorganized, but because she was handed two jobs.”","f":"biases"},{"id":"projection","t":"Projection","a":["projecting","assuming they feel what I feel"],"x":["Sometimes we assume other people feel what we’d feel in their place, or we read our own mood into their words. If you’re irritated, a neutral text can seem irritated too.","Checking (“are you upset, or am I reading into it?”) clears up a lot of mix-ups."],"p":"WP-09 asks: would this read the same if you weren’t already stretched?","go":["/library/thinking.html#false-consensus","False consensus (Library)"],"see":["false-consensus-at-home","clarifying-questions"],"ev":"In everyday use, projection means assuming others share our feelings or motives. Research on false consensus supports the general tendency; the classic psychoanalytic idea is more debated.","ex":"“I’m assuming you’re annoyed because I would be. Are you?”","f":"biases"},{"id":"false-consensus-at-home","t":"False consensus at home","a":["everyone would agree","normal people do it this way","obviously"],"x":["We tend to think our own way of doing things is the normal way. Different families have different habits about cleaning, money, time and holidays, and each feels “obvious” to the people raised in it.","Recognizing that your normal is one of many makes it easier to agree on a shared way instead of arguing about the right way."],"p":"The Library’s family scripts entry covers habits we bring from our families.","go":["/library/thinking.html#false-consensus","False consensus (Library)"],"see":["projection","curse-of-knowledge-in-instructions"],"ev":"The false consensus effect (Ross, Greene and House, 1977) is well documented: we overestimate how much others share our views and habits.","ex":"“I assumed everyone washes pans straight away. Clearly that’s just my family’s way.”","f":"biases"},{"id":"curse-of-knowledge-in-instructions","t":"Curse of knowledge in instructions","a":["curse of knowledge","obvious to me","unclear instructions"],"x":["When you know how something is done, it’s easy to give instructions that leave out the steps that seem obvious to you. Then the other person does it “wrong”, and both feel frustrated.","Spelling out what “done” means (once, kindly) often solves recurring “you did it wrong” arguments."],"p":"WP-03 can include a short “what done looks like” note for jobs that keep going wrong.","go":["/library/thinking.html#curse-of-knowledge","The curse of knowledge (Library)"],"see":["false-consensus-at-home","clear-yes-or-no-questions"],"ev":"The curse of knowledge is well documented: once we know something, it’s hard to imagine not knowing it, so our explanations leave out steps.","ex":"“Can you ‘do the bins’?” might mean three things to you (take out, rinse, new bags) and one to them.","f":"biases"},{"id":"ladder-of-inference","t":"Ladder of inference","a":["ladder of inference","jumping to conclusions","the story I’m telling myself"],"x":["The ladder of inference describes how we climb quickly from what we saw to conclusions: we notice a detail, add meaning, make assumptions and reach a belief, often in seconds.","Climbing back down (“what did I actually see?”) separates the fact from the story you added, which makes a calm conversation much easier."],"p":"WP-09 asks for the fact in one sentence with no adjectives. The Frequency Journey even has a level about telling what you saw from the story you added.","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["clarifying-questions","assume-positive-intent"],"ev":"A thinking model developed by Chris Argyris and popularized by Peter Senge in The Fifth Discipline (1990). It’s a teaching tool rather than a research finding.","ex":"“The story I’m telling myself is that you don’t care about the plans. Is that fair?”","f":"biases"},{"id":"better-than-average-effect","t":"Better-than-average effect","a":["better than average","illusory superiority","I’m a good driver"],"x":["Most people see themselves as a bit above average: more considerate, more reasonable, more helpful. In a shared home, both people can sincerely believe they’re the more thoughtful one.","That’s a good reason to rely on shared records rather than impressions."],"p":"WP-01 and the Lemonade Stand replace impressions with a written week.","go":["/library/fairness.html#egocentric-bias","Why we each think we do more (Library)"],"see":["self-serving-bias","false-consensus-at-home"],"ev":"Many studies show most people rate themselves above average on positive traits, which can’t be true for everyone. The effect varies by trait and culture.","ex":"“I think I’m the tidy one. Maybe we both think that?”","f":"biases"},{"id":"zero-sum-thinking","t":"Zero-sum thinking","a":["zero sum","if you win I lose","fixed pie"],"x":["Zero-sum thinking assumes that any gain for one person is a loss for the other. In relationships, that’s rarely true: many arrangements can be better for both.","Looking for trades, different timing or new options often turns a tug-of-war into teamwork."],"p":"The Library’s win-win entry and the negotiation notes on trading explain how to expand the options.","go":["/library/conflict.html#integrative-distributive","Win-win and win-lose (Library)"],"see":["trading-across-issues","splitting-the-difference"],"ev":"Research on negotiation shows people often assume a “fixed pie” even when both sides could gain. Recognizing this opens up better options.","ex":"“Your free evening doesn’t have to be my lost evening. What if we swap nights?”","f":"biases"},{"id":"scarcity-mindset","t":"Scarcity mindset","a":["scarcity","tunneling","bandwidth"],"x":["When time, money or energy is very short, attention narrows to the urgent problem in front of you. That helps in the moment but makes it harder to plan ahead or notice others.","Knowing this helps you go easier on yourself and others in stretched seasons, and time important decisions for when things ease."],"p":"WP-02 and Today’s Weather help you notice when you’re running low, so you can pace hard talks.","go":["/workpapers/wp-02-how-much-are-you-carrying.html","WP-02: How full is your battery?"],"see":["overwhelm-as-a-feeling","energy-audit"],"ev":"Sendhil Mullainathan and Eldar Shafir described how scarcity of time or money narrows attention in Scarcity (2013). Some specific findings have been debated in replication work.","ex":"“We’re both stretched this month, so let’s not make any big decisions until it eases.”","f":"biases"},{"id":"ikea-effect","t":"IKEA effect","a":["IKEA effect","valuing what we made","my way"],"x":["We tend to value things we built ourselves more highly, including household systems and routines. That can make it hard to let someone else do a job their way.","Involving everyone in designing a shared system makes it more likely everyone will value and keep it."],"p":"WP-03 is filled in together for exactly this reason. The Library covers gatekeeping.","go":["/library/fairness.html#gatekeeping","Gatekeeping (Library)"],"see":["gatekeeping-around-chores","status-quo-bias"],"ev":"Michael Norton, Daniel Mochon and Dan Ariely (2012) found people value things more when they helped build them.","ex":"“I set up the kitchen system, so I might be overprotective of it. Let’s redesign it together.”","f":"biases"},{"id":"kinds-of-boundaries","t":"Kinds of boundaries","a":["types of boundaries","time boundaries","emotional boundaries","physical boundaries"],"x":["Boundaries can be about time (when you’re available), space (your room, privacy), things (belongings, money), emotions (what you’re willing to discuss) and digital life (messages, sharing photos).","Thinking about which kind of boundary you need makes it easier to say clearly."],"p":"The Library’s boundaries entry explains what boundaries are and aren’t. WP-01’s kind ways to say no help you express them.","go":["/library/communication.html#boundaries","Boundaries (Library)"],"see":["requests-versus-boundaries","boundary-scripts"],"ev":"A common framework from counseling and self-help. The categories are practical, not formal science.","ex":"Time: “I’m not free on Sunday mornings.” Space: “Please knock first.” Things: “Ask before borrowing my car.”","f":"boundaries"},{"id":"requests-versus-boundaries","t":"Requests versus boundaries","a":["request or boundary","boundary versus rule","boundaries are not rules"],"x":["A request asks someone else to change what they do. A boundary says what you will do. Both are fine, but they’re different.","Boundaries work because they’re in your control: you don’t need the other person to agree. Requests invite a conversation and may get a no."],"p":"WP-01’s three steps for saying no are a way to hold your own limit kindly.","go":["/library/communication.html#boundaries","Boundaries (Library)"],"see":["kinds-of-boundaries","boundaries-without-control"],"ev":"A practical distinction discussed by many counselors. It helps avoid using the language of boundaries to control others.","ex":"Request: “Please don’t call during work.” Boundary: “I don’t answer calls during work; I’ll call you back after 6.”","f":"boundaries"},{"id":"boundaries-without-control","t":"Boundaries without control","a":["controlling boundaries","boundaries as control","telling others what to do"],"x":["A boundary is about protecting your wellbeing, not about restricting someone else’s life. “You can’t…” statements aimed at another adult’s choices are rules, and rules imposed on a partner or friend usually harm the relationship.","If you notice a “boundary” is really about controlling someone, it may be a need worth expressing as a request instead."],"p":"The program treats every person as the expert on themselves. Each workpaper is filled in only about yourself.","go":["/library/communication.html#boundaries","Boundaries (Library)"],"see":["requests-versus-boundaries","kinds-of-boundaries"],"ev":"A common caution from counselors: the language of boundaries can be misused to control others’ behavior. Healthy boundaries focus on your own actions.","ex":"Not “You’re not allowed to see your friends,” but “I need us to have one evening a week that’s just ours.”","f":"boundaries"},{"id":"holding-a-boundary-calmly","t":"Holding a boundary calmly","a":["enforcing a boundary","following through","broken record"],"x":["Setting a boundary is one step; holding it is another. People may push back, especially at first. Calmly repeating your position, without over-explaining, is usually more effective than arguing.","You don’t need to justify a boundary endlessly. One clear reason, if you want to give one, is enough."],"p":"WP-01’s neutral refusals are short on purpose: why the request is fair, what you have left, and what you can offer.","go":["/workpapers/wp-01.html","WP-01: Kind ways to say no"],"see":["boundary-scripts","guilt-after-setting-a-boundary"],"ev":"Assertiveness training has long used the “broken record” technique (calmly repeating your position). Consistency is widely recommended.","ex":"“I understand. I’m still not able to host this year.” Repeat, kindly, as needed.","f":"boundaries"},{"id":"guilt-after-setting-a-boundary","t":"Guilt after setting a boundary","a":["guilty for saying no","boundary guilt","feeling bad after a boundary"],"x":["It’s very common to feel guilty after saying no or setting a limit, especially with family. Guilt can be a sign you’re breaking an old habit, not that you’ve done harm.","It usually fades as the new boundary becomes normal. Being kind to yourself during that stretch helps."],"p":"The Library’s shame and guilt entry explains the difference between the two feelings.","go":["/library/emotions.html#shame-and-guilt","Shame and guilt (Library)"],"see":["holding-a-boundary-calmly","self-talk"],"ev":"Many people report guilt after setting limits, especially if they were raised to put others first. Guilt alone doesn’t mean the boundary was wrong.","ex":"Tell yourself: “Feeling guilty doesn’t mean I did something wrong. It means I’m doing something new.”","f":"boundaries"},{"id":"boundaries-with-parents-as-an-adult","t":"Boundaries with parents as an adult","a":["adult children and parents","boundaries with mom","boundaries with parents"],"x":["As adults, many people need to reshape how they relate to their parents: how often to talk, how much advice to take, how visits work. Old roles can pull you back into being treated (or acting) like a child.","Kind, consistent limits, together with warmth, often help both sides settle into an adult-to-adult relationship. Cultural expectations vary a lot, and there’s no single right way."],"p":"The relationships page’s family section and the Library’s family scripts entry cover these patterns.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#family","Where families start"],"see":["kinds-of-boundaries","family-roles"],"ev":"Family research describes renegotiating relationships with parents as a normal part of adulthood. Specific approaches depend on culture and family.","ex":"“I love that you want to help. I’ll ask for advice when I need it.”","f":"boundaries"},{"id":"boundaries-at-work","t":"Boundaries at work","a":["work boundaries","after hours messages","work life boundaries"],"x":["At work, boundaries might mean not answering messages after hours, protecting focus time, or saying no to extra projects when you’re at capacity.","Framing them around doing good work (“I do my best work when I have focus time in the mornings”) usually lands well."],"p":"The Library’s work-life entry covers spillover. The coworker road suggests clear owners for tasks.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#coworkers","Where coworkers and teams start"],"see":["kinds-of-boundaries","response-time-expectations"],"ev":"Research on work-life boundaries suggests clearer separation is linked with better recovery for many people, though preferences differ.","ex":"“I don’t check messages after 7. If it’s urgent, please call.”","f":"boundaries"},{"id":"boundary-scripts","t":"Boundary scripts","a":["boundary scripts","what to say","boundary phrases"],"x":["Having a few phrases ready makes boundaries easier to say in the moment. Some examples: “I’m not able to do that.” “That doesn’t work for me.” “I need to think about it.” “I’d love to help, and I can’t this week.”","Keep them short, kind and firm. Adding an alternative, where you have one, helps the other person move forward."],"p":"WP-01 includes scripts for different kinds of “not now”.","go":["/workpapers/wp-01.html","WP-01: Kind ways to say no"],"see":["holding-a-boundary-calmly","kinds-of-boundaries"],"ev":"Practical phrases drawn from assertiveness and communication training. Wording should fit your own voice.","ex":"“That doesn’t work for me, but here’s what I can do…”","f":"boundaries"},{"id":"boundaries-with-kids","t":"Boundaries with kids","a":["limits with kids","setting limits with children","kind but firm"],"x":["Children need clear limits that are held warmly. Acknowledging the feeling (“you’re disappointed”) while keeping the limit (“and it’s still bedtime”) teaches both empathy and structure.","Offering small choices within the limit helps kids feel some control."],"p":"The Library’s parenting styles entry covers the research. The family road uses one owner per job, including age-sized jobs for kids.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#family","Where families start"],"see":["natural-and-logical-consequences","emotion-coaching"],"ev":"Parenting research consistently links warm, consistent limits with good outcomes for children (often called authoritative parenting).","ex":"“I know you want to keep playing. It’s bedtime. You can choose which book we read.”","f":"boundaries"},{"id":"porous-and-rigid-boundaries","t":"Porous and rigid boundaries","a":["porous boundaries","rigid boundaries","healthy boundaries balance"],"x":["Some people have very open boundaries: they take on others’ problems, struggle to say no and overshare. Others have very closed ones: they keep people at a distance and rarely ask for help.","Most of us are a mix, and it varies by relationship. The aim isn’t a perfect setting but flexibility: open where it’s safe and helpful, firmer where it’s needed."],"p":"Know your own wiring helps you notice patterns like this, and separate them from today’s conditions.","go":["/library/communication.html#boundaries","Boundaries (Library)"],"see":["kinds-of-boundaries","boundaries-without-control"],"ev":"A popular counseling framework describing boundaries as more open or more closed. It’s descriptive, not a measured scale.","ex":"Notice: do I say yes to everything (porous), or keep everyone at arm’s length (rigid)?","f":"boundaries"},{"id":"care-calendar","t":"Care calendar","a":["care calendar","caregiving schedule","shared care schedule"],"x":["A care calendar shows who is doing what and when: appointments, visits, meals, errands, overnight stays. It helps share the load and makes gaps visible.","It also makes it easier for others to offer help, because they can see exactly where it’s needed."],"p":"WP-03 turns the care tasks into named owners. The caregiver road starts with your own battery.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["care-notebook","asking-for-specific-help"],"ev":"Shared care calendars are widely recommended by caregiver organizations. Evidence is practical.","ex":"One shared calendar with appointments, visits, meals and who is covering each one.","f":"caregiving"},{"id":"care-notebook","t":"Care notebook","a":["care notebook","care binder","caregiving information"],"x":["A care notebook keeps important information in one place: contacts, upcoming appointments, questions to ask professionals, and notes from each visit. It saves repeated searching and helps anyone who steps in.","Keep it where others helping can find it, and respect the privacy of the person you care for."],"p":"This is invisible work made visible (Pillar I). A care notebook helps others take over tasks.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#caregivers","Where caregivers start"],"see":["care-calendar","care-handoff-notes"],"ev":"Caregiver organizations commonly recommend keeping key information in one place. It’s a practical tool.","ex":"One notebook or shared document: contacts, appointments, questions for next time, and who’s helping with what.","f":"caregiving"},{"id":"asking-for-specific-help","t":"Asking for specific help","a":["asking for help","how to ask for help","specific requests for help"],"x":["“Let me know if you need anything” is kind, but hard to act on. Specific requests (“could you bring dinner on Tuesday?”) are easier to say yes to, and easier to follow through.","Keeping a list of small, concrete tasks ready makes it easy to answer when someone offers."],"p":"The caregiver road suggests asking for one specific kind of help, with a time.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#caregivers","Where caregivers start"],"see":["care-calendar","explicit-requests"],"ev":"Caregiver guidance often notes that specific requests are more likely to get a yes than general ones. It matches research on helping behavior.","ex":"“Could you take Mum to her Thursday appointment and stay for lunch?”","f":"caregiving"},{"id":"sibling-care-meetings","t":"Sibling care meetings","a":["siblings caring for parents","family care meeting","sharing care between siblings"],"x":["When siblings share care for a parent, it often falls mostly on one person, usually whoever lives closest or started first. A regular meeting to divide jobs helps.","Long-distance siblings can own tasks that work remotely: bills, calls, paperwork, research."],"p":"WP-03 gives every care job one owner; the Lemonade Stand can show how the time splits.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["sibling-rivalry-in-adulthood","care-calendar"],"ev":"Research on family caregiving finds care often falls unevenly among siblings, frequently on one person. Regular family meetings are commonly recommended.","ex":"A monthly call: what’s changed, what’s needed, who owns what next month.","f":"caregiving"},{"id":"respite-and-breaks-for-caregivers","t":"Respite and breaks for caregivers","a":["respite","caregiver break","time off from caring"],"x":["Caregivers often feel guilty taking time off, but regular breaks help you keep caring over the long run. Breaks can be short and simple: an afternoon out, a walk, time with friends.","Friends, family, community services or paid help may be able to cover. Local caregiver organizations can often point to options."],"p":"WP-02 helps you notice when your battery is running low, before you’re running on empty.","go":["/workpapers/wp-02-how-much-are-you-carrying.html","WP-02: How full is your battery?"],"see":["asking-for-specific-help","recovery-after-a-hard-day"],"ev":"Caregiver research links regular breaks with lower strain. Availability of formal respite varies by place.","ex":"Book one regular break each week, even a few hours, with a named person covering.","f":"caregiving"},{"id":"long-distance-caregiving","t":"Long-distance caregiving","a":["caring from far away","long distance caregiver","remote caregiving"],"x":["Caring from a distance brings its own stresses: worry, guilt and feeling out of the loop. Taking on tasks that can be done remotely helps both the person cared for and the local caregivers.","Regular check-ins with whoever is nearby help you stay informed and share the load."],"p":"WP-03 works across distance: owners don’t have to be in the same house.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#caregivers","Where caregivers start"],"see":["sibling-care-meetings","care-notebook"],"ev":"Long-distance caregiving is common. Guidance focuses on coordination, communication and supporting local caregivers.","ex":"Own the remote jobs: bills, paperwork, calls to services, and a weekly call with the local caregiver.","f":"caregiving"},{"id":"dignity-and-preferences","t":"Dignity and preferences","a":["respecting preferences","autonomy in care","asking what they want"],"x":["The person being cared for still has preferences, routines and pride. Asking what they want, and letting them do what they can, helps them keep their dignity and sense of self.","It can be slower, but it usually makes care feel less like a takeover."],"p":"Pillar IV: tune how you send and receive. Care goes better when it’s shaped around the person, not just the tasks.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#caregivers","Where caregivers start"],"see":["care-notebook","care-roles-reversing"],"ev":"Person-centered care is a central principle in caregiving guidance. Involving people in decisions about their own lives supports dignity.","ex":"“Would you prefer I come in the mornings or afternoons? And what would you like to keep doing yourself?”","f":"caregiving"},{"id":"care-roles-reversing","t":"Care roles reversing","a":["caring for a parent","role reversal","parent child role reversal"],"x":["When adult children care for parents, roles can feel turned upside down. Both people may feel awkward, sad or resistant, even when the care is loving.","Naming the change gently, and keeping as much of the old relationship as possible (a shared hobby, a joke, a routine), helps."],"p":"The Library’s ageing parents entry goes deeper. Ambiguous loss, also in the Library, covers the grief of change.","go":["/library/life.html#ageing-parents","Ageing parents (Library)"],"see":["dignity-and-preferences","caregiver-identity"],"ev":"Research on adult children caring for parents describes complex feelings, including grief and role confusion. These feelings are common.","ex":"“It’s strange for both of us that I’m helping you now. Can we figure out together what works?”","f":"caregiving"},{"id":"caring-for-a-partner","t":"Caring for a partner","a":["partner caregiving","caring for my husband","caring for my wife","spouse caregiver"],"x":["When one partner becomes the other’s caregiver, the relationship changes: more tasks, different roles, and sometimes less time just being a couple. Both people may miss how things were.","Sharing care tasks with others where you can, and protecting small moments of partnership, helps."],"p":"The caregiver road starts with your battery (WP-02) and one owner per job (WP-03), so you’re not doing all of it alone.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#caregivers","Where caregivers start"],"see":["care-roles-reversing","respite-and-breaks-for-caregivers"],"ev":"Research on spousal caregiving describes shifts in roles and intimacy. Keeping some of the couple relationship alive is commonly recommended.","ex":"Protect one small ritual that’s just about being a couple, not about care.","f":"caregiving"},{"id":"caregiver-identity","t":"Caregiver identity","a":["identity as a caregiver","losing myself in caregiving","who am I besides a caregiver"],"x":["Caregiving can gradually take over your identity until you’re “the caregiver” and little else. Keeping some parts of your life that aren’t about caring (friends, a hobby, work you enjoy) helps you stay yourself.","It isn’t selfish. It’s part of being able to keep caring."],"p":"Know your own wiring and the self road in the Workpaper Suite are about understanding and caring for yourself.","go":["/know-yourself.html","Know your own wiring"],"see":["respite-and-breaks-for-caregivers","values-clarification"],"ev":"Caregiver research describes how caring can take over a person’s identity. Keeping other roles and interests is linked with wellbeing.","ex":"Name one thing that’s just yours this week, and protect time for it.","f":"caregiving"},{"id":"care-handoff-notes","t":"Care handoff notes","a":["care handover","notes for the next helper","caregiver handover"],"x":["When care passes from one person to another, a short note helps the next person know what’s happened and what’s coming. It avoids repeated questions and missed tasks.","Keep it simple, factual and in a shared place."],"p":"Clear handoffs are part of Pillar II: fix the setup, not the person.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["care-notebook","handoff-notes-at-work"],"ev":"Structured handoffs are used widely in professional care to reduce errors. The same idea helps family caregivers.","ex":"“Today: ate well, walked to the garden. Tomorrow: appointment at 2. Watch out for: tired after lunch.”","f":"caregiving"},{"id":"coordinating-visitors","t":"Coordinating visitors","a":["too many visitors","visitor schedule","managing visits"],"x":["Visitors can be wonderful, but too many at once can be tiring for the person cared for and for the caregiver who hosts. A simple schedule spreads visits out.","A named contact person can handle requests so the main caregiver doesn’t have to."],"p":"This is a boundary about time, and a job worth giving an owner in WP-03.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#caregivers","Where caregivers start"],"see":["care-calendar","kinds-of-boundaries"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Visits can lift spirits but also tire people who are unwell or recovering.","ex":"A simple visit schedule in the care calendar, with suggested lengths and a named contact person.","f":"caregiving"},{"id":"paraphrasing","t":"Paraphrasing","a":["paraphrase","saying it back","reflecting back","restating"],"x":["Paraphrasing means putting what someone said into your own words and checking it back with them. It isn’t agreeing. It’s showing you heard, and giving them a chance to correct you before you respond.","Good paraphrases are short and focus on the heart of what was said, often including the feeling as well as the facts. A paraphrase that’s too long, or that sneaks in your own argument, stops working.","It feels awkward at first. That’s normal. Most people find that one honest “did I get that?” slows a heated talk down more than any clever reply."],"p":"The Check-ins guide builds this in: one person says one fact and how it landed, the other says it back and asks, “Did I get that?”, then you swap.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins: hear it back before you answer"],"see":["summarizing-a-conversation","looping-for-understanding","clarifying-questions"],"ev":"Paraphrasing is a core skill in counseling and mediation training. Studies of listening suggest people feel more understood when they hear their words reflected, though the effect depends on sounding sincere rather than mechanical.","ex":"“So the part that got to you wasn’t the dishes, it was feeling like you’re the only one who notices. Did I get that right?”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"summarizing-a-conversation","t":"Summarizing a conversation","a":["summarising","wrap-up summary","recap at the end"],"x":["A summary pulls a whole conversation together into a few sentences: what each of you said matters, what you agreed, and what happens next. It’s different from paraphrasing one comment. It’s the wrap-up.","Summaries are especially useful after long or emotional talks, when memories quickly drift apart. Saying the agreement out loud, and inviting corrections, turns a vague feeling of “we sorted it” into something both people can point to later."],"p":"End every check-in with one ask, one owner and one time, then say it back. If the agreement is about jobs, write it into WP-03 so it isn’t just remembered.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["paraphrasing","written-recaps-after-a-talk","ending-a-hard-conversation-well"],"ev":"A standard step in meeting facilitation and mediation. It’s practical wisdom more than a tested finding, but it reliably reduces “I thought we agreed…” moments.","ex":"“Okay, so we agreed you’ll own the grocery list, I’ll own the bins, and we’ll look again at the end of the month. Anything I missed?”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"clarifying-questions","t":"Clarifying questions","a":["clarifying question","checking what they meant","asking before assuming"],"x":["A clarifying question asks about meaning before you react: “Do you mean…?”, “Which part…?”, “When you say ‘always’, what are you thinking of?” It treats a confusing comment as a puzzle to solve together rather than an attack to defend against.","The trick is tone. A clarifying question asked with a raised eyebrow sounds like a challenge. Asked with genuine curiosity, it tells the other person you want to get it right."],"p":"WP-09 asks you to weigh what you heard before reacting: could a neutral meaning fit? A quick clarifying question is often the fastest way to find out.","go":["/signal-translator.html","The Signal Translator"],"see":["paraphrasing","open-and-closed-questions","assume-positive-intent"],"ev":"Clarifying is a basic listening skill taught in communication and conflict-resolution courses. Research on misunderstandings in text and speech suggests people overestimate how clearly their meaning comes across, which is why checking helps.","ex":"“When you said ‘we need to talk about the weekend’, did you mean the plans, or how the chores went?”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"open-and-closed-questions","t":"Open and closed questions","a":["open questions","closed questions","open-ended questions"],"x":["Closed questions can be answered with a word: yes, no, Tuesday. Open questions invite a fuller answer: “How did it go?”, “What was that like?”, “What would help?”","Open questions are good for connection and understanding. Closed questions are good for logistics and for tired people who don’t have many words left. A mix works best. If someone seems overwhelmed, switching to a simple either-or question can be a kindness."],"p":"Turning Toward suggests small ways to reach for someone. A good open question about their world is one of the easiest.","go":["/turning-toward.html","Turning Toward"],"see":["clarifying-questions","the-and-what-else-question","talking-side-by-side"],"ev":"The distinction is used widely in counseling, interviewing and teaching. It’s a practical tool, not a tested theory, and both kinds of question have their place.","ex":"Instead of “Did you have a good day?” (closed), try “What was the best bit of your day?” (open).","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"the-and-what-else-question","t":"The “and what else?” question","a":["and what else","what else","is there anything else"],"x":["Often the first thing someone says isn’t the whole story. Asking “and what else?” once or twice, gently, gives them room to get to what’s really bothering them, before you jump to solving the first thing you heard.","It works because it slows you down as the listener. You’re not preparing your reply. You’re making space. Use it sparingly: asked five times in a row, it starts to feel like an interrogation."],"p":"In a check-in, the first topic that comes up may not be the real one. If the real one appears, jot it down and give it its own time, keeping to one topic per talk.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["open-and-closed-questions","asking-permission-before-giving-advice"],"ev":"Popularized by coach Michael Bungay Stanier in The Coaching Habit (2016). It comes from coaching practice rather than controlled research, but it’s simple and low-risk.","ex":"“That makes sense. And what else is going on with it?”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"asking-permission-before-giving-advice","t":"Asking permission before giving advice","a":["do you want advice","asking before advising","unsolicited advice"],"x":["When someone shares a problem, many of us leap to solutions. Sometimes that’s exactly what they want. Often they first want to be heard. Asking “Do you want my thoughts, or do you just want me to listen?” lets them choose.","This small question prevents a common mix-up: one person feels dismissed (“I didn’t ask you to fix it”), while the other feels unappreciated (“I was trying to help”)."],"p":"The Library’s entry on advice versus support goes deeper. In a daily check-in (WP-13), the rule is simply to listen: nothing gets solved there.","go":["/library/communication.html#advice-vs-support","Advice versus support (Library)"],"see":["the-and-what-else-question","listening-mode-or-fixing-mode"],"ev":"Research on social support suggests unasked-for advice can backfire, leaving people feeling less capable. Asking first is a practical way around that, though the research is about patterns, not every situation.","ex":"“Do you want ideas, or do you mostly want to vent right now? Either is fine.”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"listening-mode-or-fixing-mode","t":"Listening mode or fixing mode","a":["fix it or listen","venting versus solving","solution mode"],"x":["Conversations go better when both people know which mode they’re in. In listening mode, the goal is understanding: nod, reflect, ask gently. In fixing mode, the goal is a plan: options, owners, next steps.","Many frustrations come from mismatched modes, not bad intentions. Saying which mode you want at the start (“I need to vent for five minutes, then maybe brainstorm”) saves both people guessing."],"p":"Check-ins are for one topic, heard both ways, with one ask at the end. The Wiring Card can also note what you need in a hard moment: to be heard, or a plan.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["asking-permission-before-giving-advice","paraphrasing"],"ev":"A widely used practical idea. It’s often framed as a gender difference, but that framing is oversimplified: individuals vary a lot, and the same person may want different things on different days.","ex":"“Right now I just need you to listen. I’ll ask for ideas later if I want them.”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"using-and-instead-of-but","t":"Using “and” instead of “but”","a":["and not but","the word but","yes but"],"x":["“I love you, but…” tends to erase the first half of the sentence. The listener braces for what comes after the “but”. Swapping in “and” lets both parts stand: the appreciation and the request can both be true.","It won’t fix a harsh message on its own, and people notice if it’s used as a trick. As a habit, though, it keeps your good intentions from being cancelled by your grammar."],"p":"WP-09’s fact, feeling and ask pairs naturally with “and”: “The bin wasn’t out, and I felt a bit unseen, and could we pick a fixed day for it?”","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["owning-your-part","tentative-language"],"ev":"A common tip in communication and negotiation training. It’s practical rather than research-tested, and the tone behind the words still matters more than the word itself.","ex":"“I appreciate you cooking, and I’d love it if the pans got soaked before bed.”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"owning-your-part","t":"Owning your part","a":["taking responsibility","my part in it","accountability in conflict"],"x":["Most tangles have contributions on both sides. Naming your own part first (“I let this build up”, “I said it sharply”) makes it easier for the other person to lower their guard and look at theirs.","Owning your part isn’t taking all the blame, and it isn’t a trade (“I admitted mine, now you admit yours”). It’s just honest. It also keeps the focus on the setup and the pattern, not on who is the bad guy."],"p":"The program looks at arrangements, not people. Owning your part fits Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person: what did each of us do that let this setup keep producing the problem?","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["using-and-instead-of-but","anatomy-of-an-apology","self-serving-bias"],"ev":"Taking responsibility for your own contribution is a common theme in conflict-resolution and couples research. It tends to lower defensiveness in the other person, though it works best when it’s genuine, not a bargaining chip.","ex":"“I think my part in this was waiting until I was fed up before saying anything.”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"tentative-language","t":"Tentative language","a":["softeners","hedging kindly","it seems to me"],"x":["Tentative language (“it seems to me”, “I wonder if”, “I might be wrong, but”) leaves room for the other person’s view. It’s useful when you’re making a guess about their feelings or motives, where certainty would be unfair.","Use it for interpretations, not for clear needs. “I wonder if maybe it might be nice to…” can blur an ask so much that it gets missed, especially by people who take words literally. State the ask plainly, and soften the guess."],"p":"The Signal Translator shows how the same ask can land with more or less softening, and for different kinds of wiring.","go":["/signal-translator.html","The Signal Translator"],"see":["using-and-instead-of-but","clarifying-questions"],"ev":"Linguists and communication researchers describe how softeners change how requests land. Too much hedging can make a message unclear, so the balance matters.","ex":"“It seems like the kitchen is getting away from us lately. What do you think?”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"assume-positive-intent","t":"Assume positive intent","a":["benefit of the doubt","generous interpretation","most generous interpretation"],"x":["Assuming positive intent means starting from the most generous reasonable explanation for what someone did, until you know more. “They forgot because they’re overloaded” rather than “They don’t care.”","It doesn’t mean ignoring a pattern, and it doesn’t replace asking. It just keeps your first reaction from being built on the worst guess, which tends to make the other person defensive and the conversation harder."],"p":"The Library’s entries on attribution errors explain why we default to harsh guesses. Wired Differently adds that a mix-up between different kinds of wiring usually runs both ways.","go":["/wired-differently.html","Wired Differently"],"see":["clarifying-questions","ladder-of-inference","just-world-thinking"],"ev":"A popular workplace and relationship principle. Research on attribution suggests we often judge others’ actions more harshly than our own, which is the problem this habit tries to counter. It isn’t meant to excuse repeated harm.","ex":"Before replying, ask yourself: “What’s the kindest reasonable reason they might have done this?”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"looping-for-understanding","t":"Looping for understanding","a":["looping","closing the loop","checking you understood"],"x":["Looping means listening, saying back what you heard, and then asking whether you got it right, and repeating until the other person says yes. The loop is only closed when they feel understood, not when you think you’ve understood.","It’s slower than a normal conversation, which is the point. It’s especially useful in conversations that keep going in circles, because people often repeat themselves when they don’t feel heard."],"p":"This is the same move as the Check-ins guide’s “hear it back before you answer”. Try it once in your next check-in, then swap.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["paraphrasing","summarizing-a-conversation"],"ev":"A technique from mediation practice, described in Amanda Ripley’s book High Conflict (2021). It’s a structured form of paraphrasing; evidence comes mostly from practitioners’ experience.","ex":"“Let me see if I’ve got it: you’re not upset about the money itself, you’re upset that you found out after. Is that it, or is there more?”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"mirroring-and-labeling","t":"Mirroring and labeling","a":["mirroring","labelling","tactical empathy"],"x":["Mirroring means repeating a few key words someone just said, often as a soft question. It invites them to say more without you steering. Labeling means naming the feeling you think you’re hearing: “It sounds like you’re really worn out.”","Both are simple and quick. The risk is sounding like you’re using a technique on someone. Use them to understand, not to win, and drop them if they feel forced."],"p":"In Check-ins, saying back what you heard and how it landed is the heart of the ground rules.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["paraphrasing","naming-feelings-in-others"],"ev":"Popularized by former FBI negotiator Chris Voss in Never Split the Difference (2016). It draws on counseling skills; research on the specific techniques is limited, and it works best when sincere rather than as a tactic.","ex":"They say, “I’m just so done with this week.” You say, gently, “Done with this week?” and let them keep going.","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"naming-feelings-in-others","t":"Naming feelings in others","a":["emotion labeling","it sounds like you feel","guessing feelings"],"x":["A gentle guess at what someone is feeling (“you seem disappointed”) can help them feel seen, and helps them find words if they’re struggling. Offering two options makes it easier for people who find feelings hard to name.","Always leave room for “no, that’s not it.” The goal isn’t to be right about their feelings. It’s to show you’re paying attention."],"p":"Wired Differently notes that some people feel plenty but can’t find the word, and suggests asking about the body or actions instead: “more like tired, or more like annoyed?”","go":["/library/emotions.html#affect-labeling","Putting feelings into words (Library)"],"see":["mirroring-and-labeling","the-emotion-wheel","tentative-language"],"ev":"Research on “affect labeling” suggests naming feelings can reduce their intensity for the person feeling them. Naming someone else’s feelings is less studied, and a wrong guess can annoy, so offering it tentatively matters.","ex":"“I might be off, but you seem a bit flat since the call. Frustrated, or just tired?”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"speaking-for-yourself","t":"Speaking for yourself","a":["speak for yourself","I language","not speaking for others"],"x":["Speaking for yourself means talking about your own experience, not claiming to speak for “everyone”, “the family” or “any normal person”. Bringing in imaginary allies makes the other person feel ganged up on, and turns a conversation into a trial.","It also keeps you honest. You know your own experience. You can only guess at everyone else’s."],"p":"Each workpaper asks you to fill in only your own side. The same rule works in conversation.","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["i-messages-that-arent-blame-in-disguise","owning-your-part"],"ev":"A standard ground rule in facilitation, mediation and family meetings. It’s practical guidance rather than a research finding.","ex":"Instead of “Everyone thinks you’re always late,” try “I’ve been feeling anxious when we’re late.”","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"i-messages-that-arent-blame-in-disguise","t":"I-messages that aren’t blame in disguise","a":["fake I-statements","I feel that you","I feel like you"],"x":["Starting a sentence with “I feel” doesn’t automatically make it an I-statement. “I feel that you’re selfish” is still a verdict on the person. The giveaway is the word “that” or “like” followed by a claim about them.","A true I-message names a feeling (lonely, stretched, worried), the specific situation, and ideally what would help. It’s about your experience, which the other person can’t argue with, rather than their character, which they will."],"p":"WP-09’s three parts (fact, feeling and ask) are a practical way to build a real I-message.","go":["/library/communication.html#i-statements","I-statements (Library)"],"see":["speaking-for-yourself","feelings-versus-thoughts"],"ev":"A practical refinement of the well-known I-statement technique. There’s research support for I-statements lowering hostility in some studies, though effects are modest and tone matters more than grammar.","ex":"“I feel like you don’t care” is a judgment. “I feel lonely when we don’t talk in the evenings” is a feeling.","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"feelings-versus-thoughts","t":"Feelings versus thoughts","a":["I feel that","thoughts dressed as feelings","feelings or judgments"],"x":["Some words sound like feelings but are really interpretations of what someone else did: ignored, manipulated, disrespected, abandoned. They’re understandable, but they point a finger, so the other person tends to defend.","Looking underneath for the plain feeling (hurt, lonely, scared, tired, frustrated) makes it easier to be heard. You can still describe what happened. Just keep the feeling about you."],"p":"WP-09 asks for “the feeling underneath, like tired or unseen”, then the fact and the ask.","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["i-messages-that-arent-blame-in-disguise","the-emotion-wheel"],"ev":"The distinction is central to nonviolent communication training and to cognitive approaches. It’s a teaching tool, not a strict scientific line: thoughts and feelings are tangled together in real life.","ex":"“I feel ignored” includes a guess about what they did. “I feel lonely” is the feeling underneath it.","f":"communication-skills"},{"id":"conflict-avoidance-costs","t":"Conflict avoidance costs","a":["avoiding conflict","conflict avoidant","keeping the peace"],"x":["Avoiding conflict can be sensible for small, passing issues. Used for everything, though, it lets problems and resentment build, until something small triggers a big reaction.","Raising things early, while they’re small, is often gentler than waiting."],"p":"The daily check-in (WP-13) is designed to keep small things small, by giving them a daily outlet.","go":["/library/conflict.html#thomas-kilmann","Conflict modes (Library)"],"see":["compromise-and-its-limits","choosing-your-battles"],"ev":"Research on conflict styles suggests avoiding can protect relationships from small issues but, used for everything, can let resentment build.","ex":"“I tend to let things slide, and then they pile up. Can I raise one small thing now?”","f":"conflict-styles"},{"id":"compromise-and-its-limits","t":"Compromise and its limits","a":["compromise","meeting halfway","when compromise fails"],"x":["Compromise means each person gives up some of what they want. It’s quick and often fair, especially for small issues.","For important, recurring issues, compromise can leave both people partly unhappy. Exploring what each person really needs sometimes finds a solution that works better for both."],"p":"The Library’s interests-versus-positions entry explains how to look beneath what people say they want.","go":["/library/conflict.html#thomas-kilmann","Conflict modes (Library)"],"see":["splitting-the-difference","trading-across-issues"],"ev":"Conflict research treats compromise as useful but not always best; collaborating on underlying interests can produce better solutions for important issues.","ex":"For something that matters deeply to both of you, try asking “what do we each really need here?” before splitting the difference.","f":"conflict-styles"},{"id":"choosing-your-battles","t":"Choosing your battles","a":["pick your battles","let it go","is it worth it"],"x":["Not every irritation needs a conversation. Letting small things go, especially one-offs, can keep a relationship light.","The key questions: is it a pattern, does it really matter to me, and am I reacting to this or to my battery? Patterns and things that matter deserve a calm conversation."],"p":"WP-02 helps with the last question: is this about the thing, or about what I was already carrying?","go":["/workpapers/wp-02-how-much-are-you-carrying.html","WP-02: How full is your battery?"],"see":["conflict-avoidance-costs","one-topic-at-a-time"],"ev":"Common advice, and consistent with research suggesting relationships do better when small irritations are sometimes let go. It shouldn’t mean ignoring things that matter.","ex":"Ask: “Will this matter in a month? Is it a pattern or a one-off? Am I just tired?”","f":"conflict-styles"},{"id":"conflict-escalation-ladder","t":"Conflict escalation ladder","a":["escalation ladder","Glasl model","stages of conflict escalation"],"x":["Conflicts tend to escalate in steps: from a disagreement about a topic, to hardened positions, to talking about each other instead of the issue, to trying to win at any cost.","The earlier you notice the climb, the easier it is to step back down. A pause, a return to one topic, or a neutral helper can help."],"p":"The Library’s escalation entry covers this in depth. The Carrier Wave Decoder helps in the moment a conversation starts going sideways.","go":["/library/conflict.html#escalation","Escalation (Library)"],"see":["fighting-fair","de-escalation-phrases"],"ev":"Friedrich Glasl described nine stages of conflict escalation (1980s). It’s widely used in mediation training as a descriptive model.","ex":"Notice early signs: “We’ve stopped talking about the bins and started talking about each other.”","f":"conflict-styles"},{"id":"de-escalation-phrases","t":"De-escalation phrases","a":["calming phrases","de-escalate","cool it down"],"x":["A few calm phrases can slow a heated conversation: “I hear you.” “Let me make sure I understand.” “I don’t want to fight about this.” “Can we take a break and come back at 7?”","They work best said slowly, in a softer voice. Said sarcastically, they escalate things further."],"p":"The Carrier Wave Decoder offers guided scripts for exactly this moment.","go":["/carrier-wave-decoder.html","The Carrier Wave Decoder"],"see":["conflict-escalation-ladder","humor-that-repairs"],"ev":"Practical phrases from mediation and crisis-free conflict training. Tone and sincerity matter as much as the words.","ex":"“I think we’re both getting heated. I want to understand you. Can we slow down?”","f":"conflict-styles"},{"id":"agree-to-disagree","t":"Agree to disagree","a":["agree to disagree","we see it differently","accepting differences"],"x":["Some differences (about politics, how clean is clean enough, how social to be) may never fully resolve. Agreeing to disagree, and finding a way to live alongside the difference, is often healthier than arguing endlessly.","It works best when both people still feel respected, and when practical decisions are agreed even if the underlying views differ."],"p":"The Library’s perpetual problems entry explains why this is normal.","go":["/library/relationships.html#perpetual-problems","Perpetual problems (Library)"],"see":["compromise-and-its-limits","choosing-your-battles"],"ev":"Relationship research (for example, John Gottman’s work on perpetual problems) suggests many disagreements in lasting relationships are never fully solved, and couples manage them rather than resolve them.","ex":"“We just see this differently, and that’s okay. How do we live with it kindly?”","f":"conflict-styles"},{"id":"pursuing-and-distancing-in-everyday-life","t":"Pursuing and distancing in everyday life","a":["pursue withdraw","chasing and retreating","the more I push the more they pull away"],"x":["In a common cycle, one person pushes to talk and the other pulls back, which makes the first push harder, which makes the second pull back further. Both feel frustrated.","Naming the cycle together (“we’re doing our thing again”) and agreeing a pause-and-return plan often breaks it."],"p":"The Calm-Down Kit (WP-11) gives the withdrawing person a clear pause line, and the pursuing person a time.","go":["/library/relationships.html#pursue-withdraw","Pursue and withdraw (Library)"],"see":["conflict-avoidance-costs","coming-back-after-a-break"],"ev":"The pursue-withdraw (or demand-withdraw) pattern is well documented in couples research. It’s a cycle, not one person’s fault.","ex":"If you pursue: say your need once, then give space with a time. If you withdraw: say when you’ll come back.","f":"conflict-styles"},{"id":"conflict-in-front-of-others","t":"Conflict in front of others","a":["arguing in public","fighting in front of kids","conflict in front of friends"],"x":["Arguing in front of friends, colleagues or children adds pressure and embarrassment, and makes it harder to back down.","Mild disagreements handled calmly (and repaired) can actually teach kids healthy conflict. Hostile or unresolved fights are best taken somewhere private."],"p":"Check-ins suggest a private place, phones down, at an agreed time.","go":["/check-ins.html#room","Check-ins: a good time and place"],"see":["talking-kindly-about-the-other-parent","choosing-the-channel"],"ev":"Research on children and parental conflict suggests calm, resolved disagreements are less harmful than hostile ones, and seeing repair can be reassuring.","ex":"“Let’s talk about this later, just us.”","f":"conflict-styles"},{"id":"cooling-off-before-replying","t":"Cooling off before replying","a":["cool off","wait before responding","sleep on a reply"],"x":["When a message or comment stings, the urge to reply immediately is strong. Waiting even a little while lets the intensity drop and gives you a chance to reply to what was said, not to how it felt in the first moment.","Writing a draft and not sending it can release some of the pressure."],"p":"WP-09 is a quick self-check before you answer something that stung.","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["dont-fight-by-text","de-escalation-phrases"],"ev":"Research on emotion suggests intensity drops with time. Waiting before replying to a charged message is widely recommended.","ex":"Write the reply, don’t send it. Read it again in an hour.","f":"conflict-styles"},{"id":"repair-during-a-fight","t":"Repair during a fight","a":["making up mid-argument","softening in a fight","calling a truce"],"x":["A repair attempt is anything that slows down or softens a conflict mid-way: a joke, an apology, a touch, “I’m sorry, let me start over.” Noticing and accepting the other person’s repair attempts matters as much as making them.","Repairs land best early, before things get too heated, and when they’re sincere. If a repair is brushed off, it’s often a sign you both need a real break, then a calmer restart."],"p":"The Library’s repair attempts entry goes deeper.","go":["/library/relationships.html#repair-attempts","Repair attempts (Library)"],"see":["humor-that-repairs","de-escalation-phrases"],"ev":"John Gottman’s research highlighted “repair attempts” (small moves to de-escalate) as important in couples who handle conflict well. Whether a repair works depends on the relationship’s overall warmth.","ex":"“Wait, I’m on your side here. Let me try that again.”","f":"conflict-styles"},{"id":"a-simple-agenda-for-a-hard-talk","t":"A simple agenda for a hard talk","a":["conversation agenda","agenda for a difficult conversation","planning a talk"],"x":["A hard conversation goes better with a small, agreed shape: the one topic, roughly how long, and what “done” looks like (usually one agreed next step). Saying it at the start lowers the sense of threat. Nobody is walking into an ambush.","For many people, especially those who process slowly or dislike surprises, knowing the topic in advance makes a big difference. A text like “Can we talk about the bills tonight? Nothing bad, just want a plan” gives them time to think."],"p":"Check-ins are built this way: a named time, one topic, hearing each other, one ask, one owner, one time.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["the-parking-lot","timeboxing-a-conversation","ending-a-hard-conversation-well"],"ev":"Borrowed from meeting facilitation. It’s practical advice rather than a tested method, but structure tends to lower anxiety for many people, especially those who dislike surprises.","ex":"“Tonight: ten minutes on the weekend plan. Each of us says what we need, then we pick one thing to try. That’s it.”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"the-parking-lot","t":"The parking lot","a":["parking lot list","park it","topics for later"],"x":["When a second topic pops up in a hard talk, the parking lot is a note where it goes to wait safely. It isn’t dismissed. It’s scheduled. That lets you finish the first topic without the conversation sprawling into every old grievance.","Keep the list somewhere both people can see (a note on the fridge or a shared phone note), and actually come back to it. A parking lot that never gets visited stops being trusted."],"p":"The Check-ins ground rules say: if a second topic comes up, jot it down for another day.","go":["/check-ins.html#ground","Check-in ground rules"],"see":["a-simple-agenda-for-a-hard-talk","one-topic-at-a-time"],"ev":"A standard facilitation technique for keeping meetings on track. There’s little formal research, but it’s widely used because it works simply.","ex":"“That’s a real one, and it deserves its own time. Let’s put it on the list for Sunday.”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"one-topic-at-a-time","t":"One topic at a time","a":["single topic","stay on topic","no kitchen sinking"],"x":["A conversation about the bins that turns into a conversation about everything is almost impossible to finish well. One topic keeps it small enough to actually solve, and small wins build trust for the bigger ones.","If one topic keeps dragging others in, that’s useful information too: maybe the topics share a root, like a missing owner or a timing clash, worth its own conversation."],"p":"The Library’s one-topic entry goes deeper. WP-04, the monthly look-back, is where you look for the shared root behind several topics.","go":["/library/conflict.html#one-topic","One topic (Library)"],"see":["the-parking-lot","a-simple-agenda-for-a-hard-talk"],"ev":"Relationship researchers describe “kitchen-sinking” (throwing in every complaint) as a sign of escalating conflict. Keeping to one topic is common advice from couples researchers and mediators.","ex":"“I want to stay on the holiday plan for now. Can we save the money stuff for Thursday?”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"timeboxing-a-conversation","t":"Timeboxing a conversation","a":["time limit on a talk","timeboxing","20 minute talk"],"x":["Hard talks that have no end can feel endless. Agreeing a time limit (often twenty or thirty minutes) makes it easier to start, and stops a tired conversation from sliding into a fight.","When the time is up, stop, even mid-topic, and agree when to continue. Stopping on purpose is a success, not a failure. It protects both people’s batteries."],"p":"This fits Pillar III, Read your state first. The daily check-in (WP-13) is the extreme version: 90 seconds, no debate.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["a-simple-agenda-for-a-hard-talk","coming-back-after-a-break"],"ev":"A practical technique from work and facilitation. Many couples and families find shorter, more frequent talks easier than long ones, though there’s no magic number.","ex":"“Let’s give this twenty minutes. If we’re not done, we’ll pick another time.”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"coming-back-after-a-break","t":"Coming back after a break","a":["returning after a timeout","resuming a conversation","after a pause"],"x":["A pause only works if it ends. Coming back at the time you named shows the break was about calming down, not walking out. That promise is what makes the next pause easier to take.","When you return, start small and gentle: thank them for the space, restate the one topic, and maybe begin with something easy you can agree on. Diving straight back into the hardest point often reignites things."],"p":"The Calm-Down Kit (WP-11) uses your battery score to decide when to go back, and suggests starting with one small, simple task first.","go":["/wp-11.html","WP-11: The Calm-Down Kit"],"see":["timeboxing-a-conversation","conflict-escalation-ladder"],"ev":"Couples research suggests breaks during flooded arguments help only if people actually calm down (rather than rehearsing grievances) and come back. The returning part is what builds trust.","ex":"“I’m back. Thanks for the space. Can we start with just the plan for tomorrow?”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"ending-a-hard-conversation-well","t":"Ending a hard conversation well","a":["ending on a good note","closing a conversation","wrapping up a talk"],"x":["How a hard talk ends colors how both people remember it. Ending with a clear next step, a thank-you and something warm (even a small joke or a hug, if welcome) helps the whole thing feel worth doing.","If you didn’t reach agreement, you can still end well: name what you understood about each other, and when you’ll pick it up again."],"p":"The Library entry on the peak-end rule explains why endings matter. Check-ins end with one ask, one owner and one time.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["summarizing-a-conversation","written-recaps-after-a-talk"],"ev":"Research on memory (the peak-end rule) suggests people remember experiences partly by how they end. Applying that to conversations is a reasonable inference rather than a direct finding.","ex":"“Thanks for talking this through with me. I feel better. So: you’ll book the plumber, and we’ll check in Friday.”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"written-recaps-after-a-talk","t":"Written recaps after a talk","a":["recap message","follow-up message","write it down afterward"],"x":["Memories of a conversation drift, especially emotional ones. A short written recap, sent the same day, gives both people the same record. It’s not a contract, just a shared note.","Keep it neutral and brief, stick to what was agreed, and invite corrections. A recap that adds new complaints stops being a recap."],"p":"For household jobs, write agreements into WP-03 and both initial it. It’s the same idea: change it on purpose, not by drift.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["summarizing-a-conversation","handoff-notes-at-work"],"ev":"A common workplace practice. Research on memory shows people remember conversations differently afterward, which is the problem a written note solves.","ex":"“Thanks for today. Just so we’re on the same page: I’ll handle school forms, you’ll do Friday pickups, and we’ll review next month.”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"talking-side-by-side","t":"Talking side by side","a":["side by side talks","talking in the car","walk and talk"],"x":["Some people talk more easily side by side than face to face: in the car, on a walk, while cooking or fixing something. Less eye contact and a shared task can make a conversation feel lower-stakes.","This is especially useful with teenagers, with people who find direct eye contact uncomfortable, and with anyone who tenses up at “can we talk?”. The talk can be shorter and lighter, and it still counts."],"p":"Turning Toward is about small everyday moments rather than big sit-down talks. A side-by-side chat is a good example.","go":["/turning-toward.html","Turning Toward"],"see":["open-and-closed-questions","emotion-coaching"],"ev":"A widely shared observation from parents, therapists and youth workers, especially about teenagers and many men. It’s practical wisdom rather than a tested finding.","ex":"“Want to walk the dog with me?” rather than “We need to talk.”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"stop-start-continue","t":"Stop, start, continue","a":["stop start continue","three questions review","start stop keep"],"x":["A quick way to review how something is going: what should we stop doing, start doing, and continue doing? It keeps a review practical and balanced, because “continue” makes room for what’s working.","It works for households, friendships with shared plans, teams and co-parents. Keep each list short, one or two items, and give each change one owner."],"p":"The monthly look-back (WP-04) is a deeper version for households, focused on what keeps slipping and why.","go":["/workpapers/wp-04-what-keeps-coming-back.html","WP-04: What keeps coming back?"],"see":["rose-bud-thorn","retrospective-for-households"],"ev":"A common review format from workplace teams and teaching. It’s simple and widely used; there’s little formal research on it specifically.","ex":"“Stop: leaving laundry in the machine overnight. Start: a Sunday menu plan. Continue: the daily check-in.”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"rose-bud-thorn","t":"Rose, bud, thorn","a":["rose bud thorn","highs and lows","best and hardest part"],"x":["Each person shares a rose (something good), a thorn (something hard) and a bud (something they’re looking forward to). It’s quick, gentle and works well at dinner, even with kids.","It gives everyone a turn and naturally mixes good news with hard news, so nobody has to announce “I need to talk about something”."],"p":"The 90-second daily check-in (WP-13) has a similar rhythm: how full you were today, one thing you appreciated, one small thing that didn’t feel great, and one thing that would help tomorrow.","go":["/workpapers/wp-13-daily-check-in.html","WP-13: The 90-second daily check-in"],"see":["stop-start-continue","open-and-closed-questions"],"ev":"A popular reflection game from camps, schools and design teams. It’s a conversation tool, not a researched method.","ex":"“Rose: lunch with you. Bud: the trip next month. Thorn: that email from work.”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"retrospective-for-households","t":"Retrospective for households","a":["household retro","family retrospective","home review meeting"],"x":["A retrospective is a regular, blame-free look back at how things went: what worked, what didn’t, and one or two changes to try. Teams use them after projects. Households can use them monthly.","The key rule is the same as in good team retros: look at the setup and the process, not at who failed. A slipped job is a clue about the system, like a missing owner or a bad time slot."],"p":"WP-04 is the program’s household retro, and the Library’s entry on retrospectives covers the team version.","go":["/workpapers/wp-04-what-keeps-coming-back.html","WP-04: What keeps coming back?"],"see":["stop-start-continue","family-meetings"],"ev":"Adapted from team retrospectives in software and project work. Evidence for home use is anecdotal, but the structure (look at the process, not blame) matches the program’s approach.","ex":"“What went well this month? What kept slipping? What’s one change, and who owns it?”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"choosing-the-channel","t":"Choosing the channel","a":["text or talk","which channel","call or text","in person or message"],"x":["Logistics suit texts: times, lists, confirmations. Feelings, disagreements and anything complicated usually go better by voice or in person, where tone and a face can soften the words.","Some people, especially many autistic people or those who need time to process, prefer writing for hard topics because it lets them think. That’s a good reason to agree the channel together rather than assuming."],"p":"The Carrier Wave Decoder looks at pace, tone and timing, which a channel can distort. The Wiring Card can say which channel works best for you.","go":["/carrier-wave-decoder.html","The Carrier Wave Decoder"],"see":["dont-fight-by-text","when-to-switch-to-voice"],"ev":"Communication research suggests text carries fewer cues (tone, face, timing) than voice or in-person talk, so ambiguity rises. The best channel still depends on the people and the topic.","ex":"“This one feels like a talk, not a text. Can I call you on my lunch break?”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"one-ask-at-a-time","t":"One ask at a time","a":["a single request","one clear ask","make one request"],"x":["A conversation that ends with five requests usually ends with none of them happening. One clear ask, with a who and a when, is much more likely to stick.","If you have several, pick the one that matters most or would make the biggest difference. The others can wait for the next check-in, or go on the parking lot list."],"p":"Every check-in ends with one ask, one owner, one time. WP-09’s last step is the ask.","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["one-topic-at-a-time","clear-yes-or-no-questions"],"ev":"A practical guideline from communication and negotiation training. People can only act on so much at once, especially under stress.","ex":"“Could you take the recycling out on Tuesdays from now on?” rather than a list of five things.","f":"conversations"},{"id":"starting-from-a-shared-goal","t":"Starting from a shared goal","a":["common ground","shared goal first","we want the same thing"],"x":["Before discussing a disagreement, naming what you both want (a calm home, happy kids, a project that ships) reminds you that you’re on the same side, facing the problem together.","It changes the question from “who wins?” to “how do we get there?”. Even when you disagree on the how, agreeing on the why makes the disagreement feel smaller."],"p":"The Check-ins opener says it out loud: “I am not against you. I do not think you meant harm. I want to sort out this one thing.”","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["a-simple-agenda-for-a-hard-talk","trading-across-issues"],"ev":"Negotiation and mediation research emphasizes shared interests as a foundation for agreement. Naming the shared goal first is a practical application of that.","ex":"“We both want Sundays to feel restful. Let’s figure out how to get there.”","f":"conversations"},{"id":"parallel-parenting","t":"Parallel parenting","a":["parallel parenting","low contact co-parenting","separate parenting"],"x":["In parallel parenting, each parent runs their own home with little direct coordination, and communication is kept brief, written and focused on logistics. It’s often used when direct cooperation keeps leading to conflict.","It isn’t a failure. It can protect children from ongoing tension, and some families move toward more cooperation over time."],"p":"The co-parent road keeps messages short and factual, with one owner per task.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#co-parents","Where co-parents start"],"see":["biff-responses","co-parenting-calendar"],"ev":"Parallel parenting is a recognized approach for high-conflict separations, described by family courts and practitioners. It aims to reduce contact and conflict between parents.","ex":"Each home keeps its own routines; communication is written, brief and only about the kids’ logistics.","f":"coparenting"},{"id":"biff-responses","t":"BIFF responses","a":["BIFF","brief informative friendly firm","replying to a hostile message"],"x":["A BIFF response is a reply to a hostile or emotional message that’s Brief, Informative, Friendly and Firm. It gives the needed information, ignores the bait, and doesn’t invite more argument.","It’s especially useful for co-parents, but works with anyone whose messages tend to escalate."],"p":"WP-09 turns a raw reaction into fact, feeling and ask. For co-parents, the fact and the ask are often enough.","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["parallel-parenting","dont-fight-by-text"],"ev":"BIFF (Brief, Informative, Friendly, Firm) was developed by Bill Eddy of the High Conflict Institute. It’s a practical method widely used in family law settings; formal research is limited.","ex":"“Thanks for letting me know. I’ll have Sam ready at 5 on Friday. Have a good week.”","f":"coparenting"},{"id":"co-parenting-calendar","t":"Co-parenting calendar","a":["shared calendar","custody calendar","parenting schedule"],"x":["A shared calendar gives both homes the same picture of the children’s schedule: custody days, school events, appointments and activities. It reduces “you didn’t tell me” arguments.","Keep it factual, update it promptly, and agree who adds what."],"p":"WP-03 can list “calendar updates” as a job with a named owner.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["transition-bags","schedule-change-protocol"],"ev":"Shared calendars are widely recommended by family mediators to reduce conflict over schedules. Evidence is practical.","ex":"One shared calendar with the kids’ schedule, activities, appointments and who has them each day.","f":"coparenting"},{"id":"transition-bags","t":"Transition bags","a":["transition bag","kids’ bag between homes","what goes back and forth"],"x":["Moving between homes is easier when kids don’t have to pack everything each time. Duplicates of basics in both homes, and a short checklist for what travels, cut down on forgotten items and tense texts.","Let children have a say in what comes with them, especially comfort items."],"p":"A checklist with an owner (WP-03) helps: who packs, who checks.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#co-parents","Where co-parents start"],"see":["co-parenting-calendar","handoffs-between-homes"],"ev":"Practical advice from family educators. Having duplicates of basics in each home is commonly recommended to reduce stress.","ex":"Keep toothbrushes, pajamas and basics in both homes; the bag carries school things, medicines and one comfort item.","f":"coparenting"},{"id":"handoffs-between-homes","t":"Handoffs between homes","a":["exchanges","custody exchange","drop off and pick up"],"x":["Handoffs are often the tensest moments of the week, for parents and children. Keeping them brief, friendly and predictable helps children feel secure.","Anything more than a quick note belongs in a planned message or conversation, never at the door and never in front of the kids."],"p":"This is the program’s co-parent advice: keep handoffs short and calm, and keep bigger topics for a planned time.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#co-parents","Where co-parents start"],"see":["transition-bags","biff-responses"],"ev":"Family practitioners widely recommend short, predictable, conflict-free exchanges for children’s sake. Some families use school or neutral places.","ex":"Keep it to hello, the bag, one kid-related note, and goodbye.","f":"coparenting"},{"id":"schedule-change-protocol","t":"Schedule change protocol","a":["changing the schedule","swapping weekends","last minute changes"],"x":["Last-minute changes are a common source of co-parenting conflict. An agreed protocol (how much notice, how it’s asked, how it’s confirmed) takes the heat out.","Being flexible when you can, and asking for the same, tends to build goodwill over time."],"p":"Write the protocol down, like a WP-03 agreement, so it isn’t renegotiated every time.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["co-parenting-calendar","biff-responses"],"ev":"Practical guidance from family mediation. Agreed notice periods reduce conflict over changes. Always check any court order first.","ex":"“Changes need 48 hours’ notice by text, except emergencies. Confirm in writing.”","f":"coparenting"},{"id":"kids-as-messengers","t":"Kids as messengers","a":["using kids as messengers","tell your dad","children in the middle"],"x":["Asking children to carry messages between parents (even simple ones) puts them in the middle of adult tension. It can make them feel responsible for keeping the peace.","Keeping all parent-to-parent communication direct protects children and keeps messages accurate."],"p":"The relationships page is clear: never use the kids as messengers.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#co-parents","Where co-parents start"],"see":["triangulation","handoffs-between-homes"],"ev":"Research on children of separated parents consistently finds being caught in the middle of parental conflict is harmful for children.","ex":"Instead of “Tell your mom she’s late with the money,” message her directly.","f":"coparenting"},{"id":"talking-kindly-about-the-other-parent","t":"Talking kindly about the other parent","a":["speaking well of the other parent","not badmouthing","in front of the kids"],"x":["Children often feel that criticism of a parent is partly criticism of them. Speaking neutrally or kindly about the other parent, even when it’s hard, protects them.","Venting belongs with adult friends or a professional, not with the children."],"p":"Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive: children pick up tone even more than words.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#co-parents","Where co-parents start"],"see":["kids-as-messengers","parallel-parenting"],"ev":"Research suggests children do better when parents avoid criticizing each other in front of them.","ex":"“Your dad loves you a lot. We just do things differently in our two homes.”","f":"coparenting"},{"id":"co-parenting-check-in-agenda","t":"Co-parenting check-in agenda","a":["co-parent meeting","co-parenting meeting","monthly co-parent review"],"x":["A regular, business-like co-parenting check-in (in person, by phone or by structured email) keeps logistics from piling up into conflict.","A fixed agenda keeps it focused on the kids and away from old issues."],"p":"The co-parent road includes a monthly look-back (WP-04) to catch what keeps slipping.","go":["/workpapers/wp-04-what-keeps-coming-back.html","WP-04: What keeps coming back?"],"see":["co-parenting-calendar","a-simple-agenda-for-a-hard-talk"],"ev":"Structured, business-like co-parenting meetings are recommended by many family practitioners. Evidence is practical.","ex":"Monthly: school updates, health and appointments, activities and costs, upcoming schedule changes, one thing that’s going well.","f":"coparenting"},{"id":"shared-child-expenses","t":"Shared child expenses","a":["splitting kids’ costs","child expenses","school costs"],"x":["Extra costs (school trips, activities, clothes, devices) often cause friction between co-parents. Agreeing in advance which costs are shared, how, and how they’re recorded reduces arguments.","Keep records simple and visible to both."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand’s money side can track shared costs as plain facts.","go":["/lemonade-stand.html","The Lemonade Stand"],"see":["money-and-fairness-splits","co-parenting-calendar"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Any legal arrangements come first; clear records reduce disputes.","ex":"A shared list of kid costs, receipts attached, reviewed at the monthly check-in.","f":"coparenting"},{"id":"new-partners-and-co-parenting","t":"New partners and co-parenting","a":["introducing a new partner","new partner and kids","dating after separation"],"x":["New relationships can stir strong feelings in co-parenting. Going slowly with introductions, and letting the other parent know in advance, usually reduces tension.","Children may need time and reassurance that the new person isn’t replacing anyone."],"p":"The Library’s stepfamilies entry covers blended families in more depth.","go":["/library/life.html#stepfamilies","Stepfamilies (Library)"],"see":["talking-kindly-about-the-other-parent","stepfamily-basics"],"ev":"Family research suggests gradual introductions and giving children time help adjustment. Timing depends on the family.","ex":"A heads-up to the other parent before a new partner meets the kids is often appreciated.","f":"coparenting"},{"id":"holiday-schedules-for-two-homes","t":"Holiday schedules for two homes","a":["holiday schedule","alternating holidays","splitting holidays"],"x":["Holidays can be emotionally loaded for separated families. Planning early, following any agreed schedule, and keeping the children’s experience in focus helps.","Some families create new traditions in each home so children look forward to both."],"p":"The Library’s holidays entry covers family tensions around celebrations.","go":["/library/life.html#holidays","Holidays (Library)"],"see":["co-parenting-calendar","holiday-load"],"ev":"Common practical arrangements include alternating years or splitting days. Court orders and parenting plans come first.","ex":"Settle holiday plans by the end of summer for the whole year, and put them in the shared calendar.","f":"coparenting"},{"id":"high-context-and-low-context-communication","t":"High-context and low-context communication","a":["high context","low context","implicit communication","explicit communication"],"x":["In low-context communication, meaning is mostly in the words: say it plainly. In high-context communication, much of the meaning is in context, tone and what’s left unsaid.","When people from different styles meet, one may feel the other is blunt, and the other may feel the first is vague. Talking about it openly helps."],"p":"Pillar IV: a mismatch is tuning, not a moral failing. Wired Differently covers similar mismatches from wiring.","go":["/library/conflict.html#culture-and-conflict","Culture and conflict (Library)"],"see":["direct-is-not-rude","saving-face"],"ev":"Anthropologist Edward T. Hall described high- and low-context cultures (Beyond Culture, 1976). It’s a useful lens but has been criticized as too broad; individuals vary widely within any culture.","ex":"“In my family, if you didn’t say no, you meant yes. In yours, silence might mean no. Let’s check.”","f":"culture"},{"id":"saving-face","t":"Saving face","a":["face","saving face","losing face","face saving"],"x":["Face is the public image of dignity that people want to protect. Criticism in front of others, or cornering someone so they must admit fault, can feel like losing face, and often triggers defensiveness.","Private conversations, gentle framing and letting people save face make it easier for everyone to change course."],"p":"Check-ins happen in private for the same reason.","go":["/library/conflict.html#culture-and-conflict","Culture and conflict (Library)"],"see":["high-context-and-low-context-communication","conflict-in-front-of-others"],"ev":"Stella Ting-Toomey developed face-negotiation theory in the 1980s. “Face” (public dignity) matters in every culture, with different emphasis.","ex":"Raise a correction privately, and offer a graceful way forward: “Maybe the timing got mixed up.”","f":"culture"},{"id":"individual-and-group-focus","t":"Individual and group focus","a":["individualism","collectivism","me versus we cultures"],"x":["Some cultures emphasize personal choice and independence; others emphasize family and group harmony. These differences shape expectations about decisions, obligations to family, and how conflict is handled.","In mixed-culture relationships, neither approach is wrong. Naming expectations helps."],"p":"The Library’s culture and conflict entry goes deeper.","go":["/library/conflict.html#culture-and-conflict","Culture and conflict (Library)"],"see":["family-scripts-in-daily-life","boundaries-with-parents-as-an-adult"],"ev":"Geert Hofstede and others described differences between more individualist and more collectivist cultures. The framework is influential but criticized for overgeneralizing.","ex":"“In my family, decisions were made together with the grandparents. How was it in yours?”","f":"culture"},{"id":"time-attitudes-across-cultures","t":"Time attitudes across cultures","a":["monochronic","polychronic","punctuality differences","cultural time"],"x":["People differ in how they treat time: some see schedules as firm and lateness as disrespectful; others see time as flexible and relationships as the priority.","Clarifying what a time means (“sharp” or “roughly”) avoids a lot of friction."],"p":"Frequency Calibration maps rhythms and pace, which often reflect culture as well as personality.","go":["/tools/frequency-calibration.html","Frequency Calibration"],"see":["high-context-and-low-context-communication","different-paces"],"ev":"Edward T. Hall described “monochronic” (one thing at a time, schedules) and “polychronic” (many things at once, flexible timing) orientations. Individuals vary within cultures.","ex":"“Is 7 o’clock ‘7 sharp’ or ‘around 7’ for you?”","f":"culture"},{"id":"hierarchy-and-speaking-up","t":"Hierarchy and speaking up","a":["power distance","speaking up to elders","hierarchy in families"],"x":["In some families, workplaces and cultures, questioning elders or bosses directly is uncomfortable or disrespectful. In others, it’s expected.","Knowing this helps you understand why someone might not speak up, and find respectful ways to invite their view."],"p":"The Library’s psychological safety entry covers making it safe to speak up.","go":["/library/conflict.html#culture-and-conflict","Culture and conflict (Library)"],"see":["individual-and-group-focus","managing-up"],"ev":"Hofstede’s “power distance” describes how much people expect and accept unequal power. It helps explain differences in speaking up, though it’s a broad generalization.","ex":"At work: “I want to make sure I’m being respectful. Is it okay if I share a concern?”","f":"culture"},{"id":"intercultural-couples","t":"Intercultural couples","a":["intercultural relationships","mixed culture couples","cross cultural couples"],"x":["Partners from different cultures bring different expectations about family, money, roles, holidays and communication. These can be a source of richness and of friction.","Curiosity (“tell me how it worked in your family”) and choosing traditions together help build a shared culture."],"p":"The Library’s family scripts entry covers bringing different habits into one home.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#partners","Where partners start"],"see":["family-scripts-in-daily-life","individual-and-group-focus"],"ev":"Research on intercultural couples suggests differences in family expectations, communication and roles can create friction, and that talking openly about culture helps.","ex":"“Which traditions from each of our families do we want to keep?”","f":"culture"},{"id":"hospitality-and-food-norms","t":"Hospitality and food norms","a":["hospitality norms","food culture","hosting differences"],"x":["Expectations about hosting (how often, how much food, how long guests stay, whether to refuse the first offer) vary a lot. Mismatched expectations can lead to hurt feelings on both sides.","Talking about them before big gatherings saves misunderstandings."],"p":"Hosting creates invisible work; the Lemonade Stand can make it visible.","go":["/library/life.html#holidays","Holidays (Library)"],"see":["intercultural-couples","holiday-load"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Hospitality norms vary widely across cultures and families.","ex":"“In my family, guests always stay for dinner. What’s normal for you?”","f":"culture"},{"id":"ways-of-showing-care","t":"Ways of showing care","a":["showing love differently","cultural expressions of care","acts of care"],"x":["Some people show love with words, others with food, practical help, time or small gifts. These patterns are often shaped by family and culture.","Recognizing someone’s way of showing care helps you notice it, even when it isn’t your own way."],"p":"Turning Toward is about noticing and answering small reaches for connection, in whatever form they come.","go":["/turning-toward.html","Turning Toward"],"see":["love-languages-caveats","specific-thanks"],"ev":"Cultural research suggests expressions of care vary: some cultures emphasize words, others practical help or food. Individuals vary too.","ex":"“When you fix my bike, I think that’s how you say you care. Is that right?”","f":"culture"},{"id":"asking-about-culture-respectfully","t":"Asking about culture respectfully","a":["asking about someone’s culture","cultural curiosity","respectful questions"],"x":["Asking about someone’s background with genuine curiosity, rather than assumptions, shows respect. Questions about their own experience work better than questions that treat them as representing a whole group.","Listening more than explaining is the key."],"p":"Open questions (in the communication notes) are a good place to start.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["ways-of-showing-care","intercultural-couples"],"ev":"Practical guidance from intercultural communication. Open, curious questions tend to be welcomed more than assumptions.","ex":"“I’d love to understand how holidays worked in your family. What mattered most?”","f":"culture"},{"id":"generational-culture-gaps","t":"Generational culture gaps","a":["generation gap","generational differences","older and younger"],"x":["Different generations may have different norms about phones, work, gender roles, directness and privacy. Stereotypes are often exaggerated, but real differences in habits can cause friction.","Treating them as differences in habit, not in values, makes compromise easier."],"p":"Pillar IV: tune how you send and receive, across ages as well as wiring.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#family","Where families start"],"see":["boundaries-with-parents-as-an-adult","texting-norms-that-prevent-fights"],"ev":"Research on generational differences is mixed; many supposed differences are smaller than stereotypes suggest. Life stage often matters as much as generation.","ex":"“You call; I text. Could we do a quick call on Sundays and texts in between?”","f":"culture"},{"id":"reversible-and-irreversible-decisions","t":"Reversible and irreversible decisions","a":["two way doors","one way doors","reversible decisions"],"x":["Some decisions are easy to undo (a new routine, a restaurant choice); others aren’t (moving house, a big purchase). Reversible decisions can be made quickly and tested. Irreversible ones deserve more time and care.","Treating reversible decisions as experiments takes pressure off both people."],"p":"The monthly look-back (WP-04) treats household changes as experiments to check, not permanent verdicts.","go":["/library/life.html#joint-decisions","Joint decisions (Library)"],"see":["satisficing","sleep-on-it"],"ev":"A decision heuristic popularized by Amazon’s Jeff Bezos (“one-way and two-way doors”). It’s a practical rule of thumb rather than a research finding.","ex":"“This is a two-way door: let’s try the new chore split for a month and switch back if it’s not working.”","f":"decisions"},{"id":"satisficing","t":"Satisficing","a":["good enough decision","satisficer","maximizer"],"x":["Satisficing means choosing the first option that’s good enough, rather than searching for the best one. For many everyday decisions, it saves time and stress, and people are often just as happy with the result.","Save the careful searching for the decisions that really matter."],"p":"The Library’s decision fatigue entry explains why many small decisions wear people out.","go":["/library/motivation.html#decision-fatigue","Decision fatigue (Library)"],"see":["reversible-and-irreversible-decisions","analysis-paralysis"],"ev":"Herbert Simon introduced “satisficing” in the 1950s. Later research (for example, Barry Schwartz and colleagues) suggested “maximizers” who seek the best option are often less satisfied than “satisficers”.","ex":"“Any restaurant that’s nearby and has a veggie option is fine tonight.”","f":"decisions"},{"id":"the-10-10-10-question","t":"The 10-10-10 question","a":["10 10 10","ten minutes ten months ten years","10/10/10"],"x":["Asking how you’ll feel about a choice in ten minutes, ten months and ten years helps separate short-term feelings from long-term priorities.","It’s especially useful for decisions clouded by strong feelings in the moment."],"p":"Pillar III, Read your state first: strong feelings in the moment can distort choices.","go":["/library/life.html#joint-decisions","Joint decisions (Library)"],"see":["reversible-and-irreversible-decisions","sleep-on-it"],"ev":"A decision tool popularized by writer Suzy Welch (10-10-10, 2009). It’s practical advice, not a research-tested method.","ex":"“How will I feel about this in 10 minutes, 10 months and 10 years?”","f":"decisions"},{"id":"pre-mortem","t":"Pre-mortem","a":["premortem","imagine it failed","what could go wrong"],"x":["A pre-mortem imagines a plan has already failed and asks why. It surfaces likely problems before they happen, when they’re easy to plan around.","It works well for new household routines, trips and projects."],"p":"The monthly look-back (WP-04) is the after version; a pre-mortem is the before version.","go":["/workpapers/wp-04-what-keeps-coming-back.html","WP-04: What keeps coming back?"],"see":["reversible-and-irreversible-decisions","retrospective-for-households"],"ev":"Gary Klein described the pre-mortem (Harvard Business Review, 2007). It’s widely used in project planning; evidence is mostly practical.","ex":"“Imagine it’s a month from now and the new chore plan has failed. What went wrong?”","f":"decisions"},{"id":"sleep-on-it","t":"Sleep on it","a":["sleep on it","wait before deciding","decide tomorrow"],"x":["For big or emotional decisions, waiting a day often leads to a calmer choice. Strong feelings fade, and new considerations surface.","It doesn’t work for every decision, and endless postponing becomes avoidance. Set a time to decide."],"p":"WP-02 suggests putting off anything that doesn’t need deciding in the next hour when your battery is high.","go":["/workpapers/wp-02-how-much-are-you-carrying.html","WP-02: How full is your battery?"],"see":["the-10-10-10-question","negotiating-with-deadlines"],"ev":"Common advice. Research on emotion and decision-making suggests strong emotions and tiredness can affect choices; claims about “unconscious thought” helping decisions are debated.","ex":"“This is a big one. Can we both sleep on it and decide tomorrow evening?”","f":"decisions"},{"id":"consensus-and-consent","t":"Consensus and consent","a":["consensus","consent decision making","no objections"],"x":["Consensus means everyone agrees. Consent means nobody has a strong objection. Consent is often faster, especially in groups, because it asks “is this good enough to try?” rather than “is this everyone’s favorite?”.","It works well for households and teams trying something new."],"p":"Treating changes as a trial for a month, then reviewing with WP-04, pairs well with a consent approach.","go":["/library/life.html#joint-decisions","Joint decisions (Library)"],"see":["decision-rights-at-work","deciding-by-domain-at-home"],"ev":"Consent-based decision-making comes from sociocracy and similar approaches. It’s a practical method; research is limited.","ex":"Instead of “does everyone love this?”, ask “can everyone live with this for a month?”","f":"decisions"},{"id":"deciding-by-domain-at-home","t":"Deciding by domain at home","a":["who decides what","decision domains","dividing decisions"],"x":["Not every decision needs two people. Some couples and households divide decisions by area: one person decides dinners, another decides the weekend schedule. Bigger decisions are made together.","It saves time and reduces friction, as long as both people agree the split is fair."],"p":"Frequency Calibration includes decision rhythms, and WP-03 can list decision owners too.","go":["/tools/frequency-calibration.html","Frequency Calibration"],"see":["consensus-and-consent","planning-doing-and-following-through"],"ev":"A practical approach. Research on couple decision-making suggests shared decision-making is linked with satisfaction, and dividing some areas can reduce friction.","ex":"“You decide weekday dinners, I decide the kids’ activities, and we decide money over $200 together.”","f":"decisions"},{"id":"analysis-paralysis","t":"Analysis paralysis","a":["overthinking decisions","can’t decide","decision paralysis"],"x":["Too many options or too much information can make it hard to decide at all. The search feels productive, but nothing moves.","Limiting options, setting a deadline and accepting “good enough” all help."],"p":"The Library covers choice overload and decision fatigue.","go":["/library/life.html#joint-decisions","Joint decisions (Library)"],"see":["satisficing","decision-journals"],"ev":"Research on choice overload (for example, Iyengar and Lepper, 2000) suggests too many options can make choosing harder, though the effect varies across studies.","ex":"Narrow it to three options, set a deadline, and pick.","f":"decisions"},{"id":"decision-journals","t":"Decision journals","a":["decision journal","recording decisions","why we decided"],"x":["A decision journal records what you decided, why, and what you expected. Later, you can look back fairly, without hindsight rewriting the reasons.","It’s useful for big household decisions and for teams."],"p":"REPORT-01 keeps a week-by-week record of your household numbers, which serves a similar purpose.","go":["/workpapers/report-01.html","REPORT-01: Your progress, week by week"],"see":["analysis-paralysis","outcome-bias"],"ev":"Recommended by some decision researchers and investors to reduce hindsight bias. Evidence is mostly practical.","ex":"Write down: the decision, the options, why you chose, and what you expect to happen.","f":"decisions"},{"id":"values-based-decisions","t":"Values-based decisions","a":["deciding by values","what matters most","values in decisions"],"x":["When options seem equal, asking “which fits what we care about most?” can break the tie. Naming shared values also makes it easier to accept a decision you didn’t fully love.","It helps to name your values before the options are on the table, so you’re not tempted to pick values that justify the choice you already wanted."],"p":"The Library’s values entry goes deeper.","go":["/library/motivation.html#values","Values (Library)"],"see":["values-clarification","the-10-10-10-question"],"ev":"Values-based approaches (for example, in acceptance and commitment therapy) suggest aligning choices with values supports wellbeing. This is general information.","ex":"“We both value time together more than money right now. So the cheaper holiday, with more days, makes sense.”","f":"decisions"},{"id":"pros-and-cons-that-actually-help","t":"Pros and cons that actually help","a":["pros and cons list","weighing options","decision matrix"],"x":["A plain list of pros and cons can mislead if every item counts equally. Weighting each item by importance, and noticing gut reactions to the result, makes the list more useful.","Doing it together also shows what matters to each person."],"p":"Useful for joint decisions; the Library’s joint decisions entry covers deciding together.","go":["/library/life.html#joint-decisions","Joint decisions (Library)"],"see":["values-based-decisions","analysis-paralysis"],"ev":"A simple, long-standing decision tool. Weighting items by importance generally makes it more useful than simply counting.","ex":"List pros and cons, then give each a weight from 1 to 3 for how much it matters.","f":"decisions"},{"id":"default-decisions","t":"Default decisions","a":["default decisions","decide once","standing decisions"],"x":["Making a decision once and letting it stand (a weekly meal plan, a set chore day, a regular date night) removes repeated negotiation.","Defaults can be changed, but they don’t need to be decided every time."],"p":"Pillar V is about the power of defaults. WP-03 is a set of standing decisions about who owns what.","go":["/five-pillars.html#quiet-incentives","Pillar V: Notice the quiet incentives"],"see":["defaults-at-home","deciding-by-domain-at-home"],"ev":"Research on decision fatigue and defaults suggests reducing repeated choices can ease daily stress, though the size of decision-fatigue effects is debated.","ex":"“Taco Tuesday, pasta Thursday. Two fewer dinner decisions every week.”","f":"decisions"},{"id":"dont-fight-by-text","t":"Don’t fight by text","a":["fighting over text","arguing by text","texting arguments"],"x":["Text strips out tone, timing and facial expression, so disagreements escalate easily. Each person fills the gaps with their worst guess, and replies get sharper.","Moving hard conversations to voice or in person, and keeping texts for logistics and warmth, prevents a lot of misunderstandings."],"p":"The Conversation Reader shows where a text thread turned, and suggests a calmer reply.","go":["/conversation-reader.html","The Conversation Reader"],"see":["when-to-switch-to-voice","cooling-off-before-replying"],"ev":"Research on text communication suggests tone is often misread and negative intent is overestimated. Many relationship experts advise against resolving conflict by text.","ex":"“I don’t want to argue over text. Can we talk tonight?”","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"when-to-switch-to-voice","t":"When to switch to voice","a":["call instead of text","move to a call","switch channels"],"x":["Signs it’s time to switch from text to a call: the thread is getting longer and sharper, you’re rereading messages for hidden meaning, or feelings are involved.","A two-minute call often clears up what twenty messages couldn’t. If the other person prefers writing, a calm, longer message can work too."],"p":"The Carrier Wave Decoder helps when a conversation, in any channel, starts going sideways.","go":["/carrier-wave-decoder.html","The Carrier Wave Decoder"],"see":["dont-fight-by-text","choosing-the-channel"],"ev":"Communication research suggests voice carries emotional cues that text lacks. Some people prefer text for hard topics, so ask.","ex":"“This thread is getting tangled. Quick call?”","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"texting-norms-that-prevent-fights","t":"Texting norms that prevent fights","a":["texting rules","texting etiquette","text norms"],"x":["People text very differently: some are brief, some use lots of emoji, some reply instantly, some once a day. Without shared norms, brief replies can seem cold and slow replies can seem like ignoring.","Talking about texting habits openly, and agreeing a few basics, removes a lot of guesswork."],"p":"The Signal Translator helps you see how a message may land before you send it.","go":["/signal-translator.html","The Signal Translator"],"see":["dont-fight-by-text","tone-indicators"],"ev":"Practical guidance. People have very different texting habits, and mismatched expectations are a common source of friction.","ex":"Agree a few basics: “A thumbs-up means yes, not annoyed. No serious topics by text.”","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"response-time-expectations","t":"Response time expectations","a":["reply time","slow replies","left on read","not replying"],"x":["Different people have very different expectations about how quickly messages should be answered. A slow reply is usually about a busy day, not about you.","Saying your usual response pattern out loud (“I check messages at lunch and in the evening”) prevents worry and hurt."],"p":"The Wiring Card can include what silence means for you.","go":["/wiring-card.html","The Wiring Card"],"see":["texting-norms-that-prevent-fights","notifications-and-boundaries"],"ev":"Research on messaging suggests people often interpret slow replies negatively, even though delays usually have ordinary causes.","ex":"“I often can’t reply during work. No reply means busy, not upset.”","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"read-receipts","t":"Read receipts","a":["read receipts","seen but no reply","left on read"],"x":["Read receipts show when a message has been seen, which can create pressure to reply instantly and worry when there’s no reply.","Some people find them reassuring; others find them stressful. Agreeing on them (or turning them off) is a small kindness."],"p":"The Wiring Card is a place to note preferences like this.","go":["/wiring-card.html","The Wiring Card"],"see":["response-time-expectations","texting-norms-that-prevent-fights"],"ev":"A common source of anxiety in messaging. Research on read receipts is limited, but people report reading meaning into them.","ex":"Decide together whether you use read receipts, and agree that “seen” doesn’t mean “ignoring you”.","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"group-chat-norms","t":"Group chat norms","a":["group chat rules","group chat etiquette","muting group chats"],"x":["Group chats work best with a clear purpose (planning, sharing news) and a shared understanding that muting is fine and not everyone needs to reply.","Disagreements in group chats tend to escalate because everyone watches. Taking them private helps."],"p":"The Conversation Reader can help you understand a tense thread.","go":["/conversation-reader.html","The Conversation Reader"],"see":["family-group-chats","dont-fight-by-text"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Group chats combine many readers and little tone, which makes misunderstandings more likely.","ex":"“This group is for plans and photos. Anything personal, let’s take it to a private message.”","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"emails-that-get-read","t":"Emails that get read","a":["clear emails","email subject lines","writing clear emails"],"x":["Emails get read and answered faster when the subject line says what’s needed, the request comes first, and there’s one clear ask.","Long emails with the request buried at the end often get missed."],"p":"The Signal Translator helps with tone, and one ask at a time helps with clarity.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#coworkers","Where coworkers and teams start"],"see":["status-updates-that-help","one-ask-at-a-time"],"ev":"Practical guidance from workplace communication. Short, clear emails with one request tend to get faster, more accurate replies.","ex":"Subject: “Decision needed by Friday: venue for team lunch.” Body: two options and your recommendation.","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"notifications-and-boundaries","t":"Notifications and boundaries","a":["notifications","turning off notifications","always on"],"x":["Constant notifications pull attention away from the people and tasks in front of you. Setting quiet times, and telling people when you’re offline, protects focus and presence.","It’s a boundary, and it’s okay to have it."],"p":"The Library’s phones-and-presence entry covers how phones affect connection.","go":["/library/calm.html#phones-and-presence","Phones and presence (Library)"],"see":["response-time-expectations","boundaries-at-work"],"ev":"Research on interruptions suggests frequent notifications can fragment attention. Effects vary by person and context.","ex":"Turn off non-urgent notifications during dinner and after 9 p.m.","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"location-sharing-and-consent","t":"Location sharing and consent","a":["location sharing","tracking location","sharing location"],"x":["Sharing locations can feel reassuring for some couples, families and friends, and intrusive for others. It works best when it’s mutual, freely agreed, and easy to change.","It shouldn’t be required as proof of trust."],"p":"The program’s approach is that each person decides what they share about themselves.","go":["/legal/privacy-policy.html","Privacy policy (this site)"],"see":["kinds-of-boundaries","posting-about-others-online"],"ev":"A common modern discussion topic. The healthiest arrangements are based on mutual, freely given agreement.","ex":"“Would you be comfortable sharing locations when we travel? Totally fine if not.”","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"posting-about-others-online","t":"Posting about others online","a":["posting photos of others","sharing online","asking before posting"],"x":["Not everyone likes being posted about online. Asking before posting photos or stories about others (including kids, as they get older) respects their privacy.","It’s a small courtesy that can prevent a lot of hurt."],"p":"A boundary about digital life, and a quick act of respect.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#family","Where families start"],"see":["location-sharing-and-consent","kinds-of-boundaries"],"ev":"A common modern etiquette topic. People’s comfort with being posted about varies widely.","ex":"“Mind if I post this photo? Happy not to.”","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"voice-notes-etiquette","t":"Voice notes etiquette","a":["voice notes","voice messages","audio messages"],"x":["Voice notes carry tone better than text, which can help with warmth. But long voice notes can be hard for busy listeners, and some people process text more easily.","Asking which someone prefers is simple and kind."],"p":"Pillar IV: tune how you send so the other person can receive.","go":["/signal-translator.html","The Signal Translator"],"see":["texting-norms-that-prevent-fights","when-to-switch-to-voice"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Preferences vary: some people love voice notes, others find them hard to listen to.","ex":"“Do you prefer voice notes or texts? I’ll send whichever’s easier for you.”","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"the-pause-before-sending","t":"The pause before sending","a":["pause before sending","send delay","draft first"],"x":["A short pause before sending an emotional message gives you a chance to read it as the other person will. Many messages get softened, shortened or deleted in those few minutes.","Some email apps let you delay sending. A draft folder works too."],"p":"WP-09 is a quick self-check before you reply to something that stung.","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["cooling-off-before-replying","dont-fight-by-text"],"ev":"Practical advice. Emotion typically fades with time, and rereading a draft often reveals a harsher tone than intended.","ex":"Write it, wait five minutes, reread it as if you were receiving it, then decide.","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"social-media-and-comparison-at-home","t":"Social media and comparison at home","a":["comparing to other couples","instagram families","comparison online"],"x":["Online, other households often look tidier, calmer and happier than yours. What you’re seeing is a highlight reel, not the whole week, including the invisible work behind it.","Noticing when scrolling leaves you feeling worse about your home or relationship is useful. A break, or following fewer accounts that spark comparison, can help."],"p":"The Library’s social comparison entry goes deeper. The Lemonade Stand shows your real week, which is a kinder comparison.","go":["/library/life.html#social-comparison","Social comparison (Library)"],"see":["envy","notifications-and-boundaries"],"ev":"Research on social comparison and social media suggests seeing curated highlights of others’ lives can lower satisfaction for some people. Findings vary, and effects are often modest.","ex":"“Their photos are the best five minutes of their week. Ours looks different, and that’s okay.”","f":"digital-etiquette"},{"id":"the-emotion-wheel","t":"The emotion wheel","a":["emotion wheel","feelings wheel","feeling wheel","Plutchik wheel"],"x":["An emotion wheel arranges feeling words in rings: a few broad feelings in the middle (like happy, sad, angry, scared), with more specific words further out (like content, lonely, irritated, anxious). You start with the broad word and move outward to find a better fit.","It’s useful when you only have “fine”, “bad” or “stressed” to hand. More precise words help you understand what you need, and help others understand you."],"p":"The Library’s entry on emotional granularity explains why precise feeling words help. WP-09 asks for “the feeling underneath”, and the wheel is a handy way to find it.","go":["/library/emotions.html#emotional-granularity","Emotional granularity (Library)"],"see":["primary-and-secondary-emotions","feelings-intensity-scale","mixed-feelings"],"ev":"Robert Plutchik proposed a wheel of emotions in 1980, and Gloria Willcox published “The Feeling Wheel” in 1982. They’re teaching tools, not settled science: researchers still debate how emotions should be categorized.","ex":"If “bad” is all you can find, look at the wheel: is it closer to sad, scared or angry? Then go one ring out: lonely, anxious, frustrated?","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"primary-and-secondary-emotions","t":"Primary and secondary emotions","a":["primary emotions","secondary emotions","the feeling under the feeling"],"x":["Sometimes the first feeling we show isn’t the deepest one. Anger often sits on top of hurt, fear or embarrassment. Irritation can sit on top of exhaustion. The surface feeling is real, but the one underneath is often what needs attention.","Sharing the underneath feeling (“I felt left out”) usually invites care, while the surface one (“I’m furious”) tends to invite defense."],"p":"WP-09 asks for the feeling underneath, like tired or unseen, before the fact and the ask.","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["the-emotion-wheel","anger-as-a-signal","hurt"],"ev":"A common idea in emotion-focused approaches and everyday counseling. The exact split is debated by researchers, but the practical point (look underneath the first feeling) is widely used.","ex":"“I snapped because I was angry, but underneath I think I was scared we’d be late and it’d be my fault.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"feelings-intensity-scale","t":"Feelings intensity scale","a":["intensity scale","one to ten feelings","how strong is it"],"x":["Rating a feeling from 1 to 10 turns a vague storm into something you can track and share. A 3 is “noticeable”; an 8 is “hard to think past”. Numbers can be easier to say than words, especially in a tense moment.","It also shows you when a feeling is fading, which it usually does, and helps you judge whether now is the time for a talk."],"p":"Drift asks how strong a feeling is before it begins. Today’s Weather and WP-02 do a similar check on your overall load.","go":["/quick-checks.html#today","Today’s Weather"],"see":["the-emotion-wheel","body-clues-of-emotion"],"ev":"Rating intensity is used in many coaching and counseling settings. It’s a self-report tool, not a measurement, but it helps people notice change.","ex":"“I’m about a 7 out of 10 frustrated right now, so I’d like to wait before we talk.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"mixed-feelings","t":"Mixed feelings","a":["ambivalence","two feelings at once","conflicted"],"x":["It’s common to feel two things at once: proud and scared about a new job, relieved and guilty about saying no, happy for a friend and a little envious. Mixed feelings don’t mean you’re confused. They mean the situation matters in more than one way.","Naming both, without rushing to pick one, often makes them easier to hold."],"p":"Know your own wiring helps you sort where a feeling comes from: your wiring, a learned pattern, or today’s conditions.","go":["/know-yourself.html","Know your own wiring"],"see":["the-emotion-wheel","relief"],"ev":"Research on mixed emotions shows people can feel positive and negative emotions at the same time, especially during transitions. It’s a normal experience.","ex":"“I’m relieved we decided, and also a bit sad. Both are true.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"body-clues-of-emotion","t":"Body clues of emotion","a":["body signals","where I feel it","physical signs of feelings"],"x":["Emotions often show up in the body before we have words: a tight chest, a hot face, a clenched jaw, a sinking stomach, restless legs. Learning your own early signs gives you a head start to pause before a reaction takes over.","Everyone’s map is different. Some people notice very little in their body, especially when stressed; that’s okay, and it can be practiced gently."],"p":"The Calm-Down Kit asks where you feel it first. The Library’s entry on interoception goes deeper.","go":["/wp-11.html","WP-11: The Calm-Down Kit"],"see":["feelings-intensity-scale","stress-versus-pressure"],"ev":"Research on interoception (sensing your body’s internal state) suggests people vary a lot in how well they notice body signals. Noticing can be practiced.","ex":"“My jaw is tight and my shoulders are up. I’m more wound up than I thought.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"frustration","t":"Frustration","a":["frustrated","fed up","exasperated"],"x":["Frustration is the feeling of something getting in the way of what you want or expect: a task that won’t work, a plan that keeps changing, a request that keeps getting missed. It often builds with repetition.","Frustration is useful information. It points to a need or goal that matters to you. Naming the goal (“I want the evenings to feel calmer”) often turns frustration into a request."],"p":"Repeated frustration about the same thing is a sign to look at the setup: the monthly look-back (WP-04) is designed for exactly that.","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["anger-as-a-signal","impatience","irritation"],"ev":"Psychologists generally describe frustration as a response to a blocked goal. It’s among the most common everyday emotions.","ex":"“I’m frustrated because I’ve asked about this three times, and I want us to find a way that works.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"disappointment","t":"Disappointment","a":["disappointed","let down","letdown"],"x":["Disappointment comes when something we hoped for doesn’t happen. It’s often quieter than anger, and easier to hide, which can let it pile up unspoken.","Saying it simply and without blame (“I’m disappointed, I was looking forward to it”) is honest and usually lands softly. It also helps others understand what matters to you."],"p":"If disappointment keeps coming from the same source (like plans falling through), look at the setup: a shared calendar, clearer owners, or a check-in on expectations.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["frustration","hurt"],"ev":"Disappointment is widely studied as a reaction to unmet expectations. It’s a normal part of close relationships.","ex":"“I was really looking forward to Saturday, so I’m disappointed. Can we find another day?”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"irritation","t":"Irritation","a":["irritated","annoyed","annoyance","grumpy"],"x":["Irritation is a low-level, prickly annoyance. On its own it’s small. When your battery is low, though, small annoyances stack up quickly and can come out sharper than you mean.","Noticing “I’m just irritable today” can stop you from turning a general mood into a complaint about someone."],"p":"WP-02 separates how much you’re already carrying from how upset you are about one thing. Irritation is often mostly the first.","go":["/workpapers/wp-02-how-much-are-you-carrying.html","WP-02: How full is your battery?"],"see":["frustration","hungry-tired-and-snappy"],"ev":"Everyday irritability is strongly linked with tiredness, hunger and stress in research on mood. Small annoyances land bigger when you’re depleted.","ex":"“I’m irritable today, and it’s not about you. Let me get some food and I’ll be better company.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"resentment","t":"Resentment","a":["resentful","resent","bitterness","silent resentment"],"x":["Resentment is a slow-building feeling that something unfair keeps happening, often without being acknowledged. It tends to grow in silence: each unmentioned unfairness adds a little more.","It’s best treated as information, not as a character flaw. It usually points to invisible work, an unbalanced setup or a need that hasn’t been said out loud. Saying it early, and kindly, stops it hardening."],"p":"The Preface calls the pile of unseen work “unbilled debt”. The Lemonade Stand and WP-01 help make it visible before it turns into resentment.","go":["/book/preface.html","Preface: The work nobody sees"],"see":["frustration","kinkeeping","thanking-for-invisible-work"],"ev":"Relationship research links resentment with perceived unfairness, especially in unpaid household work. It tends to grow when grievances go unspoken.","ex":"“I’ve been quietly resentful about always doing the planning. I don’t want to be, so I want to talk about it.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"hurt","t":"Hurt","a":["hurt feelings","feeling hurt","wounded"],"x":["Hurt is the pain of feeling let down, dismissed or not valued by someone you care about. Because it feels vulnerable, many people show anger or silence instead.","Saying “I felt hurt” is often more effective than saying “you were rude”, because it invites the other person to care rather than to defend."],"p":"The Check-ins guide suggests saying one fact and how it landed, then letting them say it back.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["primary-and-secondary-emotions","disappointment"],"ev":"Research on hurt feelings links them to feeling devalued by someone who matters to us. Hurt often sits underneath anger.","ex":"“When you laughed at my idea in front of everyone, I felt hurt. I don’t think you meant it that way.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"embarrassment","t":"Embarrassment","a":["embarrassed","mortified","awkward"],"x":["Embarrassment is the hot, awkward feeling of having been seen doing something clumsy or wrong. It’s strong but usually short-lived.","It helps to remember that others notice our slips much less than we think. A light acknowledgment (“well, that was awkward!”) often ends it faster than hiding."],"p":"The Library’s spotlight effect entry explains why embarrassment feels bigger inside than it looks outside.","go":["/library/thinking.html#spotlight-effect","The spotlight effect (Library)"],"see":["spotlight-feelings-after-a-mistake","hurt"],"ev":"Research on the spotlight effect (Gilovich and colleagues, 2000) found people overestimate how much others notice their mistakes and appearance.","ex":"“That was embarrassing. Most people probably forgot it by lunch.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"spotlight-feelings-after-a-mistake","t":"Spotlight feelings after a mistake","a":["after a mistake","I messed up in public","replaying a mistake"],"x":["After a public mistake, it’s natural to replay it and imagine everyone else is too. Usually they aren’t. The replay is mostly happening in your own head.","A useful move is to turn the replay into one practical step (an apology, a fix, a note for next time) and then give yourself permission to stop."],"p":"If replaying becomes a habit, the Library’s rumination entry and the Calm-Down Kit can help settle it.","go":["/library/thinking.html#spotlight-effect","The spotlight effect (Library)"],"see":["embarrassment","self-forgiveness"],"ev":"Based on the spotlight-effect research and on work on rumination. Replaying a mistake rarely changes it.","ex":"Write one line: what happened, what you’d do differently, and one small repair. Then close the notebook.","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"envy","t":"Envy","a":["envious","envy versus jealousy","comparing"],"x":["Envy is wanting something another person has: their time off, their job, their calm household. It’s a normal feeling, and it can point to something you want more of.","It becomes a problem when it turns into resentment of the other person. Asking “what does this tell me I want?” turns envy into direction."],"p":"The Library’s social comparison entry explains why comparison often makes us feel worse. The Library also has a separate entry on jealousy.","go":["/library/life.html#social-comparison","Social comparison (Library)"],"see":["social-media-and-comparison-at-home","mixed-feelings"],"ev":"Researchers distinguish envy (wanting what someone else has) from jealousy (fearing losing someone to a rival). Both are common and normal.","ex":"“I felt envious when I saw their holiday photos. Maybe I just need a break too.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"anxiety-and-fear","t":"Anxiety and fear","a":["fear versus anxiety","anxious","scared","afraid"],"x":["Fear tends to be about something here and now. Anxiety tends to be about what might happen. Both show up in the body: a racing heart, tight chest, restlessness.","Everyday anxiety before a hard conversation is very normal, and a bit of preparation helps. If anxiety is intense, frequent or getting in the way of life, it’s worth talking with a qualified professional."],"p":"The Calm-Down Kit and the Night Garden’s box breathing are gentle ways to settle before a hard talk.","go":["/wp-11.html","WP-11: The Calm-Down Kit"],"see":["worry-versus-problem-solving","dread"],"ev":"Psychologists often describe fear as a response to a present threat and anxiety as a response to a possible future one. This is general information, not a diagnosis.","ex":"“I’m anxious about the talk tonight. Can we agree to keep it to one topic and take a break if it gets heavy?”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"worry-versus-problem-solving","t":"Worry versus problem-solving","a":["worry versus planning","productive worry","worrying"],"x":["Worry and problem-solving can feel similar, but they’re different. Problem-solving ends in a next step. Worry circles, asking “what if?” without landing.","A simple test helps: can I act on this now? If so, pick one step. If not, set it aside on purpose, perhaps with a note, so your mind doesn’t have to keep holding it."],"p":"The Library’s rumination entry goes deeper. Writing it down is also the idea behind the parking lot in check-ins.","go":["/library/emotions.html#rumination","Rumination (Library)"],"see":["anxiety-and-fear","dread","worry-time"],"ev":"Research on worry distinguishes repetitive worrying from constructive problem-solving. Turning worries into specific next steps is a common technique in cognitive approaches.","ex":"Ask: “Is there something I can do about this today?” If yes, write the step. If no, give the worry a time slot later.","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"dread","t":"Dread","a":["dreading","sense of dread","dreading a conversation"],"x":["Dread is the heavy feeling of anticipating something unpleasant: a difficult talk, a family gathering, a bill. It often grows the longer something is put off.","Shrinking the event (a short, scheduled talk with one topic) and preparing one opening sentence usually makes it more manageable than the dread suggested."],"p":"The Signal Translator lets you test your opening line, which takes a lot of the dread out of starting.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["anxiety-and-fear","a-simple-agenda-for-a-hard-talk"],"ev":"Research on anticipation suggests people often dread upcoming unpleasant events more than the events themselves turn out to warrant. Waiting can be the hardest part.","ex":"“I’ve been dreading this talk, so I’d like to just get it started. Can we do ten minutes now?”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"overwhelm-as-a-feeling","t":"Overwhelm as a feeling","a":["feeling overwhelmed","overwhelm","swamped"],"x":["Overwhelm is the feeling that there’s more than you can handle: too many tasks, too much noise, too many decisions. It often makes it harder to start anything, which adds to the pile.","It’s usually a signal about load, not about your ability. Getting everything out of your head onto paper, and choosing one thing, is often the first way through."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand and WP-01 help you see the whole load; WP-02 helps you notice how full your battery is.","go":["/workpapers/wp-02-how-much-are-you-carrying.html","WP-02: How full is your battery?"],"see":["irritation","energy-audit"],"ev":"Overwhelm isn’t a technical term, but it describes something research on stress calls high demands relative to resources.","ex":"“I’m overwhelmed. Can we list what’s on my plate and see what can move?”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"helplessness","t":"Helplessness","a":["helpless","powerless","nothing I do matters"],"x":["Helplessness is the feeling that nothing you do will make a difference. It often grows when problems repeat and efforts seem to go nowhere.","Finding one small thing within your control, and doing it, can gently push back against that feeling. Small wins add up. If helplessness is heavy or lasting, please reach out to someone you trust or a qualified professional."],"p":"The Library’s small wins entry explains why tiny progress matters. WP-04 helps you see that a repeating problem is the setup, not you.","go":["/library/motivation.html#small-wins","Small wins (Library)"],"see":["small-wins-at-home","overwhelm-as-a-feeling"],"ev":"Research on perceived control suggests that feeling in control of even small things supports wellbeing. Persistent helplessness is worth talking to a professional about.","ex":"“I can’t fix the whole thing, but I can decide what we have for dinner tonight.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"insecurity","t":"Insecurity","a":["insecure","feeling insecure","not good enough feeling"],"x":["Insecurity is doubt about whether you’re good enough, liked enough or safe in a relationship. Everyone feels it sometimes, especially when tired, stressed or in new situations.","Naming it simply, and asking for what would help, is often more effective than testing people or looking for hidden signs."],"p":"Know your own wiring helps you separate a lasting pattern from how today is going. The Library covers attachment and self-esteem in depth.","go":["/know-yourself.html","Know your own wiring"],"see":["self-talk","reassurance-that-helps"],"ev":"A broad everyday word covering self-doubt and worry about acceptance. Research on self-esteem and attachment both touch on it.","ex":"“I’m feeling a bit insecure today. A quick ‘we’re good’ would really help.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"reassurance-that-helps","t":"Reassurance that helps","a":["reassurance","asking for reassurance","how to reassure someone"],"x":["Good reassurance is warm, specific and honest: “I’m not angry, I’m just tired,” or “I love you, and I want to sort this out together.” It speaks to the actual worry.","If someone needs reassurance very often about the same thing, it may help to gently talk about the worry itself, or what would help them feel steadier, rather than repeating the same words."],"p":"Turning Toward’s small moments of connection are a steady, everyday form of reassurance.","go":["/turning-toward.html","Turning Toward"],"see":["insecurity","anxiety-and-fear"],"ev":"Research suggests reassurance is comforting, but repeated reassurance-seeking can sometimes keep anxiety going. Balance and specific, warm responses help.","ex":"Instead of “Stop worrying,” try “I’m here, and we’re okay. What’s on your mind?”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"contentment","t":"Contentment","a":["content","contented","satisfied","at ease"],"x":["Contentment is a calm sense that things are okay right now. It’s quieter than joy and easy to miss, especially in busy lives focused on the next problem.","Noticing and naming contentment, even for a moment, can make ordinary good times feel fuller."],"p":"The Library’s savoring entry explains how lingering on good moments helps. The Night Garden is a place to practice a few minutes of calm.","go":["/library/connection.html#savoring","Savoring (Library)"],"see":["relief","joy"],"ev":"Research on positive emotions treats contentment as a low-energy, pleasant state. It tends to get overlooked because it’s quiet.","ex":"“This is nice. Just sitting here with a cup of tea.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"relief","t":"Relief","a":["relieved","weight off my shoulders"],"x":["Relief is the release when a worry passes or a burden is lifted. It’s worth saying out loud, because it tells the other person their effort mattered.","Relief sometimes comes mixed with other feelings, like tiredness or even sadness. That’s normal."],"p":"Sharing relief and thanks fits Pillar V: appreciation keeps things steady. The daily check-in has a line for one thing you appreciated.","go":["/workpapers/wp-13-daily-check-in.html","WP-13: The 90-second daily check-in"],"see":["mixed-feelings","contentment"],"ev":"Relief is widely described in emotion research as the feeling after a threat or burden passes.","ex":"“I’m so relieved we sorted out the schedule. Thank you.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"pride","t":"Pride","a":["proud","feeling proud","healthy pride"],"x":["Pride in something you worked for is a healthy feeling. It helps you notice progress and keep going.","Shared pride (“we did that”) is especially good for relationships, households and teams. It builds a sense of being on the same side."],"p":"Noticing progress over weeks is what REPORT-01 is for: your record, so progress builds instead of starting over.","go":["/library/motivation.html#small-wins","Small wins (Library)"],"see":["small-wins-at-home","joy"],"ev":"Researchers distinguish pride in effort or achievement from arrogant pride. The first is linked with motivation and wellbeing.","ex":"“I’m proud of us for sticking with the daily check-in for a whole month.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"joy","t":"Joy","a":["joyful","happiness in the moment","delight"],"x":["Joy is a bright, lively feeling of delight. It often comes from small things: a good joke, a message from a friend, a sunny morning.","Sharing joy is a form of connection. When someone shares good news or a delight with you, responding with warmth and interest strengthens the bond."],"p":"The Library’s entry on capitalization covers responding to good news. Turning Toward is full of small ways to share joy.","go":["/library/connection.html#savoring","Savoring (Library)"],"see":["contentment","amusement"],"ev":"Research on positive emotions suggests they broaden attention and help build relationships over time, though the size of these effects is debated.","ex":"“That made my day. Look at this!”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"hope","t":"Hope","a":["hopeful","optimism","looking forward"],"x":["Hope is believing things can get better and seeing at least one way there. It isn’t the same as ignoring problems.","In relationships, hope often comes from a concrete plan: one change to try, a time to look back. Hope grows when small plans work."],"p":"The program’s whole approach is hopeful: problems usually live in the setup, and setups can be changed.","go":["/five-pillars.html","The Five Pillars"],"see":["joy","small-wins-at-home"],"ev":"Research on hope (for example, C. R. Snyder’s work) describes it as having goals plus ways to reach them. It’s linked with coping in many studies.","ex":"“It’s hard now, but I think if we try the new setup for a month, it could get easier.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"amusement","t":"Amusement","a":["amused","laughter","finding it funny"],"x":["Amusement is the light feeling of finding something funny. Shared amusement is one of the easiest ways to feel close.","Gentle humor can defuse a tense moment, but only if it’s shared, not aimed at the other person."],"p":"The Library’s humor entry explains the difference between humor that connects and humor that stings.","go":["/library/communication.html#humour","Humor (Library)"],"see":["joy","humor-that-repairs"],"ev":"Research suggests shared laughter can ease tension and build closeness, though humor at someone’s expense has the opposite effect.","ex":"When a small tension breaks into a shared laugh, let it. It’s a good sign.","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"humor-that-repairs","t":"Humor that repairs","a":["repair humor","joking to make up","breaking tension with a joke"],"x":["A light joke, a silly face or a shared reference can signal “we’re okay” in the middle of a tense moment. It works best when the tension is mild and the humor is about the situation, not the person.","If the other person is really hurt, humor can feel dismissive. Then a sincere word works better."],"p":"The Library’s repair-attempts entry covers the many small ways people signal “let’s not fight”.","go":["/library/relationships.html#repair-attempts","Repair attempts (Library)"],"see":["amusement","anatomy-of-an-apology"],"ev":"Relationship research counts humor among common repair attempts. Whether it works depends on timing and whether both people are in on the joke.","ex":"After a silly argument about the thermostat: “Okay, so we’re arguing about two degrees. Truce?”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"impatience","t":"Impatience","a":["impatient","can’t wait","hurry"],"x":["Impatience is the tense feeling of wanting something to happen faster. Between people, it often comes from different paces: one person decides fast, the other slowly.","Naming the pace difference, and agreeing a time, usually helps more than pushing."],"p":"Frequency Calibration maps your natural pace in areas like decisions and money, and suggests agreements to close the gap.","go":["/tools/frequency-calibration.html","Frequency Calibration"],"see":["frustration","different-paces"],"ev":"Impatience is closely tied to differences in pace and expectations. The program’s Frequency Framework treats pace mismatch as a common source of friction.","ex":"“I’m getting impatient, but I know you like to think things through. When could you give me an answer?”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"defensiveness-as-a-feeling","t":"Defensiveness as a feeling","a":["feeling defensive","getting defensive","defensive reaction"],"x":["Feeling defensive is a natural response to feeling criticized or blamed. The body tenses, and the mind rushes to explain, justify or counter-attack.","Noticing the defensive urge, and taking a breath before responding, gives you a chance to hear the need underneath the criticism. Owning even a small part lowers the heat for both people."],"p":"WP-09 asks: are you answering their words, or an old argument? That question is a good pause for defensiveness.","go":["/library/relationships.html#four-horsemen","The four horsemen (Library)"],"see":["hurt","owning-your-part"],"ev":"Relationship research lists defensiveness as a common pattern in escalating conflict. Taking some responsibility is the usual antidote.","ex":"Notice the urge to say “but I…”, pause, and try: “You’re right that I forgot. Let me fix it.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"feeling-flat","t":"Feeling flat","a":["flat","numb","empty","checked out"],"x":["Sometimes, instead of feeling a lot, you feel very little: flat, far away, hard to get going. It’s often a sign of being depleted rather than of not caring.","Telling others (“I’m flat today, it’s not about you”) prevents them from reading your quietness as distance or anger. If the flatness lasts a long time, please talk with someone you trust or a professional."],"p":"Today’s Weather calls this “fogged in”: running on empty. It suggests what today is good for, and what to leave until the fog lifts.","go":["/quick-checks.html#today","Today’s Weather"],"see":["body-clues-of-emotion","helplessness"],"ev":"Feeling flat or far away is a common response to stress and exhaustion. Persistent numbness or low mood is worth talking to a qualified professional about.","ex":"“I’m feeling a bit flat today. Not upset with anyone, just low on everything.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"anger-as-a-signal","t":"Anger as a signal","a":["anger signal","what anger tells you","using anger well"],"x":["Anger often points to something that feels unfair, disrespectful or blocked. The feeling itself is information. What you do with it is what matters.","Pausing until the intensity drops, then naming the unfairness clearly and calmly, usually gets further than acting in the heat."],"p":"The Library’s anger entry goes deeper, including the myth that venting helps. The Calm-Down Kit (WP-11) helps you plan the pause.","go":["/library/emotions.html#anger","Anger (Library)"],"see":["frustration","primary-and-secondary-emotions"],"ev":"Emotion researchers often describe anger as a response to perceived unfairness or blocked goals. How it’s expressed matters more for relationships than whether it’s felt.","ex":"“I’m angry because I think this is unfair. Let me cool down, and then I want to talk about the split.”","f":"emotions-vocabulary"},{"id":"triangulation","t":"Triangulation","a":["triangulation","triangles in families","pulling in a third person"],"x":["Triangulation happens when tension between two people gets routed through a third: a parent complains to a child about the other parent, or two siblings talk about a third instead of to them. It can relieve pressure for a while but keeps the real problem from being solved.","The person in the middle often feels stuck and stressed. Gently stepping out (“I love you both, but I’d rather you talk to each other”) usually helps everyone."],"p":"For co-parents, never use children as messengers. The relationships page’s family section suggests agreeing who owns what, so nobody has to guess.","go":["/library/life.html#family-systems","Family systems (Library)"],"see":["informal-mediation-between-friends-or-family","shuttle-conversations"],"ev":"Triangulation comes from Murray Bowen’s family systems theory. It’s influential in family therapy; the theory as a whole has limited experimental testing, but the pattern is widely recognized.","ex":"Instead of venting to your sister about your mom, try talking to your mom directly, or asking your sister to stay out of the middle.","f":"family-systems"},{"id":"differentiation-of-self","t":"Differentiation of self","a":["differentiation","staying yourself in a family","being close without losing yourself"],"x":["Differentiation means being able to stay close to your family while still thinking and choosing for yourself. You can hear their feelings without being swept up in them, and hold your own view without cutting them off.","It’s a lifelong process, not a finish line. It often shows up as calmer responses to family tension."],"p":"Know your own wiring helps you separate what’s yours from patterns you learned growing up.","go":["/library/life.html#family-systems","Family systems (Library)"],"see":["triangulation","family-roles"],"ev":"A central idea in Bowen family systems theory. It’s widely used in family therapy; research support is mixed and mostly correlational.","ex":"“I understand you see it differently. I’m going to make a different choice, and I still love you.”","f":"family-systems"},{"id":"family-roles","t":"Family roles","a":["family roles","the peacemaker","the responsible one","the scapegoat","the joker"],"x":["In many families, people fall into roles: the responsible one, the peacemaker, the funny one, the one who gets blamed. Roles can help a family cope, but they can also stick long after they’re needed.","Noticing your old role (and whether you still play it at work or at home) can explain why certain situations feel so heavy."],"p":"The Library’s family scripts entry covers habits we carry from our families. Where your lens came from shows how each stage of growing up shapes roles and expectations, with the rulebook from home to fill in. Know your own wiring separates learned patterns from wiring.","go":["/library/life.html#family-scripts","Family scripts (Library)"],"see":["differentiation-of-self","family-scripts-in-daily-life"],"ev":"Popular descriptions of family roles come from family therapy and addiction-recovery writing. They’re useful as reflection prompts but aren’t scientific categories.","ex":"Ask yourself: “What role did I play growing up? Do I still play it with my own household?”","f":"family-systems"},{"id":"family-scripts-in-daily-life","t":"Family scripts in daily life","a":["how we did it growing up","family habits","the way my family did it"],"x":["Each of us grows up with unspoken rules about chores, money, holidays, arguments and affection. In a new household, these scripts collide, and each feels like the normal way.","Talking about your family scripts (without judging whose was better) makes it easier to write a new, shared one."],"p":"The Library’s family scripts entry goes deeper. WP-03 is a good place to write down the new, shared way.","go":["/library/life.html#family-scripts","Family scripts (Library)"],"see":["family-roles","false-consensus-at-home"],"ev":"A practical application of family scripts research (associated with John Byng-Hall). Couples commonly bring different family habits into a shared home.","ex":"“In my family, we always cleaned on Saturdays. What was it like in yours?”","f":"family-systems"},{"id":"emotional-cutoff","t":"Emotional cutoff","a":["cutting off family","emotional distance from family","no contact"],"x":["In family systems thinking, emotional cutoff means managing unresolved tension by reducing or ending contact. Sometimes distance is a healthy choice; sometimes it leaves old tension unresolved.","There’s no single right answer, and only you can weigh your situation. If family relationships feel very painful, a qualified professional can help you think it through."],"p":"The program doesn’t tell anyone whether to keep or leave a relationship. It helps with the everyday setup and how you talk, within whatever relationship you choose.","go":["/library/life.html#family-systems","Family systems (Library)"],"see":["differentiation-of-self","boundaries-with-parents-as-an-adult"],"ev":"A concept from Bowen family systems theory describing managing tension through distance. Decisions about family contact are deeply personal and depend on circumstances.","ex":"If you’re considering less contact, it may help to talk it through with someone you trust or a qualified professional first.","f":"family-systems"},{"id":"sibling-rivalry-in-adulthood","t":"Sibling rivalry in adulthood","a":["adult siblings","sibling rivalry","siblings as adults"],"x":["Old sibling tensions (who was favored, who did more) can resurface in adulthood, especially around holidays, parents’ needs or big decisions.","Focusing on a shared goal (like a parent’s care), dividing jobs clearly, and avoiding old comparisons can keep things calmer."],"p":"WP-03 works well for siblings sharing responsibilities, like care for an aging parent.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#family","Where families start"],"see":["family-roles","sibling-care-meetings"],"ev":"Research suggests sibling relationships often become warmer in adulthood but can reignite around family events, inheritance or caring for parents.","ex":"“I know we see things differently. Can we focus on what Mum needs right now, and leave the old stuff aside?”","f":"family-systems"},{"id":"birth-order-myths","t":"Birth order myths","a":["birth order","oldest child","middle child","youngest child"],"x":["Many people believe firstborns are responsible, middle children feel overlooked and youngest children are carefree. It’s a fun idea, but large studies find birth order has little effect on personality traits.","Family roles and circumstances probably matter more than birth order itself."],"p":"The Library’s Big Five entry covers what research does say about personality.","go":["/library/wiring.html#big-five","The Big Five (Library)"],"see":["family-roles","temperament-and-character"],"ev":"Popular ideas about birth order and personality aren’t well supported. Large studies (for example, Rohrer, Egloff and Schmukle, 2015) found little or no effect of birth order on personality.","ex":"Rather than “you’re such a typical oldest child”, ask what roles each sibling ended up with.","f":"family-systems"},{"id":"adult-children-at-home","t":"Adult children at home","a":["adult child living at home","boomerang kids","grown kids moving back"],"x":["When an adult child lives at home, old parent-child habits can clash with adult expectations. Unspoken assumptions about rent, chores, guests and schedules cause most of the friction.","Treating it more like a shared household (clear agreements, named jobs, regular check-ins) helps everyone feel respected."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand and WP-03 work for any household of two to eight, including adult family members.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#family","Where families start"],"see":["chore-charts-that-work","money-and-fairness-splits"],"ev":"Adult children living with parents is common in many countries and cultures. Clear expectations are widely recommended by family educators.","ex":"A short written agreement: rent or contribution (if any), jobs owned, guests, and a check-in date.","f":"family-systems"},{"id":"grandparents-and-childcare","t":"Grandparents and childcare","a":["grandparents helping with kids","grandparent childcare","in-laws and kids"],"x":["Grandparents often provide wonderful help with children, but differences in rules and parenting styles can cause friction. Gratitude plus a few clear, agreed basics usually works better than correcting in the moment.","Keeping the agreements short and focused on what matters most avoids feeling controlling."],"p":"The Library’s in-laws entry covers extended-family tensions.","go":["/library/life.html#in-laws","In-laws (Library)"],"see":["boundaries-with-parents-as-an-adult","family-meetings"],"ev":"Grandparent childcare is common and often valued. Tensions around rules and expectations are widely reported; research on specific solutions is limited.","ex":"“We’re so grateful for your help. Could we agree on a few house rules, like screens and snacks, so the kids get the same message?”","f":"family-systems"},{"id":"family-group-chats","t":"Family group chats","a":["family group chat","family WhatsApp","group chat drama"],"x":["Family group chats are great for photos and logistics, but tricky for anything sensitive: many readers, little tone, and a permanent record.","A simple rule helps: logistics and good news in the group; disagreements and feelings in private, preferably by voice."],"p":"The Conversation Reader can help you see where a thread turned tense, and suggest a calmer reply.","go":["/conversation-reader.html","The Conversation Reader"],"see":["texting-norms-that-prevent-fights","tone-indicators"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Group messaging makes misunderstandings more likely because tone is hard to read and many people see each message.","ex":"For anything sensitive, move from the group chat to a private call.","f":"family-systems"},{"id":"developmental-stages","t":"Developmental stages","a":["stages of development","developmental stages","erikson","erikson stages","psychosocial stages","stages of life","life stages"],"x":["Each stage of growing up is busy with its own big question. First years: is the world safe, will someone come? Toddler years: can I do things myself? Early childhood: is it okay to take up space? School years: am I capable, how do I measure up? Teen years: who am I, where do I belong? Young adulthood: can I be close without losing myself?","The answers we land on become understandings we carry forward and keep using as adults, often without noticing. We revisit them at big changes, like a new relationship, a move or becoming a parent."],"p":"Where your lens came from walks through each stage and what adults often carry forward from it.","go":["/growing-up-in-depth.html#stages","The stages, with a table"],"see":["why-timing-matters","sensitive-periods","core-beliefs"],"ev":"Erik Erikson described eight psychosocial stages across the whole life in the 1950s. The ages and details are debated and reflect his culture, but the idea that development continues all through life is widely accepted.","ex":"Ask yourself: which big question was I busy answering when this rule started? Is the world safe, am I capable, where do I belong, or can I be close without losing myself?","f":"growing-up"},{"id":"why-timing-matters","t":"Why timing matters","a":["why childhood matters","does age matter","certain ages","when it happened","why some things affect me more","impact of childhood","childhood experiences","age it happened"],"x":["An experience that touches the question you were answering at the time tends to become part of the answer, and to go deeper than the same event at another age. A move at 4 is mostly about routines; a move at 14, right in the middle of “where do I belong?”, can leave a longer feeling of being the outsider.","Other things that make an experience land harder: it happened again and again, you decided it meant something about you, nobody helped you make sense of it, or you were a sensitive child. Deeper isn’t permanent. People keep learning all their lives."],"p":"The full guide covers what makes an experience land harder and how change happens later.","go":["/growing-up-in-depth.html#timing","Why timing matters"],"see":["developmental-stages","sensitive-periods"],"ev":"Research on sensitive periods finds the brain is especially ready to learn from certain experiences at certain ages, most clearly in early childhood and again in the teen years. Population findings never predict what will happen to any one person.","ex":"“Part of this feeling is older than today. I was about the age where belonging mattered most.”","f":"growing-up"},{"id":"sensitive-periods","t":"Sensitive periods","a":["sensitive period","critical period","brain development","early brain development","teen brain","adolescent brain","serve and return"],"x":["A sensitive period is a window when the growing brain is especially ready to learn certain things from experience. The clearest evidence is for early childhood, when the brain’s basic architecture is built through everyday back-and-forth with caring adults. A second strong wave comes in the teen years, especially for the social brain, which is why peer approval and exclusion leave such strong marks.","Sensitive doesn’t mean fixed. Later relationships, reflection and practice genuinely reshape early understandings."],"p":"Where your lens came from explains sensitive periods without jargon.","go":["/growing-up-in-depth.html#research","The research, in plain words"],"see":["why-timing-matters","developmental-stages"],"ev":"The Harvard Center on the Developing Child summarizes research on early brain development and responsive “serve and return” care. Sarah-Jayne Blakemore and colleagues describe adolescence as a period of heightened sensitivity to peers and belonging.","ex":"If you’re raising children, you don’t need to be perfect. Noticing a rough moment and making it right teaches a lot.","f":"growing-up"},{"id":"core-beliefs","t":"Core beliefs","a":["core belief","schemas","schema","beliefs about myself","negative beliefs about myself","why do i think im not good enough","i feel like a burden","i feel too much","im only okay when im useful"],"x":["Many of the harshest things adults believe about themselves were concluded by a child with half the facts. A child can’t know the grown-ups were tired or worried; they only know how it felt. Common ones: “my needs are a burden,” “I’m too much,” “mistakes mean I’m bad,” “I have to be the strong one.”","As an adult you’re allowed to look again with the full picture, and to choose a fairer reading."],"p":"The simple version has a table of old understandings and fairer readings.","go":["/growing-up-in-depth.html#self","When the lens is wrong about you"],"see":["developmental-stages","unspoken-expectations"],"ev":"Aaron Beck’s cognitive therapy and Jeffrey Young’s schema work describe deep, early beliefs about self and others that shape how we read events. This site uses the idea in everyday terms, not as therapy.","ex":"Write the old belief, where you think you learned it, and one fairer adult sentence. “I’m only okay when I’m useful” becomes “Helping is something I do, not the price of being loved.”","f":"growing-up"},{"id":"unspoken-expectations","t":"Unspoken expectations","a":["unspoken expectations","unwritten rules","should just know","they should know what i need","should know without me saying","expectations of my partner","if you loved me you would know","mind reading","expecting them to know","family rules","rulebook","family rulebook","house rules from childhood"],"x":["We all grew up with an unwritten rulebook: how love looks, what happens when someone’s upset, who does which jobs, when rest is allowed. Carried into adult life, those rules feel like facts about how things should be, not preferences we brought from somewhere. “If you loved me, you’d just know.” “A good partner plans the birthdays.” “Rest is earned.”","An expectation that’s never said out loud can’t be agreed to, only broken. Many resentments are two people following two different rulebooks, each sure theirs is normal. Swapping rulebooks on a calm day, then choosing your own rules on purpose, helps."],"p":"Write the six rulebook lines in Where your lens came from, then give jobs one owner with WP-03 or the Lemonade Stand.","go":["/growing-up-in-depth.html#expect","The expectations we place on others"],"see":["core-beliefs","family-roles","gender-scripts","family-scripts-in-daily-life"],"ev":"Family systems writers such as Virginia Satir described unspoken family rules. Research on couples finds unmet expectations are a common source of resentment; this entry uses the idea in everyday terms.","ex":"“I think this is my old family rulebook talking. Where I grew up, whoever noticed the mess cleaned it. Can we work out our own rule?”","f":"growing-up"},{"id":"gender-scripts","t":"Gender scripts","a":["gender roles","gender expectations","boys dont cry","women do the housework","men fix things","who does the chores","learned chores from parents"],"x":["Children absorb messages about what boys, girls and everyone else are supposed to be good at, feel and do: “boys don’t cry,” “girls keep the peace,” “men fix things, women remember birthdays.”","These scripts are one of the big quiet reasons invisible work at home still tends to fall unevenly, even between people who believe in sharing it."],"p":"The Preface covers the work nobody sees. WP-01 records who actually did what in a week.","go":["/growing-up-in-depth.html#world","How the world shapes the lens"],"see":["unspoken-expectations","family-roles"],"ev":"Studies following families over time have found that how parents split housework is linked with how their grown children later split it. Researchers widely describe gender-role messages children absorb from family, school and media.","ex":"List three jobs you each assumed “someone” would do. Beside each, write who did it in the home you grew up in. Then choose an owner on purpose.","f":"growing-up"},{"id":"comparing-ourselves-with-others","t":"Comparing ourselves with others","a":["social comparison","comparing myself","everyone else has it together","social media comparison","instagram","compare my relationship"],"x":["We work out how we’re doing partly by comparing with others. Today many people, especially teens, compare their everyday life with everyone else’s highlights.","That can make ordinary, good-enough partners, families and homes look like they’re failing, when they’re simply real."],"p":"Where your lens came from explains how screens and media shape what “normal” looks like.","go":["/growing-up-in-depth.html#world","How the world shapes the lens"],"see":["core-beliefs","why-timing-matters"],"ev":"Leon Festinger’s social comparison theory (1954) describes how we judge ourselves against others. Research links heavy comparison on social media with lower mood for some people, especially teens; findings vary.","ex":"When you catch a comparison, add the missing half: “I’m comparing my ordinary Tuesday with their best, edited moment.”","f":"growing-up"},{"id":"habit-stacking","t":"Habit stacking","a":["habit stacking","stacking habits","anchor habits","after I do X"],"x":["Habit stacking means attaching a new habit to one you already do every day. The existing habit becomes the cue: “After I brush my teeth, I’ll…”, “When I sit down for dinner, we’ll…”.","It works because it removes the need to remember. The old habit reminds you. Choose an anchor that happens reliably at the right time and place."],"p":"The 90-second daily check-in (WP-13) works best stacked onto something regular, like after dinner.","go":["/library/motivation.html#habits-and-cues","Habits and cues (Library)"],"see":["tiny-habits","environment-design-at-home"],"ev":"The idea builds on research on implementation intentions and cues. The phrase “habit stacking” was popularized by writers including James Clear (Atomic Habits, 2018). Evidence for the specific technique is mostly indirect.","ex":"“After I pour my morning coffee, I’ll check the family calendar for today.”","f":"habits"},{"id":"tiny-habits","t":"Tiny habits","a":["tiny habits","start small","make it tiny","small habits"],"x":["A tiny habit is a version of a behavior so small it feels almost silly: one push-up, one sentence in a journal, one dish. Tiny habits are easy to start on low-energy days, and they can grow naturally.","The point isn’t the tiny action itself. It’s building the routine. Once it’s automatic, making it bigger is much easier."],"p":"This is Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person: make the setup easy instead of relying on willpower.","go":["/library/motivation.html#habit-formation","Habit formation (Library)"],"see":["habit-stacking","the-two-minute-start"],"ev":"BJ Fogg describes the approach in Tiny Habits (2019): start with a very small behavior, anchor it, and celebrate. Research generally supports starting small, though Fogg’s specific method has limited formal testing.","ex":"Instead of “clean the kitchen every night”, start with “wipe one counter after dinner”.","f":"habits"},{"id":"the-two-minute-start","t":"The two-minute start","a":["two minute rule","just two minutes","start for two minutes"],"x":["Starting is often the hardest part. Committing to just two minutes lowers the barrier, and once you’ve started, continuing is often easier than you expected.","Separately, a common rule says that if a task takes less than two minutes, doing it now saves the effort of remembering it later."],"p":"Situations like “I can’t get myself to start the dishes” often ease with a tiny, time-boxed start.","go":["/library/motivation.html#procrastination","Procrastination (Library)"],"see":["tiny-habits","procrastination-and-the-first-step"],"ev":"A popular productivity guideline. David Allen’s “two-minute rule” (Getting Things Done, 2001) says to do tasks taking under two minutes right away; James Clear suggests scaling a habit down to two minutes. Both are practical tips rather than research findings.","ex":"“I’ll just do two minutes of the laundry pile. I can stop after that.”","f":"habits"},{"id":"procrastination-and-the-first-step","t":"Procrastination and the first step","a":["making the first step obvious","first step","next action"],"x":["Big, vague tasks invite avoidance. A clear, concrete first step (“open the email”, “put the gloves by the sink”) is much easier to start.","Writing the very next physical action often unsticks tasks that have been sitting for weeks."],"p":"The Library covers procrastination in depth, including why it’s rarely about laziness.","go":["/library/motivation.html#procrastination","Procrastination (Library)"],"see":["the-two-minute-start","tiny-habits"],"ev":"Research on procrastination links it to how unpleasant or unclear a task feels. Breaking tasks into concrete next steps is a widely used strategy.","ex":"Not “sort the taxes” but “find last year’s folder and put it on the table”.","f":"habits"},{"id":"environment-design-at-home","t":"Environment design at home","a":["environment design","make it easy","friction","choice architecture at home"],"x":["Our surroundings nudge us all day. If the laundry basket is in another room, clothes pile on the chair. If the bin is full and awkward, the rubbish waits.","Making the helpful thing easy (and the unhelpful thing a little harder) often works better than reminders. It also takes blame out of it: the setup is doing the work."],"p":"This is Pillar II and Pillar V together: fix the setup, and notice the quiet pulls it creates.","go":["/library/motivation.html#habits-and-cues","Habits and cues (Library)"],"see":["habit-stacking","defaults-at-home"],"ev":"Research on habits and on choice architecture suggests small changes in the environment (what’s easy, visible or in the way) can shift behavior more reliably than intentions alone.","ex":"Put a laundry basket where clothes actually land, and a hook where keys actually get dropped.","f":"habits"},{"id":"defaults-at-home","t":"Defaults at home","a":["defaults","default choices","who does it by default"],"x":["A default is whatever happens when nobody makes an active choice. At home, defaults are powerful: the person who noticed first last time often becomes the person who does it forever.","Changing a default on purpose (“bins are Jordan’s now, noticing included”) is often more effective than asking for help each time."],"p":"Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives, is all about this. WP-03 turns defaults into named owners.","go":["/five-pillars.html#quiet-incentives","Pillar V: Notice the quiet incentives"],"see":["environment-design-at-home","kinkeeping"],"ev":"The default effect (people tend to stick with whatever happens if they do nothing) is well documented in behavioral economics, for example in organ donation and retirement savings research.","ex":"Ask: “What happens if nobody decides?” That’s your default, and it’s often one person doing it.","f":"habits"},{"id":"streaks-and-tracking","t":"Streaks and tracking","a":["habit tracker","streaks","tracking habits","don’t break the chain"],"x":["Tracking a habit (a tick on a calendar, a note in your phone) makes progress visible, which can be motivating. Many people like seeing a streak grow.","The danger is all-or-nothing thinking: one missed day and the whole thing feels ruined. Treat the tracker as information, not a scorecard."],"p":"REPORT-01 keeps your progress week by week, so it builds instead of starting over.","go":["/workpapers/report-01.html","REPORT-01: Your progress, week by week"],"see":["never-miss-twice","tiny-habits"],"ev":"Self-monitoring is linked with behavior change in many studies. Streaks can motivate, but some research suggests breaking a streak can be discouraging.","ex":"A simple calendar on the fridge with a tick for each day you did the daily check-in.","f":"habits"},{"id":"never-miss-twice","t":"Never miss twice","a":["never miss twice","getting back on track","missed a day"],"x":["Missing one day is normal and doesn’t undo a habit. What matters is getting back to it quickly, ideally the next day, so the gap doesn’t become the new routine.","This takes the pressure off being perfect, which helps habits last."],"p":"The daily check-in is designed to be small enough to pick straight back up.","go":["/workpapers/wp-13-daily-check-in.html","WP-13: The 90-second daily check-in"],"see":["streaks-and-tracking","restarting-after-a-break"],"ev":"A popular guideline from habit writers. Research on habit formation (for example, Lally and colleagues, 2010) found that missing a single day didn’t significantly harm habit building.","ex":"Missed the check-in yesterday? No problem. Just do it tonight.","f":"habits"},{"id":"restarting-after-a-break","t":"Restarting after a break","a":["starting again","fell off the wagon","relapse is normal"],"x":["Most people who build a habit fall off at some point: holidays, illness, a busy season. Restarting is part of the process, not a sign it didn’t work.","When you restart, start smaller than before, and don’t try to make up for lost time."],"p":"PROG-01 can be picked up again at any week. The Library covers stages of change.","go":["/prog-01.html","PROG-01: Six gentle weeks"],"see":["never-miss-twice","fresh-starts"],"ev":"Research on behavior change (for example, the stages-of-change model) treats lapses as a normal part of the process rather than failure.","ex":"“We stopped the daily check-ins over the holidays. Let’s just start again tonight.”","f":"habits"},{"id":"fresh-starts","t":"Fresh starts","a":["fresh start","new beginnings","start of the month"],"x":["Landmarks like a Monday, the first of the month or a birthday can make it feel easier to begin something new, as if you’re turning a page.","You can use this on purpose: pick a meaningful start date for a new household routine, and mark it."],"p":"The Library’s fresh start entry goes deeper. The monthly look-back (WP-04) fits naturally at the start of each month.","go":["/library/motivation.html#fresh-start-effect","The fresh start effect (Library)"],"see":["restarting-after-a-break","habit-stacking"],"ev":"Research by Hengchen Dai, Katherine Milkman and Jason Riis (2014) found people are more likely to start goals after temporal landmarks like new weeks, months or birthdays.","ex":"“The first of the month is Sunday. Want to start the monthly look-back then?”","f":"habits"},{"id":"keystone-habits","t":"Keystone habits","a":["keystone habit","one habit that changes others"],"x":["A keystone habit is one that seems to make other good changes easier. Regular family dinners, a morning planning moment or a daily check-in can play this role.","It isn’t guaranteed. It’s a useful question, though: which one small routine would make the biggest difference for us?"],"p":"The 90-second daily check-in often acts as a keystone for households using the program.","go":["/workpapers/wp-13-daily-check-in.html","WP-13: The 90-second daily check-in"],"see":["habit-stacking","tiny-habits"],"ev":"Popularized by Charles Duhigg in The Power of Habit (2012). The idea is intuitive, but evidence that specific habits reliably trigger wider change is limited.","ex":"For some couples, a daily check-in becomes a keystone: it catches small problems early and makes other routines easier.","f":"habits"},{"id":"celebrating-small-steps","t":"Celebrating small steps","a":["celebrate","celebrating progress","reward yourself"],"x":["A small moment of celebration right after doing a new habit (a smile, a “yes!”, a tick on the chart) helps connect the action with a good feeling.","For households and teams, celebrating together (“we did the check-in every day this week!”) builds shared pride."],"p":"The Library’s small wins entry explains why progress you notice keeps you going.","go":["/library/motivation.html#small-wins","Small wins (Library)"],"see":["tiny-habits","small-wins-at-home"],"ev":"BJ Fogg recommends an immediate small celebration to help habits stick. Research on small wins (Teresa Amabile and Steven Kramer) links noticing progress with motivation.","ex":"After finishing the laundry: a fist pump, a “nice!”, or a cup of tea.","f":"habits"},{"id":"small-wins-at-home","t":"Small wins at home","a":["small wins","quick wins","tiny progress"],"x":["When a household is stuck on big problems, starting with a small, fixable one builds momentum and trust. Each small win shows that change is possible.","It also makes the harder conversations easier, because you’ve proved to each other you can agree."],"p":"WP-04 sorts repeat problems; start with the ones that just need a clear owner.","go":["/library/motivation.html#small-wins","Small wins (Library)"],"see":["celebrating-small-steps","helplessness"],"ev":"Research on small wins suggests visible progress, even minor, supports motivation and mood at work. Applying it at home is a reasonable extension.","ex":"Pick the easiest fix from your monthly look-back first, not the biggest.","f":"habits"},{"id":"routines-versus-habits","t":"Routines versus habits","a":["routines","habits and routines","daily routines"],"x":["A routine is a regular sequence you choose to follow; a habit is something that has become automatic. Routines are often how habits start.","For people who find switching tasks hard, predictable routines can reduce the mental effort of each day. For people who love variety, a light routine with room for change often works better."],"p":"Frequency Calibration helps two people see where their natural rhythms differ, which affects which routines feel good.","go":["/library/motivation.html#habit-formation","Habit formation (Library)"],"see":["habit-stacking","environment-design-at-home"],"ev":"Researchers generally define habits as automatic responses to cues, while routines are sequences we still choose. The two overlap in everyday life.","ex":"A morning routine (make bed, breakfast, check calendar) can slowly turn into a habit that runs on autopilot.","f":"habits"},{"id":"shared-habits-for-households","t":"Shared habits for households","a":["household habits","family routines","couple habits"],"x":["Some habits work better done together: a quick tidy after dinner, a weekly plan, a daily check-in. Doing them together makes them lighter and turns chores into connection.","Start with one, keep it short, and attach it to something that already happens."],"p":"The daily check-in (WP-13) and weekly closing are the program’s core shared habits.","go":["/workpapers/wp-13-daily-check-in.html","WP-13: The 90-second daily check-in"],"see":["habit-stacking","weekly-planning-together"],"ev":"Research on family routines links regular shared routines with family wellbeing. Specific routines are a matter of fit.","ex":"One shared habit to start: a five-minute tidy together before bed, with music.","f":"habits"},{"id":"chore-charts-that-work","t":"Chore charts that work","a":["chore chart","chore rota","chore schedule","cleaning schedule"],"x":["The chore charts that last tend to share a few features: every job has a named owner, the list is visible, “done” is clearly defined, and the chart is reviewed occasionally rather than rewritten weekly.","Charts fail when they list jobs without owners (“someone should clean the bathroom”), when they’re too ambitious, or when one person ends up policing everyone else."],"p":"WP-03 is a chore chart with two names per job: who does it and who keeps an eye on it. Review it at the weekly closing.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["rotation-or-specialization","negotiating-household-jobs-by-preference"],"ev":"Practical advice drawn from household and parenting guidance. There’s little formal research on specific chart designs; clear ownership and visibility are the common threads.","ex":"One sheet on the fridge: each job, how often, who owns it, and what “done” looks like.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"rotation-or-specialization","t":"Rotation or specialization","a":["rotate chores","take turns","specialize","fixed jobs"],"x":["Some households give each job a permanent owner (specialization). Others rotate jobs each week. Fixed owners reduce reminders and let each person build a routine. Rotation shares the unpleasant jobs and means everyone knows how to do everything.","Many households mix the two: fixed owners for most things, rotation for the jobs nobody wants."],"p":"WP-03 works with either. If you rotate, write down whose week it is, so the rotation itself has an owner.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["chore-charts-that-work","negotiating-household-jobs-by-preference"],"ev":"Both approaches are common. Specialization lets people get good at a job and avoids re-deciding; rotation spreads dreaded jobs. No single approach is best for every household.","ex":"Fixed owners for most jobs, and a rotation just for the two jobs everyone dislikes.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"planning-doing-and-following-through","t":"Planning, doing and following through","a":["conception planning execution","plan do follow through","whole job ownership","CPE"],"x":["Many jobs have hidden parts: noticing it needs doing, planning it, and then doing it. When one person does the noticing and planning and hands out the doing, the load looks shared but isn’t.","Handing over a whole job, from noticing to finishing, is often what actually lightens the load."],"p":"WP-03 asks for a Responsible and an Accountable name; for jobs like this, make sure the owner owns the noticing too.","go":["/library/fairness.html#mental-load","The mental load (Library)"],"see":["the-fair-play-card-idea","kinkeeping"],"ev":"Eve Rodsky’s book Fair Play (2019) describes owning a task’s “conception, planning and execution”. It’s a practical framework rather than a research finding, but it matches research on the mental load.","ex":"Owning “school lunches” means noticing supplies are low, planning the week, shopping, and packing, not just packing when asked.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"the-fair-play-card-idea","t":"The Fair Play card idea","a":["Fair Play","Fair Play cards","Eve Rodsky"],"x":["Fair Play turns household jobs into cards that partners “deal” between them, with each card including the full job from noticing to finishing. The idea is to make invisible work visible and to hand over whole jobs.","You don’t need the product to use the idea. A written list of jobs with full owners does much the same thing."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand and WP-03 are the program’s way of doing this: see all the jobs, then give each one a whole owner.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["planning-doing-and-following-through","chore-charts-that-work"],"ev":"Fair Play is a popular book and card system by Eve Rodsky (2019). It’s practical guidance, not a research study. Many households find the ideas useful; others find the system too detailed.","ex":"Write each household job on a card, deal them out based on preference and capacity, and revisit the deal every few months.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"kinkeeping","t":"Kinkeeping","a":["kinkeeping","kin keeping","keeping in touch with family","birthday cards"],"x":["Kinkeeping is the work of keeping family connections going: remembering birthdays, sending cards, organizing visits, calling relatives, planning holidays. It takes real time and thought but rarely gets counted as work.","Naming it as a job, and deciding who owns it, can relieve one person of an unseen burden."],"p":"WP-01 logs invisible jobs like this. The Library’s invisible work entry goes deeper.","go":["/library/fairness.html#invisible-work","Invisible work (Library)"],"see":["planning-doing-and-following-through","holiday-load"],"ev":"Anthropologist Micaela di Leonardo described “kinkeeping” in 1987: the work of maintaining family ties, often done by women. It’s a well-recognized form of invisible work.","ex":"Add “birthdays and family cards” as its own job in WP-03, with a named owner.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"holiday-load","t":"Holiday load","a":["holiday stress","holiday planning load","Christmas load"],"x":["Holidays bring a burst of extra work: gifts, cards, food, travel, hosting, family coordination. Much of it is planning and remembering, and it often falls to one person by default.","Listing holiday jobs early and assigning owners can turn a stressful season into a shared project."],"p":"A holiday list works just like WP-03. The Library’s holidays entry covers family tensions too.","go":["/library/life.html#holidays","Holidays (Library)"],"see":["kinkeeping","planning-doing-and-following-through"],"ev":"Research on holiday stress and household labor suggests holiday preparation often falls unevenly. Specific figures vary by study.","ex":"Six weeks before: list every holiday job (gifts, cards, food, travel), give each one an owner, and put it in the calendar.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"school-and-kid-admin","t":"School and kid admin","a":["school admin","permission slips","kid logistics","school emails"],"x":["Children bring a steady stream of admin: forms, emails, appointments, activities, clothes that don’t fit, birthday party invitations. It’s easy for one parent to become the default for all of it.","Dividing it into clear categories with owners, and sharing one family calendar, keeps it from falling on one person."],"p":"WP-03 can include “school emails and forms” as a named job. For two homes, see the co-parenting notes.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["planning-doing-and-following-through","co-parenting-calendar"],"ev":"Research on the mental load of parenting highlights children’s admin (forms, appointments, activities) as a large, often invisible category.","ex":"Split by child or by category: one person owns school emails and forms, the other owns activities and appointments.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"pet-care-load","t":"Pet care load","a":["pet care","dog walking","who feeds the cat"],"x":["Pets add daily and occasional jobs that are easy to forget to count: walks, feeding, cleaning up, vet visits, buying food, arranging care when you travel.","Putting pet jobs on the same list as other chores keeps them from quietly landing on the most attached (or most guilty) person."],"p":"Add pet jobs to the Lemonade Stand and give each one an owner in WP-03.","go":["/lemonade-stand.html","The Lemonade Stand"],"see":["chore-charts-that-work","rotation-or-specialization"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Pet care is often left off household lists even though it can take significant daily time.","ex":"List every pet job (feeding, walks, vet, litter, grooming, meds) with an owner, including a backup for travel.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"lowering-standards-on-purpose","t":"Lowering standards on purpose","a":["good enough","lower the bar","good enough standards"],"x":["Sometimes a household argument is about standards: one person wants things done a particular way. Deciding together which jobs need a high standard and which can be “good enough” frees everyone up.","It also makes it easier for others to take over jobs without being corrected."],"p":"WP-03 can note what “done” looks like for each job. The Library covers perfectionism.","go":["/library/motivation.html#perfectionism","Perfectionism (Library)"],"see":["gatekeeping-around-chores","different-standards-of-clean"],"ev":"Research on perfectionism suggests very high standards can increase stress. Choosing “good enough” for some tasks is common advice, not a tested intervention.","ex":"“Towels folded any way is fine. They’re clean, that’s the job.”","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"gatekeeping-around-chores","t":"Gatekeeping around chores","a":["redoing their work","my way or else","correcting how they do it"],"x":["When one person redoes or criticizes how someone else does a job, the other person often stops trying. Then the first person ends up doing it all, and resents it.","Agreeing on what “done” means, then letting the owner do it their way, breaks this cycle."],"p":"The Library’s gatekeeping entry goes deeper. WP-03 owners own the how, within the agreed standard.","go":["/library/fairness.html#gatekeeping","Gatekeeping (Library)"],"see":["lowering-standards-on-purpose","ikea-effect"],"ev":"Research on “maternal gatekeeping” and household labor suggests criticism of how others do tasks can discourage their involvement. It applies to anyone who guards a job.","ex":"If it’s their job, let it be done their way, as long as it meets the agreed “done”.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"different-standards-of-clean","t":"Different standards of clean","a":["clean enough","tidy standards","mess tolerance"],"x":["People really do notice mess at different levels. The person who notices first often cleans first, and eventually cleans always, while the other person may truly not see the problem.","Agreeing a shared baseline for common spaces (and letting private spaces be private) usually works better than arguing about who is right."],"p":"Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives: whoever notices first drifts into the job. WP-03 gives it a named owner.","go":["/library/fairness.html#anticipation-and-noticing","Noticing and anticipating (Library)"],"see":["lowering-standards-on-purpose","noticing-thresholds"],"ev":"Research suggests people genuinely differ in how much mess they notice and tolerate. It’s not simply a matter of effort.","ex":"Agree three “baseline” rules for shared spaces: sink empty by bedtime, floor clear, bathroom wiped weekly.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"noticing-thresholds","t":"Noticing thresholds","a":["noticing threshold","who notices first","mess blindness"],"x":["A noticing threshold is how much needs doing before you notice it. If yours is lower, you’ll spot the full bin sooner, and do it sooner, again and again.","That’s not a personality flaw in either person. It’s a setup that quietly assigns jobs. Making the noticing part of someone else’s job is the fix."],"p":"This is a textbook Pillar V pattern. WP-03’s Accountable name is the person who notices.","go":["/library/fairness.html#anticipation-and-noticing","Noticing and anticipating (Library)"],"see":["different-standards-of-clean","defaults-at-home"],"ev":"Related research on attention and household labor suggests the person who notices tasks first often ends up doing them. The concept is well discussed, if not precisely measured.","ex":"Ask: “Which jobs do I do only because I notice them first?” Those are the ones to hand over as whole jobs.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"thanking-for-invisible-work","t":"Thanking for invisible work","a":["thank you for invisible work","appreciating mental load","noticing effort"],"x":["Invisible work rarely gets thanked because nobody sees it. A specific thank-you for planning, remembering or smoothing things over shows you noticed, and noticing is half of what people want.","Thanks doesn’t replace sharing the load, but it keeps things steady while you sort out the split."],"p":"Turning Toward’s daily idea is often a specific thank-you. The 90-second check-in includes one thing you appreciated.","go":["/turning-toward.html","Turning Toward"],"see":["kinkeeping","specific-thanks"],"ev":"Research links appreciation with relationship satisfaction and with feeling that work is fair. Thanks for specific, unseen work may be especially meaningful.","ex":"“Thanks for remembering Mum’s birthday. I know that takes planning.”","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"outsourcing-household-jobs","t":"Outsourcing household jobs","a":["outsourcing chores","paying for help","delegating housework"],"x":["Some households pay for help with certain jobs (cleaning, laundry, meal kits, yard work) or trade with friends and neighbors. It can reduce friction when time is the scarcest resource.","It’s a budget decision and a values decision, and not everyone can or wants to. Even without money, a job can be simplified or dropped."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand shows which jobs take the most time; that’s where outsourcing or simplifying would help most.","go":["/lemonade-stand.html","The Lemonade Stand"],"see":["chore-charts-that-work","money-and-fairness-splits"],"ev":"Research (for example, Whillans and colleagues, 2017) found spending money to buy time was linked with greater life satisfaction. Whether it’s possible depends on budget, and the finding isn’t universal.","ex":"If budget allows, pick the most dreaded job and see if paying for it (or trading with a neighbor) frees up real time.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"money-and-fairness-splits","t":"Money and fairness splits","a":["splitting costs fairly","50 50 or by income","proportional split"],"x":["Shared costs can be split equally, in proportion to income, or by use (for example, bigger room pays more). Each is fair by a different standard.","Agreeing on the rule before looking at the numbers avoids arguments that are really about which standard to use."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand’s money side shows what each person actually paid. Chapter IV is about agreeing what “fair” means.","go":["/lemonade-stand.html","The Lemonade Stand"],"see":["yours-mine-and-ours-accounts","using-fair-standards"],"ev":"Different couples and housemates use different fairness rules (equal, by income, by use). No one method is right; what matters most in research on fairness is that people agree the rule is fair.","ex":"Try both: calculate a 50/50 split and an income-based split, and talk about which feels fair to both.","f":"household-fairness"},{"id":"what-a-mediator-does","t":"What a mediator does","a":["mediator role","what is a mediator","mediation explained"],"x":["A mediator is a neutral third person who helps people in a disagreement talk to each other and find their own solution. They don’t decide who’s right, and they don’t impose an answer.","Mediators mostly manage the process: ground rules, turn-taking, summarizing each side, spotting shared interests, and helping people move from positions (“I want X”) to what they actually need. Formal mediators are trained professionals; informally, a trusted friend or colleague sometimes plays a similar role."],"p":"The Check-ins guide borrows mediator moves you can use yourselves: one topic, same side, hear it back before you answer.","go":["/library/conflict.html#mediation-basics","Mediation basics (Library)"],"see":["informal-mediation-between-friends-or-family","reframing-in-mediation","when-to-bring-in-a-third-party"],"ev":"Mediation is well established in family, workplace and community settings. Studies generally find good satisfaction rates, though results depend on the case and the mediator.","ex":"A mediator might say: “Let me check I have both views. Sam, you need the rent on the 1st. Alex, your pay comes on the 5th. So the question is timing, not whether it gets paid. Is that fair?”","f":"mediation"},{"id":"ground-rules-for-a-tough-talk","t":"Ground rules for a tough talk","a":["ground rules","conversation rules","rules for fighting fair"],"x":["Ground rules are a few simple agreements made before a hard talk: how you’ll take turns, what words to avoid, how to pause. Agreeing them in a calm moment gives you both something neutral to point to when things heat up.","Keep the list short (three to five rules) and make them about behavior, not attitude. “No raised voices” is easier to keep than “be respectful”."],"p":"The program’s ground rules are on the Check-ins page, and the Signal Translator’s talking notes start with a similar opener.","go":["/check-ins.html#ground","Check-in ground rules"],"see":["what-a-mediator-does","fighting-fair"],"ev":"Mediators and facilitators routinely set ground rules; research specifically on their effect is limited, but they give people a shared way to call a pause without blame.","ex":"“No interrupting, no ‘always’ or ‘never’, one topic, and either of us can call a twenty-minute break.”","f":"mediation"},{"id":"reframing-in-mediation","t":"Reframing in mediation","a":["reframing","neutral reframe","restating neutrally"],"x":["Reframing takes a charged statement and restates it in neutral terms that keep the need but drop the attack. “She’s so controlling about money” becomes “So how you make money decisions together really matters to you.”","You can reframe your own sentences before you say them, and gently reframe what you hear. It helps the conversation stay on the problem rather than on each other."],"p":"WP-09 is essentially a self-reframe: fact, feeling and ask instead of a verdict on the person.","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["what-a-mediator-does","paraphrasing"],"ev":"A core mediator skill described in mediation training. Evidence is mostly from practice, but it maps closely onto research on how blame language raises defensiveness.","ex":"“He never lifts a finger” reframed as: “So you’d like more of the daily jobs shared.”","f":"mediation"},{"id":"caucus","t":"Caucus","a":["caucus","private sessions","separate meetings in mediation"],"x":["A caucus is a private conversation between the mediator and one side, away from the other. People sometimes say things privately (fears, what they could actually accept) that they wouldn’t say in front of the other person.","At home, the informal version is simply talking to each person separately before bringing everyone together. It can lower the temperature, as long as nobody feels talked about behind their back."],"p":"The monthly look-back (WP-04) and house meetings go better when each person has had a chance to think on their own first.","go":["/library/conflict.html#mediation-basics","Mediation basics (Library)"],"see":["what-a-mediator-does","shuttle-conversations"],"ev":"A standard mediation technique. Practitioners report it helps people speak openly, though some mediators prefer to avoid it to keep things transparent.","ex":"In a family disagreement, a relative might chat with each sibling separately first: “What matters most to you here? What could you live with?”","f":"mediation"},{"id":"informal-mediation-between-friends-or-family","t":"Informal mediation between friends or family","a":["helping two people make up","being the go-between","friend mediator"],"x":["Sometimes a friend, sibling or colleague helps two people talk. It can be very helpful if the helper stays neutral, sets a simple structure, and hands the solution back to the two people.","The risk is becoming a messenger or a judge. Passing messages back and forth, or quietly agreeing with each side separately, usually makes things worse. The healthiest role is helping them talk directly."],"p":"The family systems idea of triangulation explains why the go-between role gets messy. With co-parents especially, never use children as messengers.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["what-a-mediator-does","triangulation"],"ev":"Informal third-party help is common and often useful; research also warns that go-betweens can get pulled into taking sides. Staying neutral is harder than it looks.","ex":"“I’m not going to take sides. Would you both be up for sitting down together, and I’ll just help keep it to one topic?”","f":"mediation"},{"id":"shuttle-conversations","t":"Shuttle conversations","a":["shuttle diplomacy","passing messages","back and forth through someone"],"x":["In formal settings, a mediator sometimes shuttles between people who can’t be in the same room. It can be a useful first step when tensions are very high.","In families, though, message-passing through a third person often turns into a game of telephone, and puts the messenger in an unfair spot, especially if the messenger is a child. Moving to direct conversation as soon as possible is usually better."],"p":"For co-parents, keep communication brief, factual and direct between the adults, and never through the kids.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#family","Where families start"],"see":["caucus","triangulation"],"ev":"Used in formal mediation when people can’t yet sit together. At home, relying on messengers tends to distort messages and keep conflict going.","ex":"Instead of “Tell your dad…”, try messaging him directly: “Can we sort out the holiday plan, just us?”","f":"mediation"},{"id":"when-to-bring-in-a-third-party","t":"When to bring in a third party","a":["getting outside help","bring in help","outside perspective"],"x":["If the same disagreement keeps going nowhere, if talking always escalates, or if decisions have big consequences (money, separation arrangements, care for a family member), a neutral third party can help. That might be a trusted friend, a workplace mediator, a professional mediator or a counselor.","Asking for outside help isn’t failure. It’s a way of protecting the relationship from a pattern you can’t break alone. If anything about the situation feels unsafe, please talk with a qualified professional."],"p":"The program isn’t therapy or mediation. It’s a practical, shared way to see the load and talk kindly, and it sits happily alongside professional support.","go":["/is-this-for-you.html","Is this right for you?"],"see":["what-a-mediator-does","informal-mediation-between-friends-or-family"],"ev":"General guidance from mediation and counseling practice. There’s no single rule; the right help depends on the situation.","ex":"“We keep getting stuck on this. Would you be open to having someone neutral help us talk it through?”","f":"mediation"},{"id":"fighting-fair","t":"Fighting fair","a":["fair fighting","fighting fair rules","argue respectfully"],"x":["Fighting fair means disagreeing in a way that protects the relationship: talking about the problem, not the person’s character; no name-calling or contempt; no bringing up every old grievance; and taking breaks when things get too hot.","Disagreement itself isn’t the problem. Many close relationships have plenty of it. What matters most is how it’s done, and whether repair happens afterward."],"p":"The Library’s entries on the four horsemen and repair attempts go deeper. The Check-ins ground rules are a ready-made fair-fighting list.","go":["/check-ins.html#ground","Check-in ground rules"],"see":["ground-rules-for-a-tough-talk","conflict-escalation-ladder"],"ev":"“Fair fighting” lists come from popular psychology and couples work. The most consistent research-backed pieces are avoiding contempt and criticism of the person, and taking breaks when flooded.","ex":"Agree three rules today: one topic, no name-calling, and a twenty-minute break when either of you asks.","f":"mediation"},{"id":"neutral-language","t":"Neutral language","a":["neutral words","loaded words","blame words"],"x":["Some words reliably raise the temperature: always, never, lazy, selfish, ridiculous, typical. Neutral language describes what happened without adding judgments or labels.","It isn’t about being cold. You can still say how you feel. It’s about making sure the facts part of your message is something the other person can agree is true."],"p":"WP-09 asks for the fact “in one sentence with no adjectives”. The Conversation Reader flags absolutes like “always” and “never”.","go":["/signal-translator.html","The Signal Translator"],"see":["reframing-in-mediation","feelings-versus-thoughts"],"ev":"Communication and mediation training consistently recommend avoiding absolutes and labels. The Conversation Reader looks for the same kinds of words.","ex":"Swap “You’re so lazy about the dishes” for “The dishes from last night are still in the sink.”","f":"mediation"},{"id":"family-meetings","t":"Family meetings","a":["family meeting","household meeting","house meeting"],"x":["A family meeting is a short, regular sit-down where everyone (kids included, at their level) looks at the week ahead, shares appreciations and solves one problem together.","Keep it brief and predictable, start with something positive, and let kids suggest solutions. When children help make a rule, they’re usually more willing to follow it."],"p":"Use it to update WP-03 (one owner per job) and to agree age-appropriate jobs. For very busy families, even ten minutes a week helps.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["retrospective-for-households","house-meetings-for-roommates"],"ev":"Family meetings are recommended by many parenting approaches, including Adlerian parenting. Evidence is mostly practical, but they give children a voice and make household agreements visible.","ex":"Sunday, 20 minutes: something good from the week, the calendar ahead, one problem to solve together, and a treat.","f":"mediation"},{"id":"money-dates","t":"Money dates","a":["money date","money check-in","monthly money talk"],"x":["A money date is a regular, relaxed time to look at finances together: what came in, what went out, upcoming costs and shared goals. Making it routine takes the dread out of money talk.","Keeping it calm, short and paired with something pleasant helps. It isn’t the time for blame about past spending."],"p":"Frequency Calibration includes money as one of five rhythms. The Lemonade Stand’s money side shows shared costs as plain facts.","go":["/library/conflict.html#money-disagreements","Money disagreements (Library)"],"see":["yours-mine-and-ours-accounts","talking-about-debt-without-shame"],"ev":"A popular practice recommended by many financial educators. Research suggests couples who communicate about money report less financial conflict.","ex":"First Sunday of the month: coffee, the bank statements, one goal, and something nice afterward.","f":"money-conversations"},{"id":"yours-mine-and-ours-accounts","t":"Yours, mine and ours accounts","a":["joint accounts","separate accounts","yours mine and ours"],"x":["Some couples pool everything; some keep everything separate; many do a mix: a shared pot for joint costs plus personal money each. Each can work.","What matters most is that both people understand and agree with the arrangement, and that it’s revisited when life changes."],"p":"The Library’s money meanings entry explains why money arrangements can feel emotional.","go":["/library/life.html#money-meanings","What money means to us (Library)"],"see":["money-dates","money-and-fairness-splits"],"ev":"Couples use many arrangements. Some research suggests joint accounts are linked with relationship satisfaction, but no single arrangement fits everyone.","ex":"A shared account for bills and household costs, plus a personal account each for individual spending.","f":"money-conversations"},{"id":"spending-thresholds","t":"Spending thresholds","a":["spending limit","check before buying","big purchase rule"],"x":["A spending threshold is an agreed amount above which you check with each other before buying. It prevents surprises without needing approval for every coffee.","The number depends on your budget. The point is agreeing it together."],"p":"This is a “default decision”: decided once, then followed without re-negotiating.","go":["/library/conflict.html#money-disagreements","Money disagreements (Library)"],"see":["money-dates","yours-mine-and-ours-accounts"],"ev":"A common practical agreement suggested by financial educators. It reduces surprise purchases, a frequent source of conflict.","ex":"“Anything over $200 that isn’t a regular bill, we check with each other first.”","f":"money-conversations"},{"id":"money-histories","t":"Money histories","a":["money scripts","money beliefs","how we grew up with money"],"x":["We learn attitudes to money early: whether it’s scarce or plentiful, talked about or hidden, a source of security or stress. Two people with different money histories can see the same purchase very differently.","Sharing your money histories, without judging, builds understanding for later money conversations."],"p":"The Library’s money meanings entry covers why money is rarely just about money.","go":["/library/life.html#money-meanings","What money means to us (Library)"],"see":["family-scripts-in-daily-life","yours-mine-and-ours-accounts"],"ev":"Financial psychologists describe “money scripts”: beliefs about money learned in childhood. Research suggests these beliefs relate to financial behavior.","ex":"Ask each other: “What did money feel like in your house growing up?”","f":"money-conversations"},{"id":"talking-about-debt-without-shame","t":"Talking about debt without shame","a":["debt conversation","admitting debt","talking about debt"],"x":["Debt can carry a lot of shame, which makes it tempting to hide. But hidden money problems tend to grow, and discovery can hurt trust.","Raising it calmly, owning it, and focusing on a plan together usually goes better than waiting. For complex financial situations, a qualified financial adviser can help."],"p":"The situation advice for raising something hard suggests a soft start and one topic at a time.","go":["/library/conflict.html#money-disagreements","Money disagreements (Library)"],"see":["money-dates","money-histories"],"ev":"Research links financial stress with relationship strain. Shame often makes people hide debt, which can make things worse.","ex":"“I need to tell you something I’ve been embarrassed about. I have some credit card debt, and I want us to make a plan.”","f":"money-conversations"},{"id":"lending-money-to-friends-and-family","t":"Lending money to friends and family","a":["lending money","loaning money to family","borrowing from friends"],"x":["Lending money to people you love can strain relationships if expectations aren’t clear. Being explicit about the amount, the repayment plan and what happens if plans change helps.","Some people prefer to give only what they can afford to lose, so the relationship isn’t at stake."],"p":"This is a boundary about things. WP-01’s kind ways to say no can help if you need to decline.","go":["/library/conflict.html#money-disagreements","Money disagreements (Library)"],"see":["kinds-of-boundaries","talking-about-debt-without-shame"],"ev":"Common advice from financial educators: lend only what you could afford to lose, and be clear about terms.","ex":"“I can lend you $300, and I’d like it back by the end of March. Does that work?”","f":"money-conversations"},{"id":"splitting-costs-with-roommates","t":"Splitting costs with roommates","a":["splitting bills","roommate money","paying back roommates"],"x":["Shared bills are a common roommate flashpoint. A simple, visible system (who paid, who owes, when it’s due) turns money into a plain fact instead of a source of suspicion.","Agree the split method in advance (equal, by room, by use)."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand’s money side is built for exactly this, for two to eight people.","go":["/lemonade-stand.html","The Lemonade Stand"],"see":["shared-or-separate-food","money-and-fairness-splits"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Clear records and due dates reduce disputes in shared housing.","ex":"One shared list of bills, one due date for everyone’s share, and a reminder a few days before.","f":"money-conversations"},{"id":"gifts-and-budgets","t":"Gifts and budgets","a":["gift budget","holiday spending","gift giving budget"],"x":["Gifts can quietly strain budgets and stir up feelings about fairness. Agreeing a budget and who’s buying for whom (often an invisible job) makes the season easier.","Many families find simpler traditions, like a gift swap, reduce pressure."],"p":"Gift buying is kinkeeping, an invisible job worth an owner in WP-03.","go":["/library/life.html#holidays","Holidays (Library)"],"see":["holiday-load","spending-thresholds"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Research on gift-giving suggests thoughtfulness is valued more than cost, though expectations vary.","ex":"“Let’s set a gift budget for the family this year, and agree who buys for whom.”","f":"money-conversations"},{"id":"kids-and-money-conversations","t":"Kids and money conversations","a":["allowance","pocket money","teaching kids about money"],"x":["Talking openly about money, at a child’s level, helps them learn how it works. Some families use allowances; others involve kids in small budget decisions.","Being honest (without burdening kids with adult worries) builds healthy habits."],"p":"The family road encourages age-sized jobs that kids fully own; money decisions can be part of that.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#family","Where families start"],"see":["money-histories","natural-and-logical-consequences"],"ev":"Financial educators widely recommend talking about money with children in age-appropriate ways. Research on allowances is mixed.","ex":"“We have this much for the holiday. Want to help plan how we spend it?”","f":"money-conversations"},{"id":"different-money-personalities","t":"Different money personalities","a":["saver and spender","spender versus saver","money personalities"],"x":["One person likes to save, the other likes to spend: a very common pairing. Both approaches have value. Conflict comes when each treats their own way as the only sensible one.","Agreements that honor both (savings first, then personal fun money) often ease the tension."],"p":"Frequency Calibration maps money as one of your natural rhythms and suggests agreements where you differ.","go":["/tools/frequency-calibration.html","Frequency Calibration"],"see":["money-histories","different-paces"],"ev":"Research (for example, Rick and colleagues, 2011) suggests people often partner with others who have opposite spending tendencies, and that this difference can lead to conflict.","ex":"“You like a cushion; I like to enjoy it now. Could we set aside savings first, then each have guilt-free money?”","f":"money-conversations"},{"id":"no-blame-money-review","t":"No-blame money review","a":["money review","reviewing spending together","budget review"],"x":["A money review works best when it looks at patterns, not culprits. “Takeout was high this month” is a fact to plan around, not an accusation.","Focusing on the setup (busy weeks, no meal plan) often reveals practical fixes."],"p":"The monthly look-back (WP-04) uses the same blame-free approach for household jobs.","go":["/workpapers/wp-04-what-keeps-coming-back.html","WP-04: What keeps coming back?"],"see":["money-dates","retrospective-for-households"],"ev":"A practical application of blameless review ideas to household finances.","ex":"“Where did the money go this month, and what would we like to change? No blame, just the facts.”","f":"money-conversations"},{"id":"anchoring-in-negotiation","t":"Anchoring in negotiation","a":["anchoring","first offer","anchor"],"x":["The first number or proposal put on the table tends to pull the final agreement toward it. People adjust from the anchor, but usually not enough.","In everyday life, this means the first suggestion about a split, a price or a schedule carries extra weight. Knowing that helps you avoid being pulled too far by someone else’s opening, and reminds you to base proposals on something fair and explainable."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand and a week of WP-01 give you real numbers to start from, so the anchor is a fact rather than a guess.","go":["/library/conflict.html#principled-negotiation","Principled negotiation (Library)"],"see":["making-the-first-offer","framing-gains-and-losses"],"ev":"Anchoring is one of the best-replicated findings in judgment research, going back to Tversky and Kahneman (1974). Its size in real negotiations varies with how much people know about fair values.","ex":"If a housemate opens with “I’ll pay 20% of the rent for the small room,” that number becomes the reference point, even if 30% might be fairer.","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"making-the-first-offer","t":"Making the first offer","a":["first offer","who goes first","opening proposal"],"x":["Going first lets you set the frame, which is helpful when you’ve done your homework. If you’re unsure what’s fair, it can be wiser to ask for the other person’s view first or gather facts together.","At home, a first offer works best when it’s explained, not just stated: “here’s what I suggest, and here’s why”. It invites a counter-proposal rather than a standoff."],"p":"Bring the Lemonade Stand split to the conversation, so any first offer is grounded in the week you both actually had.","go":["/lemonade-stand.html","The Lemonade Stand"],"see":["anchoring-in-negotiation","using-fair-standards"],"ev":"Negotiation research generally finds that making the first offer can help when you have good information, because of anchoring. It can backfire if you know little about fair values.","ex":"“Based on the hours we logged, I’d suggest I take the cooking and you take laundry and bins. What do you think?”","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"walk-away-point","t":"Walk-away point","a":["reservation point","bottom line","walkaway point","limit"],"x":["Your walk-away point is the least you can accept before you’d rather take a different path. Knowing it before a negotiation keeps you from agreeing to something in the moment that you’ll resent later.","In relationships, this isn’t about threats. It’s about honesty with yourself: what can you genuinely live with? Saying it kindly (“I can’t do both days this year”) is fairer than agreeing and quietly resenting it."],"p":"WP-01’s kind ways to say no help you express a limit calmly: say why the request is fair, what you have left, and what you can offer instead.","go":["/library/conflict.html#batna","BATNA (Library)"],"see":["anchoring-in-negotiation","the-zone-of-possible-agreement"],"ev":"A standard concept in negotiation teaching. It’s a planning tool rather than a research finding.","ex":"Before a talk about who hosts the holidays, decide privately: “I can do one of the two big days, but not both.”","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"the-zone-of-possible-agreement","t":"The zone of possible agreement","a":["ZOPA","zone of possible agreement","overlap"],"x":["The zone of possible agreement is the overlap between what each side can accept. If there’s overlap, a deal is possible. If there isn’t, you need to change the question, often by adding other issues or looking at underlying interests.","Thinking this way turns a standoff into a search: where could we both be okay? It also reminds you that “no overlap” today might mean “different options”, not “impossible”."],"p":"Frequency Calibration does something similar for rhythms: it shows the gap between your natural pace in each area, and suggests one agreement to close it.","go":["/library/conflict.html#interests-vs-positions","Interests versus positions (Library)"],"see":["walk-away-point","trading-across-issues"],"ev":"A standard negotiation-teaching concept. Useful for thinking, not a measurement.","ex":"One roommate wants quiet by 10, the other by midnight. Anything from 10 to 11 might work for both on weeknights.","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"trading-across-issues","t":"Trading across issues","a":["logrolling","trading","package deals","swaps"],"x":["People care about different things by different amounts. Instead of splitting every job 50/50, you can trade: each person takes more of what they mind less, in exchange for less of what they dread.","This often feels fairer than equal splits, because it respects preferences. The key is being honest about what you actually mind, and checking the overall load still feels reasonable."],"p":"WP-03 is the perfect place to do this: when giving each job an owner, ask who minds it least, not just whose turn it is.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["the-zone-of-possible-agreement","if-then-packages"],"ev":"Research on integrative negotiation shows that trading on issues people value differently often creates better outcomes for both sides than splitting each issue down the middle.","ex":"“I really hate cleaning the bathroom but don’t mind cooking. You hate cooking. Want to swap?”","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"if-then-packages","t":"If-then packages","a":["package proposal","if you then I","conditional offers"],"x":["An if-then package links two things together so both sides get something: “If you do X, I’ll do Y.” It makes a request feel like a trade rather than a demand.","Keep it light and fair, and avoid turning every favor into a transaction. Used occasionally, it’s a friendly way to rebalance things without keeping score."],"p":"Write any package deal into WP-03 so it doesn’t rely on memory, and look again in a month.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["trading-across-issues","one-ask-at-a-time"],"ev":"A practical technique from negotiation training. It’s common sense more than research, and works best when both parts are specific.","ex":"“If you can take Tuesday and Thursday pickups, I’ll handle all the school emails.”","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"using-fair-standards","t":"Using fair standards","a":["objective criteria","fair standards","outside benchmarks"],"x":["When two people disagree about what’s fair, pointing to a standard neither of you invented (room size, hours logged, income share, what’s typical) can take the heat out of it. You’re no longer arguing about who’s right, just about which standard fits.","Agree on the standard before you apply it. Picking the standard after you see the numbers invites cherry-picking."],"p":"Chapter IV, Two kinds of fair, is about agreeing what “fair” means before you argue about it: results, or doing what each of you said.","go":["/library/conflict.html#principled-negotiation","Principled negotiation (Library)"],"see":["anchoring-in-negotiation","trading-across-issues"],"ev":"Using independent standards is a central idea in principled negotiation, described by Roger Fisher and William Ury in Getting to Yes (1981).","ex":"“Let’s split rent by room size, measured in square feet, rather than just arguing about it.”","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"separating-ideas-from-decisions","t":"Separating ideas from decisions","a":["brainstorm then decide","inventing before deciding","no judging ideas"],"x":["When every idea gets judged the moment it’s said, people stop offering ideas. Separating the stages (first list options freely, then decide) keeps a conversation creative and less tense.","It’s especially useful for stuck, repeating problems, where the obvious options have all been tried and argued about."],"p":"WP-04, the monthly look-back, sorts each repeat problem by type first, before deciding what to change.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["using-fair-standards","consensus-and-consent"],"ev":"Recommended in principled negotiation and creative problem-solving. Research on brainstorming is mixed about group idea quality, but separating the stages reduces early defensiveness.","ex":"“Let’s list every idea for the Saturday problem, even silly ones, and only then pick.”","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"framing-gains-and-losses","t":"Framing gains and losses","a":["framing effect","gain frame","loss frame"],"x":["How you describe an option changes how it feels. Framing a change as something you’ll gain together tends to invite cooperation. Framing it as a loss or threat tends to trigger defensiveness.","This isn’t about spin. It’s about choosing honest words that point toward what you both want."],"p":"Starting from a shared goal in a check-in is a gain frame: here’s what we’re building, together.","go":["/library/thinking.html","Thinking traps (Library)"],"see":["loss-aversion","anchoring-in-negotiation"],"ev":"The framing effect was demonstrated by Tversky and Kahneman (1981). The same choice described as a gain or a loss can shift people’s preferences.","ex":"“If we get the chores sorted, we get our Sundays back” lands differently from “If we don’t sort this, Sundays are ruined.”","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"splitting-the-difference","t":"Splitting the difference","a":["meet in the middle","split it 50 50","halfway"],"x":["Meeting in the middle feels fair and is fast, which is why it’s so common. For small things, it’s often the right call.","For bigger or recurring issues, it can produce an arrangement nobody really likes. Looking at what each person actually needs (sleep, time, money, predictability) often reveals a better option than halfway."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand shows the split as a fact. An even split isn’t the goal in itself: what matters is whether both of you agree it works.","go":["/library/conflict.html#integrative-distributive","Win-win and win-lose (Library)"],"see":["trading-across-issues","compromise-and-its-limits"],"ev":"Negotiation research suggests splitting the difference is quick but often leaves value on the table compared with exploring interests. It can be perfectly fine for small, low-stakes issues.","ex":"For choosing a movie, meeting in the middle is fine. For who does the night feeds, look at sleep, work and preferences first.","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"negotiating-with-deadlines","t":"Negotiating with deadlines","a":["deadline pressure","time pressure in negotiation"],"x":["Deadlines can help a stuck decision move, but artificial urgency (“we need to decide right now”) often leads to agreements that fall apart later.","If a decision doesn’t truly need to be made now, giving it a little time, and choosing a calmer moment, usually leads to something both people can stick to."],"p":"WP-02’s advice for a high battery score is to put off anything that doesn’t need deciding in the next hour.","go":["/quick-checks.html#today","Today’s Weather"],"see":["timeboxing-a-conversation","sleep-on-it"],"ev":"Negotiation research finds deadlines speed up concessions, sometimes leading to worse agreements. Stress and time pressure also narrow attention.","ex":"“We don’t have to decide the summer plan tonight. Can we both think and decide Sunday?”","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"negotiating-household-jobs-by-preference","t":"Negotiating household jobs by preference","a":["chore preferences","who minds it least","dread ranking"],"x":["Instead of dividing chores by turns or tradition, each person rates how much they mind each job. Jobs one person doesn’t mind (and the other dreads) are easy wins.","Dreaded-by-everyone jobs can rotate, be shared, be done together, or sometimes be outsourced. The time each job takes still matters, so check the overall split afterwards."],"p":"Put the ranking next to the Lemonade Stand hours, then give each job one owner in WP-03.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["trading-across-issues","chore-charts-that-work"],"ev":"A practical application of integrative negotiation to housework. There’s no strong research on this specific method, but preference-based trades generally improve satisfaction.","ex":"Each of you ranks the jobs from “don’t mind” to “really dread”, then start by giving each person their “don’t mind” jobs.","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"concessions-that-build-trust","t":"Concessions that build trust","a":["concessions","giving ground","reciprocity in negotiation"],"x":["A small concession offered freely (“I can bend on this part”) often invites the other person to bend somewhere too. It signals that you’re trying to find a solution, not win.","Name concessions out loud so they’re noticed, and avoid giving up something that genuinely matters to you just to end the tension. That tends to come back as resentment."],"p":"Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives: fairness and thanks keep things steady. A named concession, and a thank-you for theirs, both help.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["if-then-packages","trading-across-issues"],"ev":"Research on reciprocity suggests concessions tend to be matched, which is why small good-faith moves can unlock a stuck conversation. It isn’t guaranteed.","ex":"“I’ll let go of the weekday dishes thing. Could you meet me on the Saturday cleaning?”","f":"negotiation"},{"id":"explicit-requests","t":"Explicit requests","a":["be explicit","clear requests","say what you mean","direct asks"],"x":["Hints, hopes and indirect suggestions (“the bins are getting full…”) are easily missed, especially by people who take words literally. An explicit request says who, what and when.","It isn’t rude to be clear. For many people, a clear ask feels respectful: it tells them exactly how to help."],"p":"The Wiring Card lets people say “please ask me directly”. Wired Differently explains why hints can miss.","go":["/wiring-card.html","The Wiring Card"],"see":["clear-yes-or-no-questions","curse-of-knowledge-in-instructions"],"ev":"Guidance from autistic self-advocates and communication research on the double empathy problem. Explicit requests help many people, not only neurodivergent people.","ex":"Not “It would be nice if the garage got cleaned sometime” but “Could you help me clean the garage on Saturday at 10?”","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"clear-yes-or-no-questions","t":"Clear yes or no questions","a":["yes or no questions","closed questions for clarity","either or questions"],"x":["Open questions can be hard when someone is tired, overwhelmed or finds decisions draining. A clear either-or or yes-or-no question lowers the effort.","It isn’t about limiting choice. It’s about making the next step easy. You can always add “or something else?”."],"p":"Wired Differently suggests asking about the body or actions (“more like tired, or more like annoyed?”) for people who find feelings hard to name.","go":["/wired-differently.html","Wired Differently"],"see":["explicit-requests","open-and-closed-questions"],"ev":"Practical guidance used in autism-friendly and accessible communication. It also helps anyone who is tired or overwhelmed.","ex":"Instead of “What do you want to do about dinner?” try “Pasta or stir-fry tonight?”","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"processing-time","t":"Processing time","a":["time to process","thinking time","delayed response","need time to think"],"x":["Some people need time to process before they can respond well, especially to emotional or complex topics. Pushing for an instant answer often gets a worse answer, or a shutdown.","Offering time (“think about it and let me know tomorrow”) is a small kindness that usually leads to a better conversation."],"p":"The Wiring Card can say what silence means for you: for many, it means processing, not upset.","go":["/wiring-card.html","The Wiring Card"],"see":["written-follow-ups","agendas-in-advance"],"ev":"Many autistic people, people with ADHD and others describe needing extra time to process spoken information, especially under stress. Individual needs vary a lot.","ex":"“You don’t have to answer now. Can we come back to this after dinner?”","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"written-follow-ups","t":"Written follow-ups","a":["follow up in writing","put it in writing","send a text summary"],"x":["Spoken information disappears quickly, and some people find it much harder to hold onto than written words. A short written follow-up gives everyone something to check later.","It also reduces arguments about what was said, which helps every kind of wiring."],"p":"WP-03 is itself a written agreement. The Workpaper Suite lets you keep everything in one PDF.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["processing-time","written-recaps-after-a-talk"],"ev":"A common accommodation in workplaces and a frequent request from neurodivergent people. Written notes help memory for everyone.","ex":"After a talk: “Just to confirm: I’ll book the dentist, you’ll do Thursday pickup.”","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"agendas-in-advance","t":"Agendas in advance","a":["agenda ahead of time","no surprises","heads up on topics"],"x":["Knowing the topic ahead of time lets people prepare, which lowers anxiety and leads to better conversations. Surprise “we need to talk” moments can be especially hard for people who need to process.","A one-line heads-up is often enough."],"p":"Check-ins are agreed talks at an agreed time, with one topic.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["processing-time","a-simple-agenda-for-a-hard-talk"],"ev":"A widely recommended accommodation for autistic people and others who find surprises stressful. It generally helps meetings run better for everyone.","ex":"“Tomorrow at 7, can we talk about the holiday plan? Nothing bad, just want to decide dates.”","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"sensory-friendly-spaces-for-talks","t":"Sensory-friendly spaces for talks","a":["quiet room","sensory friendly","low stimulation space"],"x":["For some people, noise, bright light, strong smells or a busy room use up a lot of energy, leaving less for the conversation. A quieter, calmer space can make a real difference.","Asking “where would you be most comfortable talking?” is simple and respectful."],"p":"The Library’s sensory entries go deeper. Check-ins suggest choosing a good time and place.","go":["/library/wiring.html#sensory-profiles","Sensory profiles (Library)"],"see":["noise-light-and-stress","processing-time"],"ev":"People differ widely in sensory sensitivity. Reducing noise and visual clutter is a common accommodation and generally helps focus.","ex":"Turn off the TV, dim the lights, and sit somewhere without an echo before a hard talk.","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"info-dumping-as-connection","t":"Info-dumping as connection","a":["info dumping","infodumping","sharing special interests"],"x":["Info-dumping means sharing lots of details about something you love. For many autistic people, it’s a way of showing care and inviting connection, like saying “this matters to me, and I want to share it with you”.","Receiving it warmly, even briefly, is a powerful form of turning toward. It’s okay to say kindly when you need a break."],"p":"Turning Toward is about answering small reaches for connection, and this is one of them.","go":["/turning-toward.html","Turning Toward"],"see":["special-interests-as-connection","parallel-play"],"ev":"Described widely by autistic people as a way of sharing and bonding. Research on autistic social connection increasingly recognizes it as a genuine form of connection.","ex":"When a loved one excitedly explains their interest, try: “Tell me more. What’s the coolest part?”","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"special-interests-as-connection","t":"Special interests as connection","a":["special interests","passions","deep interests"],"x":["Deep, focused interests can be a source of joy, calm and identity. Showing interest in someone’s passion, even one you don’t share, tells them you value who they are.","Shared activities built around an interest can also be a low-pressure way to spend time together."],"p":"The Library’s monotropism entry explains how some attention works in deep channels.","go":["/library/wiring.html#monotropism","Monotropism (Library)"],"see":["info-dumping-as-connection","parallel-play"],"ev":"Autistic people often describe intense interests as a source of joy and regulation. Research on monotropism explores how attention can focus deeply.","ex":"Ask about their interest when they seem stressed. It can be a calming topic.","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"parallel-play","t":"Parallel play","a":["parallel play","being together separately","quiet company"],"x":["Parallel play means spending time in the same space while doing separate things. For many people it’s restful connection: company without the demands of conversation.","It can be a lovely way to be close after a draining day, and it counts as time together."],"p":"Connection doesn’t have to be talk-heavy. Turning Toward includes many low-key ways to be close.","go":["/turning-toward.html","Turning Toward"],"see":["body-doubling","info-dumping-as-connection"],"ev":"The term comes from child development research; adults (especially many autistic people) use it for being together while doing separate activities. It’s a described preference rather than a studied intervention.","ex":"Sit together on the sofa, each reading or gaming, with no pressure to talk.","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"body-doubling","t":"Body doubling","a":["body doubling","body double","working alongside someone"],"x":["Body doubling means doing a task while someone else is present, either doing their own thing or just keeping you company. Many people with ADHD find it makes starting and sticking with tasks much easier.","It’s a simple, kind way to help someone who struggles to get going, without taking the task over."],"p":"For situations like “I can’t get myself to start”, a body double can be a great first step.","go":["/library/wiring.html#adhd-basics","ADHD basics (Library)"],"see":["parallel-play","the-two-minute-start"],"ev":"Widely reported by people with ADHD as helpful for starting and staying on tasks. Formal research is limited.","ex":"“Could you sit with me while I do the paperwork? You don’t have to help.”","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"time-blindness","t":"Time blindness","a":["time blindness","losing track of time","always late ADHD"],"x":["Some people, especially many with ADHD, find it hard to feel time passing. Ten minutes and forty minutes can feel the same until suddenly it’s late.","External supports (visible clocks, timers, alarms, calendars) help much more than trying harder. It’s a wiring difference, not a lack of caring."],"p":"Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person: build timers and reminders into the routine. Wired Differently goes deeper.","go":["/library/wiring.html#adhd-basics","ADHD basics (Library)"],"see":["transition-warnings","planning-doing-and-following-through"],"ev":"A term widely used in the ADHD community for difficulty sensing time passing. Research on ADHD and time perception supports the general idea.","ex":"A visible timer, alarms for leaving (not just for arriving), and building in buffer time.","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"transition-warnings","t":"Transition warnings","a":["transition warning","heads up before switching","10 minute warning"],"x":["Switching from one activity to another can be hard, especially when deeply focused. A warning before a transition (“ten minutes until dinner”) gives time to wrap up.","It works for kids and adults alike, and helps avoid the frustration of being pulled away suddenly."],"p":"The Library’s autistic inertia entry explains why starting and stopping can both be hard.","go":["/wired-differently.html","Wired Differently"],"see":["time-blindness","agendas-in-advance"],"ev":"A common strategy for autistic children and adults, and for people with ADHD. It helps many people switch tasks more smoothly.","ex":"“We’re leaving in ten minutes. Five-minute warning coming.”","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"tone-indicators","t":"Tone indicators","a":["tone indicators","tone tags","slash j","slash s"],"x":["In text, tone is easy to misread. Some people add short tags to show their intent: /j (joking), /s (sarcastic), /srs (serious), /gen (genuine question).","They’re not for everyone, but agreeing on a few within a friend group or household can prevent a lot of misunderstandings."],"p":"The Conversation Reader looks for moments where a message may have been heard differently than meant.","go":["/conversation-reader.html","The Conversation Reader"],"see":["texting-norms-that-prevent-fights","explicit-requests"],"ev":"Tone indicators (like /j for joking or /srs for serious) became popular online, especially among neurodivergent people. They’re a social convention, not a researched tool.","ex":"“You’re such a menace /j” makes clear it’s a joke.","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"recovering-after-socializing","t":"Recovering after socializing","a":["social hangover","social recovery","after a party"],"x":["For many people, socializing uses energy even when it’s enjoyable. Afterward they need time alone or quiet to recharge.","Saying this openly (“it was lovely, and now I need a quiet day”) helps others not read it as rejection."],"p":"Frequency Calibration includes rest and recovery rhythms. The Wiring Card can note what you need after busy days.","go":["/library/wiring.html#introversion","Introversion (Library)"],"see":["recovery-after-a-hard-day","energy-audit"],"ev":"Many people (introverted, autistic or not) describe needing recovery time after social events. Needs vary a lot between people.","ex":"“I had a great time, and I need a quiet evening tomorrow to recharge.”","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"direct-is-not-rude","t":"Direct is not rude","a":["directness","blunt communication","autistic directness"],"x":["Some people communicate very directly: they say exactly what they mean, without softening. Others hear directness as cold or rude. Neither style is wrong. They’re different ways of sending the same care.","Talking about it openly (“I’m direct; it’s never personal”) helps both people tune in."],"p":"Pillar IV: a mismatch is tuning, not a moral failing. The Wiring Card is made for this.","go":["/library/communication.html#directness","Directness (Library)"],"see":["explicit-requests","high-context-and-low-context-communication"],"ev":"The double empathy research and cross-cultural studies both suggest directness is often misread as rudeness by people who expect indirect communication.","ex":"“I say things plainly. If it ever sounds harsh, tell me, and I’ll soften it.”","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"demand-sensitivity","t":"Demand sensitivity","a":["demand avoidance","pathological demand avoidance","PDA","pushback against requests"],"x":["Some people feel intense resistance to direct demands, even for things they want to do. It can come from a strong need for autonomy or from anxiety.","Offering choices, explaining reasons and framing requests as shared problems often helps. This is general information, not a diagnosis."],"p":"Know your own wiring helps you notice patterns like this without judgment. The program’s approach is to fix the setup, not the person.","go":["/know-yourself.html","Know your own wiring"],"see":["explicit-requests","asking-permission-before-giving-advice"],"ev":"Pathological (or persistent) demand avoidance is a proposed profile discussed mainly in the UK. It is not a formal diagnosis in major diagnostic manuals, and researchers disagree about it. General sensitivity to demands is more widely recognized.","ex":"Instead of “You need to do the dishes,” try “The dishes need doing tonight. When works for you?”","f":"neurodiversity-friendly"},{"id":"emotion-coaching","t":"Emotion coaching","a":["emotion coaching","helping kids with feelings","naming kids’ feelings"],"x":["Emotion coaching means noticing a child’s feelings, naming them, accepting them, and then helping with limits and problem-solving. All feelings are allowed; not all behaviors are.","It helps children learn to understand and manage their own emotions over time."],"p":"The Library’s parenting styles entry covers related research. The family road keeps things warm, short and practical.","go":["/library/life.html#parenting-styles","Parenting styles (Library)"],"see":["natural-and-logical-consequences","boundaries-with-kids"],"ev":"John Gottman described emotion coaching in Raising an Emotionally Intelligent Child (1997). Research suggests children whose parents acknowledge and help name feelings tend to regulate emotions better.","ex":"“You’re really disappointed we can’t go to the park. That makes sense. We can go tomorrow.”","f":"parenting-and-friendship"},{"id":"connect-before-correct","t":"Connect before correct","a":["connect before correct","connection first","calm before correcting"],"x":["When a child is upset, trying to correct them straight away rarely works. A moment of connection first (a calm voice, getting to their level, naming the feeling) helps them settle enough to listen.","The same idea works with adults: people hear feedback better when they feel connected."],"p":"Pillar III, Read your state first, applies to children too: calm first, then talk.","go":["/turning-toward.html","Turning Toward"],"see":["emotion-coaching","talking-side-by-side"],"ev":"A popular phrase from parenting educators. It’s consistent with research suggesting children learn better when calm and connected.","ex":"Kneel down, make gentle eye contact: “Hey, you seem upset. Let’s take a breath.” Then talk about the behavior.","f":"parenting-and-friendship"},{"id":"natural-and-logical-consequences","t":"Natural and logical consequences","a":["natural consequences","logical consequences","consequences for kids"],"x":["Natural consequences happen on their own (forget your coat, feel cold). Logical consequences are set by adults but related to the behavior (misuse the tablet, lose it for the evening).","Both teach responsibility better than unrelated punishments, especially when delivered calmly."],"p":"Giving kids real jobs they own (WP-03) pairs with calm consequences instead of reminders.","go":["/library/life.html#parenting-styles","Parenting styles (Library)"],"see":["boundaries-with-kids","emotion-coaching"],"ev":"These ideas come from Adlerian parenting (Rudolf Dreikurs and others). They’re widely used; research generally supports consistent, related consequences over harsh punishment.","ex":"“If the bike is left out in the rain, it’ll get rusty.” Or: “If the toys aren’t put away, they go on the shelf until tomorrow.”","f":"parenting-and-friendship"},{"id":"age-sized-jobs-for-kids","t":"Age-sized jobs for kids","a":["chores for kids","age appropriate chores","kids helping at home"],"x":["Children can help from a young age, with jobs that grow with them. Owning a real job builds skills and a sense of belonging.","It works best when jobs are clear, consistent and theirs, not assigned randomly with nagging."],"p":"WP-03 can include kids as owners of age-sized jobs. The program’s rule: never ask a child to keep track of what the adults do.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["chore-charts-that-work","natural-and-logical-consequences"],"ev":"Parenting research suggests helping at home builds children’s competence and sense of belonging. Specific ages vary by child.","ex":"Young kids: set the table, feed the pet. Older kids: pack lunches, laundry. Teens: cook a meal a week.","f":"parenting-and-friendship"},{"id":"sibling-fights","t":"Sibling fights","a":["siblings fighting","kids arguing","sibling conflict"],"x":["Sibling fights are normal. Instead of acting as judge every time, parents can help kids name feelings and find solutions together.","Stepping in firmly when anyone is getting hurt is always appropriate."],"p":"Family meetings are a good place to set shared rules for common flashpoints.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#family","Where families start"],"see":["emotion-coaching","family-meetings"],"ev":"Research on sibling conflict suggests it’s very common. Parents helping children solve problems (rather than always judging) tends to build skills.","ex":"“You both want the tablet. What could we do so it works for both of you?”","f":"parenting-and-friendship"},{"id":"screen-agreements-with-kids","t":"Screen agreements with kids","a":["screen time rules","screen agreements","tech rules for kids"],"x":["Rules made together with kids tend to stick better than rules handed down. Focusing on when and what (not just how long) usually works better.","Adults following the same rules (phones away at dinner) makes them fairer."],"p":"The Library’s phones-and-presence entry covers how phones affect connection.","go":["/library/calm.html#phones-and-presence","Phones and presence (Library)"],"see":["family-meetings","natural-and-logical-consequences"],"ev":"Research on children and screens is mixed and debated. Many experts suggest focusing on content, context and balance rather than just hours.","ex":"Agree together: screens off at dinner and an hour before bed, and weekend time for a favorite show.","f":"parenting-and-friendship"},{"id":"stepfamily-basics","t":"Stepfamily basics","a":["stepfamily","blended family basics","stepparenting"],"x":["Blended families bring together different histories, habits and loyalties. It takes time, often years, to feel like one family.","Going slowly, keeping some one-on-one time between parents and their own kids, and building stepparent relationships gradually all help."],"p":"The Library’s stepfamilies entry goes deeper.","go":["/library/life.html#stepfamilies","Stepfamilies (Library)"],"see":["new-partners-and-co-parenting","family-roles"],"ev":"Stepfamily research (for example, Patricia Papernow’s work) suggests blended families take years to settle, and that stepparents often do better building a relationship before taking on discipline.","ex":"As a stepparent: focus on connection first, and let the biological parent lead on discipline at the start.","f":"parenting-and-friendship"},{"id":"reaching-out-after-drifting-apart","t":"Reaching out after drifting apart","a":["reconnect with a friend","drifted apart friend","getting back in touch"],"x":["Friendships often fade through busy lives, not conflict. Reaching out, even after a long gap, is usually welcomed more than we expect.","A simple, low-pressure message is enough to start."],"p":"Turning Toward includes small ways to reach for someone, including old friends.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#friends","Where friends start"],"see":["friendship-across-life-stages","supporting-a-friend-through-a-hard-time"],"ev":"Research (for example, Peggy Liu and colleagues, 2022) found people underestimate how much others appreciate being reached out to.","ex":"“Hey, I was thinking about you today. How are you doing?”","f":"parenting-and-friendship"},{"id":"friendship-across-life-stages","t":"Friendship across life stages","a":["friendships changing","friends and new babies","friends after moving"],"x":["Friendships change as lives change. A friend with a new baby, a new job or a move may have less time, and it’s usually not personal.","Adapting (shorter visits, calls instead of nights out, joining their routine) keeps the connection going through changing seasons."],"p":"The Library’s friendship entry covers what keeps friendships going.","go":["/library/connection.html#friendship","Friendship (Library)"],"see":["reaching-out-after-drifting-apart","uneven-effort-in-friendships"],"ev":"Research suggests friendships change with life stages (moves, jobs, children), and that maintaining them takes intentional effort.","ex":"“I know life is busy with the baby. Could I bring lunch over on Saturday?”","f":"parenting-and-friendship"},{"id":"uneven-effort-in-friendships","t":"Uneven effort in friendships","a":["one sided friendship","always the one who reaches out","friendship effort"],"x":["If you’re always the one who reaches out, it can feel lonely. Sometimes the other person simply has a different style or less capacity, and doesn’t realize.","Naming it kindly, without blame, gives them a chance to meet you. How they respond helps you decide how much to invest."],"p":"The friends road suggests saying what you’d like plainly and kindly.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#friends","Where friends start"],"see":["friendship-across-life-stages","reaching-out-after-drifting-apart"],"ev":"Research on friendship suggests reciprocity matters for satisfaction, though what feels balanced varies between people.","ex":"“I love our friendship, and I’ve noticed I’m usually the one who reaches out. Could you suggest the next plan?”","f":"parenting-and-friendship"},{"id":"supporting-a-friend-through-a-hard-time","t":"Supporting a friend through a hard time","a":["supporting a friend","helping a friend","being there for a friend"],"x":["When a friend is going through something hard, being present often matters more than having the right words. Listening, practical help and staying in touch all count.","If you’re worried about a friend’s safety or wellbeing, encourage them to reach out to someone who can help properly, like a qualified professional."],"p":"The Library’s social support entry goes deeper.","go":["/library/connection.html#social-support","Social support (Library)"],"see":["asking-permission-before-giving-advice","validation-before-solutions"],"ev":"Research on social support suggests people value feeling listened to and practical help, and that unsolicited advice can backfire.","ex":"“I’m here. Want to talk, or want me to bring dinner?”","f":"parenting-and-friendship"},{"id":"roommate-agreement","t":"Roommate agreement","a":["roommate agreement","house agreement","flatmate agreement","roommate contract"],"x":["A roommate agreement is a short written list of how you’ll share the home: money, chores, noise, guests, food and how you’ll handle problems. It’s best made early, when everyone’s getting along.","It doesn’t need to be legal or formal. The value is in talking through expectations before they clash."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand shows chores and bills for two to eight people; WP-03 gives each job an owner.","go":["/lemonade-stand.html","The Lemonade Stand"],"see":["house-meetings-for-roommates","quiet-hours"],"ev":"Many universities and housing organizations recommend written roommate agreements. They’re practical tools; evidence is anecdotal but consistent.","ex":"One page: bills and due dates, chores and owners, quiet hours, guests, shared food, and how to raise problems.","f":"roommates"},{"id":"house-meetings-for-roommates","t":"House meetings for roommates","a":["house meeting","roommate meeting","flat meeting"],"x":["A regular house meeting gives everyone a set time to raise small issues before they turn into big ones. It also means nobody has to be the one who “calls a meeting” about a problem.","Keep it short, have a simple list, and end with owners for any changes."],"p":"With three or more people, the Lemonade Stand and one owner per job turn a house meeting into a clear plan.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#roommates","Where roommates start"],"see":["roommate-agreement","family-meetings"],"ev":"Practical guidance from shared-housing organizations. Short, regular meetings tend to prevent issues from building up.","ex":"Once a month, 20 minutes: bills, chores, anything coming up, and one thing to improve.","f":"roommates"},{"id":"quiet-hours","t":"Quiet hours","a":["quiet hours","noise rules","noise at night"],"x":["Agreeing quiet hours prevents a common source of roommate friction. Different schedules (early shifts, night owls) make this especially important.","Include a way to handle exceptions, like a heads-up for special occasions."],"p":"The Library’s sensory load entry notes that some people are much more affected by noise than others.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#roommates","Where roommates start"],"see":["roommate-agreement","guests-and-overnight-visitors"],"ev":"A common element of shared-living agreements. Needs vary with schedules and sensitivity to noise.","ex":"“Weeknights quiet from 10:30, weekends from midnight, and a heads-up for any party.”","f":"roommates"},{"id":"guests-and-overnight-visitors","t":"Guests and overnight visitors","a":["guests","overnight guests","partner staying over","visitors"],"x":["Guests, especially partners who stay often, can change the feel of a shared home and the cost of utilities. Agreeing expectations in advance avoids resentment.","It’s useful to discuss frequency, notice, shared spaces and whether frequent guests contribute to costs."],"p":"A roommate agreement can include guest rules alongside chores and bills.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#roommates","Where roommates start"],"see":["roommate-agreement","quiet-hours"],"ev":"A frequent source of roommate conflict according to housing advisers. Clear agreements help.","ex":"“Overnight guests are fine up to two nights a week with a heads-up; longer stays need a chat.”","f":"roommates"},{"id":"shared-or-separate-food","t":"Shared or separate food","a":["shared groceries","food sharing","eating my food"],"x":["Some roommates share all food, some share staples, and some keep everything separate. Each can work, but mismatched expectations (“I thought we shared!”) cause friction.","Deciding which items are shared and how they’re paid for keeps it simple."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand’s money side can track shared grocery costs.","go":["/lemonade-stand.html","The Lemonade Stand"],"see":["roommate-agreement","money-and-fairness-splits"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Different households choose different systems; clarity matters more than the choice.","ex":"Shared staples (milk, oil, toilet paper) from a kitty; everything else labeled or on your own shelf.","f":"roommates"},{"id":"cleaning-rota-for-shared-spaces","t":"Cleaning rota for shared spaces","a":["cleaning rota","cleaning schedule roommates","bathroom cleaning"],"x":["A cleaning rota assigns shared spaces to people in turn, with a clear definition of what counts as clean. Without one, shared spaces tend to be cleaned by whoever minds most.","A short checklist per space avoids arguments about standards."],"p":"WP-03 turns the rota into named owners; the Lemonade Stand shows how the time actually splits.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["chore-charts-that-work","rotation-or-specialization"],"ev":"Rotas are common in shared housing. Clear ownership and definitions of “clean” reduce disputes.","ex":"Kitchen, bathroom and living room rotate weekly; each space has a short “done” checklist.","f":"roommates"},{"id":"deposits-and-damage","t":"Deposits and damage","a":["security deposit","damage in shared house","moving out deposit"],"x":["Deposits and damage can cause disputes when a roommate moves out. Photos at move-in, a note of existing damage, and an agreement on how costs are split make it much easier.","Check your lease and local rules for anything legal."],"p":"Keeping shared records visible (like the Lemonade Stand’s money side) helps money stay a plain fact.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#roommates","Where roommates start"],"see":["moving-out-notice","roommate-agreement"],"ev":"General guidance. Local rules and your lease come first.","ex":"Take dated photos when you move in, and agree how any damage costs will be split.","f":"roommates"},{"id":"moving-out-notice","t":"Moving out notice","a":["moving out","roommate leaving","notice period"],"x":["When a roommate plans to leave, clear notice gives everyone time to adjust: finding someone new, covering rent and sorting shared items.","Agreeing this early, in the roommate agreement, avoids surprises."],"p":"A monthly house meeting is a natural place to raise upcoming changes.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#roommates","Where roommates start"],"see":["deposits-and-damage","new-roommate-onboarding"],"ev":"General guidance. Lease terms and local rules come first.","ex":"Agree a notice period (like one month) and who finds a replacement if needed.","f":"roommates"},{"id":"new-roommate-onboarding","t":"New roommate onboarding","a":["new roommate","welcoming a new roommate","new flatmate"],"x":["When someone new moves in, sharing the house agreement, rota and bill system early helps them settle in, and gives them a chance to suggest changes.","Inviting their input makes the agreement feel shared rather than imposed."],"p":"Add the new person to the Lemonade Stand and give them some whole jobs in WP-03.","go":["/lemonade-stand.html","The Lemonade Stand"],"see":["roommate-agreement","house-meetings-for-roommates"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Clear expectations early prevent misunderstandings.","ex":"Walk them through the house agreement, the rota and the bills, and ask what they’d like to add.","f":"roommates"},{"id":"temperature-and-utilities","t":"Temperature and utilities","a":["thermostat wars","heating","air conditioning","utility use"],"x":["People really do differ in what temperature feels comfortable, and heating and cooling cost money. Agreeing a default range and a way to adjust avoids silent thermostat battles.","If one person uses much more (for example, working from home all day), talking about how costs are split can help fairness."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand’s money side can show who paid which utility bills.","go":["/lemonade-stand.html","The Lemonade Stand"],"see":["roommate-agreement","money-and-fairness-splits"],"ev":"A common source of household friction. Comfortable temperatures genuinely vary between people.","ex":"Agree a default setting range and when it can change, and consider splitting utilities by use if it’s very uneven.","f":"roommates"},{"id":"borrowing-between-roommates","t":"Borrowing between roommates","a":["borrowing things","using my stuff","shared belongings"],"x":["Borrowing without asking is a common roommate irritation. Agreeing what’s shared and what’s personal makes expectations clear.","A simple rule (“ask first for personal items”) is usually enough."],"p":"This is a boundary about things. The boundaries notes cover saying it kindly.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#roommates","Where roommates start"],"see":["roommate-agreement","kinds-of-boundaries"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Unclear rules about belongings are a common source of small resentments.","ex":"“Kitchen stuff is shared. Clothes, laptops and personal items: ask first.”","f":"roommates"},{"id":"pets-in-shared-homes","t":"Pets in shared homes","a":["roommate pets","pets and roommates","pet rules"],"x":["Pets bring joy but also noise, mess, costs and care work. In shared homes, it helps to agree that the pet’s owner owns all pet-related jobs, and to discuss allergies and shared spaces early.","Agree a backup plan for when the owner is away."],"p":"WP-03 can list pet jobs with clear owners.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["pet-care-load","roommate-agreement"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Allergies, costs and care responsibilities are common points of friction.","ex":"The pet’s owner owns all pet jobs, with an agreed backup plan for travel.","f":"roommates"},{"id":"values-clarification","t":"Values clarification","a":["values clarification","my values","what matters to me"],"x":["Clarifying your values means naming what matters most to you: kindness, fairness, adventure, security, creativity. Values give direction when choices are hard.","They also explain some conflicts: two people can both be acting on good values (security and adventure) that simply pull in different directions."],"p":"The Library’s values entry goes deeper. Know your own wiring helps you understand what’s lasting about you.","go":["/library/motivation.html#values","Values (Library)"],"see":["values-based-decisions","needs-and-wants"],"ev":"Values work is part of several approaches, including acceptance and commitment therapy. Research suggests reflecting on values can support wellbeing and motivation.","ex":"Pick five words from a list (kindness, fairness, adventure, security, creativity…) that feel most like you.","f":"self-understanding"},{"id":"needs-and-wants","t":"Needs and wants","a":["needs versus wants","underlying needs","what I need"],"x":["A want is a specific thing (“I want you to call every night”). A need is what’s underneath (connection, reassurance, rest). Needs can often be met in more than one way.","Knowing your need gives both people more options."],"p":"WP-09’s “ask” works best when it serves the real need.","go":["/workpapers/wp-09-say-it-so-it-lands.html","WP-09: Say it so it lands"],"see":["values-clarification","feelings-versus-thoughts"],"ev":"A practical distinction used in negotiation and nonviolent communication. It helps separate a specific request from the need behind it.","ex":"“I want you home by 6” might come from a need for predictability, which could be met in other ways.","f":"self-understanding"},{"id":"hot-buttons","t":"Hot buttons","a":["triggers","hot buttons","what sets me off"],"x":["Most people have a few hot buttons: situations or words that set off a bigger reaction than expected. Often they connect to old experiences, tiredness or strong values.","Knowing yours doesn’t mean others must avoid them forever. It helps you notice the reaction early, and explain it to others."],"p":"The Calm-Down Kit asks what tends to set you off, and where you feel it first.","go":["/wp-11.html","WP-11: The Calm-Down Kit"],"see":["body-clues-of-emotion","temperament-and-character"],"ev":"“Trigger” in everyday use means something that sets off a strong reaction. Noticing patterns is a common step in many approaches to emotion regulation.","ex":"Write down the last three times you reacted strongly. What did they have in common?","f":"self-understanding"},{"id":"temperament-and-character","t":"Temperament and character","a":["temperament","character","nature and nurture","personality basics"],"x":["Temperament is the set of tendencies you’re born with: how sensitive, active or cautious you tend to be. Character and habits grow from experience.","Knowing which is which helps: you plan around temperament and work on habits."],"p":"Know your own wiring separates three layers: wiring, learned patterns and today’s conditions.","go":["/know-yourself.html","Know your own wiring"],"see":["hot-buttons","birth-order-myths"],"ev":"Research on temperament (for example, Jerome Kagan’s work) shows some tendencies appear early and are fairly stable, while character and habits develop through experience.","ex":"“I’ve always needed quiet to recharge. That’s temperament. Snapping when I’m tired is a habit I can work on.”","f":"self-understanding"},{"id":"self-talk","t":"Self-talk","a":["self talk","inner voice","how I talk to myself"],"x":["The way you talk to yourself shapes how you feel. Harsh self-talk (“I always mess up”) tends to drain motivation; kinder, practical self-talk (“that was hard, let’s try again”) supports it.","Noticing your inner voice is the first step. Talking to yourself as you would to a friend is the second."],"p":"The Library’s self-compassion entry goes deeper.","go":["/library/connection.html#self-compassion","Self-compassion (Library)"],"see":["self-forgiveness","guilt-after-setting-a-boundary"],"ev":"Research on self-talk suggests the way we speak to ourselves affects emotions and performance. Some studies suggest using your own name or “you” can create helpful distance.","ex":"Swap “I’m such an idiot” for “You made a mistake. What’s the next step?”","f":"self-understanding"},{"id":"johari-window","t":"Johari window","a":["Johari window","blind spots","what others see in me"],"x":["The Johari window divides self-knowledge into four parts: what you and others both know about you, what only you know, what others see that you don’t (blind spots), and what nobody knows yet.","Sharing more about yourself and asking for feedback both expand the shared area, which usually improves relationships."],"p":"The Wiring Card is a way of sharing what only you know about how you receive words.","go":["/wiring-card.html","The Wiring Card"],"see":["asking-for-feedback","personal-user-manual"],"ev":"Created by Joseph Luft and Harrington Ingham in 1955. It’s a reflection tool, not a research instrument.","ex":"Ask a trusted friend: “What’s one thing about how I come across that I might not realize?”","f":"self-understanding"},{"id":"personal-user-manual","t":"Personal user manual","a":["user manual for me","how to work with me","personal README"],"x":["A personal user manual is a short document describing how you work best: communication preferences, what stresses you, how you like feedback. Some teams share them.","It’s like a Wiring Card for work: less guessing, fewer misunderstandings."],"p":"The Wiring Card is the program’s version, focused on how you receive words.","go":["/wiring-card.html","The Wiring Card"],"see":["johari-window","explicit-requests"],"ev":"Popular in workplaces as a way to share working preferences. Evidence is anecdotal, but it matches the idea of reducing guesswork.","ex":"A few lines: how I like to get information, what I need when stressed, and what helps me do my best work.","f":"self-understanding"},{"id":"moods-and-cycles","t":"Moods and cycles","a":["mood patterns","tracking moods","my patterns"],"x":["Moods often follow patterns: time of day, days of the week, sleep, workload or particular people. Noticing patterns helps you plan around them.","If mood changes are intense or worrying, it’s worth talking to a qualified professional."],"p":"Today’s Weather keeps a private almanac on your device so you can spot your own patterns.","go":["/quick-checks.html#today","Today’s Weather"],"see":["energy-audit","hot-buttons"],"ev":"Mood tracking can help people notice patterns. It’s a self-awareness tool, not a diagnostic one.","ex":"Keep a one-word mood note each day for two weeks, and look for patterns with sleep, work and people.","f":"self-understanding"},{"id":"strengths-spotting","t":"Strengths spotting","a":["strengths","character strengths","what I’m good at"],"x":["Noticing your strengths (patience, humor, organizing, noticing details) helps you use them on purpose, and balance the natural focus on what’s going wrong.","Strengths are also useful in dividing household or team jobs: people often enjoy what they’re good at."],"p":"Know your own wiring includes the strengths that come with different wiring.","go":["/know-yourself.html","Know your own wiring"],"see":["values-clarification","pride"],"ev":"Positive psychology research (for example, the VIA character strengths work) suggests using personal strengths is linked with wellbeing. Effects are modest.","ex":"Ask: “When do I feel most like myself, and what am I doing then?”","f":"self-understanding"},{"id":"getting-to-know-your-stress-signature","t":"Getting to know your stress signature","a":["stress signature","my stress signs","how I act under stress"],"x":["Everyone has a stress signature: some get quiet, some get busy, some get snappy, some get funny. Knowing yours (and telling others) helps them read you correctly.","It also gives you an early warning to check your battery."],"p":"WP-02 helps you check how much you’re carrying; the Wiring Card lets you share your signature with others.","go":["/workpapers/wp-02-how-much-are-you-carrying.html","WP-02: How full is your battery?"],"see":["body-clues-of-emotion","hot-buttons"],"ev":"A practical self-awareness idea. People show stress in different ways; noticing your own early signs helps.","ex":"Finish the sentence: “When I’m stressed, I tend to…” Share it with someone close.","f":"self-understanding"},{"id":"stress-versus-pressure","t":"Stress versus pressure","a":["stress and pressure","good stress","helpful stress"],"x":["A bit of pressure can help you focus: a deadline, a goal, a challenge you care about. Stress becomes a problem when demands keep outrunning what you have to meet them, especially for a long time without recovery.","The same event can feel like healthy pressure one week and overwhelming stress the next, depending on what else you’re carrying. That’s why checking your overall load matters."],"p":"WP-02 separates what you’re already carrying from what just happened. The Library’s stress entries go deeper.","go":["/workpapers/wp-02-how-much-are-you-carrying.html","WP-02: How full is your battery?"],"see":["everyday-stress-spillover","recovery-after-a-hard-day"],"ev":"Research on stress (including the older Yerkes-Dodson idea) suggests moderate arousal can help performance while too much hurts it. The details are debated, but the general shape is widely accepted.","ex":"“A deadline this week is fine pressure. A deadline plus a sick kid plus no sleep is too much.”","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"everyday-stress-spillover","t":"Everyday stress spillover","a":["spillover","bringing work stress home","stress spill"],"x":["Stress from one part of life often leaks into another. A hard day at work can come out as snapping at home; a tense morning at home can color the whole workday.","Noticing spillover (“this isn’t about you, it’s the day I had”) helps protect the people around you, and helps them not take it personally."],"p":"The Library’s work and home entry covers spillover. Saying your battery out loud before a hard talk is Pillar III in action.","go":["/library/life.html#work-life","Work and home (Library)"],"see":["stress-versus-pressure","transition-rituals-after-work"],"ev":"Research on work-family spillover shows stress from one area often carries into another, including into how partners treat each other at home.","ex":"“Rough day at work. Give me fifteen minutes to reset, and then I’m all yours.”","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"transition-rituals-after-work","t":"Transition rituals after work","a":["transition ritual","decompression","coming home ritual","switching off"],"x":["A transition ritual is a small, regular routine that marks the switch from one role to another, like work to home. It gives your body a chance to settle before you walk into new demands.","It doesn’t need to be long. The point is a clear signal to yourself, and ideally an agreement with others (“give me ten minutes when I get in”)."],"p":"A soft Brain Breaker like Shooting Star, or the Breathe button’s sounds, can act as a bridge out of intense work before you walk in the door.","go":["/soundscapes.html","The soundscapes"],"see":["everyday-stress-spillover","recovery-after-a-hard-day"],"ev":"Research on psychological detachment from work suggests switching off in the evening is linked with better recovery. Specific rituals are personal choices rather than tested methods.","ex":"A ten-minute walk, one song in the car, changing clothes, or a cup of tea before joining the household.","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"recovery-after-a-hard-day","t":"Recovery after a hard day","a":["recovery","rest and recovery","recharging"],"x":["Rest isn’t just stopping. Real recovery tends to come from switching off mentally, relaxing your body, getting absorbed in something different, and having some say over your own time.","What recovers you is personal. Some people recharge alone, others with company; some with quiet, others with movement."],"p":"Frequency Calibration includes rest and recovery as one of the five rhythms. The Night Garden and Pause & Play are made for small recovery moments.","go":["/night-garden.html","The Night Garden"],"see":["micro-breaks","transition-rituals-after-work"],"ev":"Research on recovery from work stress points to four helpful experiences: detaching mentally, relaxing, doing something absorbing, and feeling in control of your free time.","ex":"Pick one thing tonight that is truly restful for you: a walk, a game, a bath, a call with a friend.","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"micro-breaks","t":"Micro-breaks","a":["short breaks","micro breaks","mini breaks"],"x":["Micro-breaks are tiny pauses of a minute or a few minutes during a busy stretch: stretching, a glass of water, looking at something far away, a few slow breaths.","They won’t fix an overloaded week, but they can keep the battery from draining quite as fast."],"p":"The Library’s breaks entry goes deeper. A few rounds of box breathing in the Night Garden make a good micro-break.","go":["/library/calm.html#breaks","Breaks (Library)"],"see":["recovery-after-a-hard-day","energy-audit"],"ev":"Research on short breaks during work finds modest benefits for energy and wellbeing, with effects varying by person and task.","ex":"Every hour or so, stand up, look out a window, and take three slow breaths.","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"worry-time","t":"Worry time","a":["scheduled worry","worry window","worry period"],"x":["Worry time means setting aside a short, fixed slot each day for worrying, and gently postponing worries to it when they pop up earlier. Many people find worries feel smaller by the time the slot arrives.","It pairs well with writing things down: once a worry is on paper with a time, your mind doesn’t have to keep holding it."],"p":"The parking lot idea from Check-ins is similar: a topic waits safely until its time.","go":["/library/emotions.html#rumination","Rumination (Library)"],"see":["worry-versus-problem-solving","a-wind-down-routine"],"ev":"“Worry postponement” is a technique used in cognitive approaches to worry, with some research support. It helps some people more than others.","ex":"Jot worries on a note during the day, then spend ten minutes at 5 p.m. looking at them and choosing next steps.","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"a-wind-down-routine","t":"A wind-down routine","a":["wind down","bedtime routine for adults","evening routine"],"x":["A wind-down routine is a predictable set of calming steps before bed. Doing the same few things in the same order signals to your body that the day is ending.","It’s also a good time not to start hard conversations. Late-night talks often go badly simply because everyone is tired. If sleep problems last or affect daily life, it’s worth talking to a qualified professional."],"p":"The soundscapes and the Night Garden are gentle options for winding down. Pillar III suggests saving big topics for a better time.","go":["/soundscapes.html","The soundscapes"],"see":["worry-time","sleep-and-irritability-at-home"],"ev":"Sleep researchers commonly recommend a regular, relaxing pre-sleep routine as part of general good sleep habits. This is general information, not medical advice.","ex":"Same rough time each night: dim the lights, put the phone away, a few pages of a book or a calm soundscape.","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"sleep-and-irritability-at-home","t":"Sleep and irritability at home","a":["tired and snappy","short sleep and mood","sleep debt"],"x":["After a short night, most people are more irritable, less patient and quicker to read things as negative. Couples and families often argue more after bad sleep.","Knowing this helps in two ways: go easier on yourself and others after a rough night, and avoid scheduling big decisions or hard talks then."],"p":"Today’s Weather gives last night’s sleep a lot of weight, because a short night makes almost everything feel heavier.","go":["/library/stress.html#sleep-and-mood","Sleep and mood (Library)"],"see":["a-wind-down-routine","hungry-tired-and-snappy"],"ev":"Research consistently links short sleep with worse mood and more conflict in relationships. This is general information, not medical advice.","ex":"“I slept badly, so I might be short today. Let’s keep the big stuff for tomorrow.”","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"hungry-tired-and-snappy","t":"Hungry, tired and snappy","a":["hangry","HALT","hungry angry lonely tired"],"x":["A lot of snapping comes from basic needs, not the topic at hand. Being hungry, tired, lonely or already wound up lowers patience for everyone.","A quick check of basic needs before a hard conversation can prevent a lot of avoidable friction. Sometimes the best first move is a sandwich."],"p":"The Library covers HALT and hangry in depth. Today’s Weather asks about body needs like hunger and thirst.","go":["/library/stress.html#halt","HALT (Library)"],"see":["sleep-and-irritability-at-home","irritation"],"ev":"HALT (hungry, angry, lonely, tired) is a popular self-check from recovery communities. Research on hunger and mood suggests low blood sugar can affect irritability, though effects vary.","ex":"Before a hard talk, check: have I eaten? Slept? Had a moment alone? If not, sort that first.","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"movement-breaks","t":"Movement breaks","a":["moving to reset","walk it off","physical activity for mood"],"x":["Moving your body, even briefly, can help shift a tense or flat state: a short walk, stretching, dancing to one song, carrying laundry up the stairs.","A slow walk is one of the calming steps suggested in the Calm-Down Kit for heated moments."],"p":"The Calm-Down Kit (WP-11) asks you to pick two calming steps in advance; movement is a common choice.","go":["/wp-11.html","WP-11: The Calm-Down Kit"],"see":["micro-breaks","walk-and-talk-for-hard-topics"],"ev":"Research generally finds physical activity is linked with better mood. This is general information, not medical or exercise advice.","ex":"When you’re too wound up to talk, a brisk ten-minute walk around the block before coming back.","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"walk-and-talk-for-hard-topics","t":"Walk and talk for hard topics","a":["walking meeting","talk while walking","walk and talk"],"x":["Walking together while you talk combines two helpful things: gentle movement, which can settle the body, and side-by-side positioning, which can make hard topics feel less confrontational.","It works well for planning conversations and for people who find sitting face to face intense. Keep it private enough to speak freely."],"p":"Check-ins say to choose a good time and place. A quiet walk can be one.","go":["/check-ins.html","Check-ins"],"see":["talking-side-by-side","movement-breaks"],"ev":"Walking side by side is often recommended by therapists and coaches. There’s limited formal research, but it combines movement with lower eye-contact pressure.","ex":"“Want to talk about the holiday plans on a walk after dinner?”","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"noise-light-and-stress","t":"Noise, light and stress","a":["sensory stress","noisy home","too much noise"],"x":["A loud, bright, busy environment drains the battery, sometimes without you noticing until you’re snapping. People differ a lot: some barely notice, others are worn out quickly.","Small changes (a quieter room, softer light, headphones) can make a big difference to how a conversation goes."],"p":"The Library’s sensory load entry goes deeper. Today’s Weather asks whether there’s been too much noise or light.","go":["/library/stress.html#sensory-load","Sensory load (Library)"],"see":["sensory-friendly-spaces-for-talks","micro-breaks"],"ev":"Research suggests noise and crowding can raise stress for many people, and much more for some sensory-sensitive people. Individual differences are large.","ex":"Before a hard talk, turn off the TV, dim harsh lights and step away from the busy kitchen.","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"energy-audit","t":"Energy audit","a":["energy audit","what drains me","what energizes me"],"x":["An energy audit is a simple look at what fills your battery and what drains it. Some tasks are draining for everyone; others drain some people and energize others.","Seeing your own pattern helps you plan: put draining tasks when you’re fresher, protect recovery time, and swap jobs with people who mind them less."],"p":"Today’s Weather keeps a private almanac so you can spot your own patterns. WP-03 is where you might swap draining jobs.","go":["/quick-checks.html#today","Today’s Weather"],"see":["recovery-after-a-hard-day","overwhelm-as-a-feeling"],"ev":"A practical coaching exercise, not a research tool. It helps people notice patterns they might miss.","ex":"For one week, mark each activity with a plus (gave energy) or a minus (drained it). Look for patterns.","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"sunday-evening-dread","t":"Sunday evening dread","a":["Sunday scaries","Sunday night anxiety","start of the week dread"],"x":["Many people feel a dip on Sunday evenings as the new week looms. It’s often driven by uncertainty about what’s coming and unfinished tasks from the week before.","A short planning moment earlier in the day, plus something enjoyable after, can take the edge off."],"p":"A Sunday weekly look at the calendar fits well with the program’s weekly closing and the Workpaper Suite’s week-by-week plan.","go":["/quick-checks.html#today","Today’s Weather"],"see":["dread","weekly-planning-together"],"ev":"A popular term rather than a scientific one. Anticipating a busy week often feels worse than the week itself.","ex":"Spend fifteen minutes on Sunday afternoon looking at the week together, then close the calendar and do something nice.","f":"stress-and-rest"},{"id":"eisenhower-matrix","t":"Eisenhower matrix","a":["urgent important matrix","Eisenhower box","urgent versus important"],"x":["The Eisenhower matrix sorts tasks by urgency and importance. Important but not urgent tasks (planning, relationships, rest) often get crowded out by urgent ones, which is why they’re worth protecting.","Urgent but unimportant tasks can often be delegated or simplified."],"p":"Today’s Weather tells you what today is good for; the matrix helps you choose what to do with it.","go":["/quick-checks.html#today","Today’s Weather"],"see":["time-blocking","weekly-planning-together"],"ev":"Named after a remark attributed to Dwight Eisenhower and popularized by Stephen Covey. It’s a planning tool, not a research-tested method.","ex":"Sort today’s list into four boxes: urgent and important, important not urgent, urgent not important, neither.","f":"time-and-energy"},{"id":"time-blocking","t":"Time blocking","a":["time blocking","calendar blocking","blocking time"],"x":["Time blocking means scheduling specific blocks for specific tasks, instead of working from an endless list. It helps important tasks actually happen.","For households, blocking shared time (a weekly planning slot, a cleaning hour) makes sure it doesn’t get crowded out."],"p":"Implementation intentions (in the Library) explain why “when and where” plans work.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["eisenhower-matrix","buffers-between-tasks"],"ev":"A popular productivity technique. Research on planning suggests specific plans (when and where) help follow-through.","ex":"Block Sunday 10 to 11 for household admin, and put it in the shared calendar.","f":"time-and-energy"},{"id":"buffers-between-tasks","t":"Buffers between tasks","a":["buffer time","transition time","padding the schedule"],"x":["Back-to-back plans leave no room for delays, so one late thing makes everything late, and stress climbs. Small buffers between tasks absorb surprises.","They also give time to switch gears, which some people find especially hard."],"p":"The Library’s planning fallacy entry explains why we underestimate time.","go":["/library/thinking.html#planning-fallacy","The planning fallacy (Library)"],"see":["time-blocking","transition-warnings"],"ev":"The planning fallacy (tasks take longer than we expect) is well documented. Buffers are a practical response.","ex":"Add 15 minutes between appointments, and leave earlier than you think you need to.","f":"time-and-energy"},{"id":"weekly-planning-together","t":"Weekly planning together","a":["weekly planning","Sunday planning","family planning meeting"],"x":["A short weekly planning session helps a household see the week ahead: appointments, deadlines, who’s doing drop-offs, what’s for dinner. Surprises cause a lot of friction.","Keep it short and practical, and end with something to look forward to."],"p":"The program’s weekly closing is a similar short review. The daily check-in handles day-to-day.","go":["/workpapers/wp-13-daily-check-in.html","WP-13: The 90-second daily check-in"],"see":["family-meetings","co-parenting-calendar"],"ev":"Practical guidance used by many families and couples. Shared planning reduces surprise conflicts.","ex":"Sunday, 15 minutes: look at the week’s calendar, meals, who’s doing what, and one fun thing.","f":"time-and-energy"},{"id":"weekly-review","t":"Weekly review","a":["weekly review","end of week review","closing the week"],"x":["A weekly review is a regular time to catch up: clear loose ends, check what’s done, and plan what matters next week. It reduces the feeling of things slipping.","For households, the same idea works as a short end-of-week closing."],"p":"REPORT-01 is updated after the weekly closing, so progress builds week by week.","go":["/workpapers/report-01.html","REPORT-01: Your progress, week by week"],"see":["weekly-planning-together","the-done-list"],"ev":"Popularized by David Allen’s Getting Things Done (2001). It’s a practical habit; formal research is limited.","ex":"Friday afternoon: clear your inbox, check your list, note what’s done, and plan next week’s top three.","f":"time-and-energy"},{"id":"the-done-list","t":"The done list","a":["done list","ta-da list","what I got done"],"x":["A to-do list is never finished. A done list records what you actually accomplished, which helps you notice progress, especially invisible work.","It can also help partners see each other’s effort."],"p":"WP-01 is a kind of shared done list: who did what, including the invisible jobs.","go":["/library/motivation.html#small-wins","Small wins (Library)"],"see":["weekly-review","celebrating-small-steps"],"ev":"A popular practice linked to research on small wins and progress. Evidence for the specific practice is anecdotal.","ex":"At the end of the day, write three things you got done, even small ones.","f":"time-and-energy"},{"id":"batching-errands","t":"Batching errands","a":["batching","batch tasks","grouping errands"],"x":["Grouping similar tasks (errands, calls, emails, admin) saves time and mental energy spent switching between them.","It also makes it easier to hand off a whole batch to one person."],"p":"The Library’s task-switching entry explains why switching costs energy.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["time-blocking","energy-audit"],"ev":"A common productivity tip. Research on task switching suggests grouping similar tasks reduces switching costs.","ex":"One trip on Saturday for groceries, pharmacy and post office.","f":"time-and-energy"},{"id":"parkinsons-law","t":"Parkinson’s law","a":["Parkinson’s law","work expands to fill time"],"x":["Tasks often stretch to fill whatever time you give them. Setting a reasonable time limit can make a task feel more manageable and leave room for rest.","It’s a rule of thumb, not a rule. Some tasks really do need more time."],"p":"Timeboxing (in the conversation notes) applies the same idea to hard talks.","go":["/library/thinking.html#planning-fallacy","The planning fallacy (Library)"],"see":["time-blocking","buffers-between-tasks"],"ev":"C. Northcote Parkinson wrote in 1955 that “work expands so as to fill the time available for its completion”. It began as a humorous observation, not a scientific law.","ex":"“Let’s give the garage clean-out two hours, not the whole Saturday.”","f":"time-and-energy"},{"id":"chunking-big-tasks","t":"Chunking big tasks","a":["break it down","chunking","smaller steps"],"x":["Big tasks feel overwhelming, which makes them easy to avoid. Breaking them into small, concrete chunks makes starting easier and progress visible.","Each chunk should be small enough to finish in one go."],"p":"This is Pillar II: change the setup of the task, not your willpower.","go":["/library/motivation.html#procrastination","Procrastination (Library)"],"see":["procrastination-and-the-first-step","tiny-habits"],"ev":"Breaking goals into smaller steps is widely recommended and consistent with research on goal setting and procrastination.","ex":"“Sort the garage” becomes: clear one shelf, then one box, then take one bag to donate.","f":"time-and-energy"},{"id":"shared-to-do-lists","t":"Shared to-do lists","a":["shared list","family to-do list","shared task list"],"x":["A shared list (on the fridge or in an app) gets tasks out of one person’s head. Anyone can add to it, and everyone can see what’s pending.","Adding an owner to each item keeps the list from becoming one person’s job to manage."],"p":"The mental load often lives in one person’s memory. A shared list with owners (like WP-03) spreads it.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["weekly-planning-together","planning-doing-and-following-through"],"ev":"Practical guidance. Shared lists reduce reliance on one person’s memory, a key part of the mental load.","ex":"One shared list for the household, with each item tagged with an owner.","f":"time-and-energy"},{"id":"protecting-peak-hours","t":"Protecting peak hours","a":["peak hours","best time of day","energy peaks"],"x":["Most people have times of day when they think best. Using those for demanding tasks (and hard conversations) makes a difference.","In households, partners may have different peak hours, which is worth knowing when planning talks."],"p":"The Library’s chronotypes entry covers morning and evening types. Frequency Calibration maps shared rhythms.","go":["/library/wiring.html#chronotypes","Chronotypes (Library)"],"see":["energy-audit","time-blocking"],"ev":"Research on chronotypes shows people differ in when they feel most alert. Matching demanding tasks to your better hours is common advice.","ex":"“I’m sharpest in the morning, so I’ll do the tax forms at 9, not 9 at night.”","f":"time-and-energy"},{"id":"different-paces","t":"Different paces","a":["different paces","fast and slow","pace mismatch","out of sync"],"x":["People run at different paces: some talk, decide and reply quickly; others need more time. Neither is wrong, but a mismatch can feel like pressure on one side and stalling on the other.","Naming the difference and agreeing a middle rhythm (a set time to decide, a regular check-in) usually works better than each person pushing their own speed."],"p":"Frequency Calibration maps your natural pace across five areas and suggests agreements. Chapter I explains how small pace differences create a “squeal”.","go":["/tools/frequency-calibration.html","Frequency Calibration"],"see":["impatience","time-attitudes-across-cultures"],"ev":"The program’s Frequency Framework describes pace mismatch as a common, blameless source of friction. General research on individual differences (for example in chronotype and processing speed) supports the idea that people naturally run at different speeds.","ex":"“You like to decide fast and I like to think overnight. Could we agree that big things get a day?”","f":"time-and-energy"},{"id":"team-working-agreements","t":"Team working agreements","a":["working agreement","team charter","team norms"],"x":["A working agreement is a short list of how a team works together: response times, meeting habits, how decisions are made, how to raise problems. It makes unwritten rules visible.","Teams write it together and revisit it when things change. It helps new members settle in, too."],"p":"WP-03 is a working agreement for jobs. The same one-owner-per-job idea works for a team of seven.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#coworkers","Where coworkers and teams start"],"see":["meeting-norms","decision-rights-at-work"],"ev":"Widely used in agile and team-development practice. Research on team norms suggests explicit agreements help, though evidence on specific formats is limited.","ex":"“Replies within a day, meetings start on time, and every task in the tracker has one owner.”","f":"workplace"},{"id":"decision-rights-at-work","t":"Decision rights at work","a":["who decides","DACI","decision roles","decision making at work"],"x":["Many workplace frustrations come from unclear decision rights: nobody knows who gets the final say, so decisions stall or get made without the right people. Frameworks like DACI (driver, approver, contributors, informed) spell this out.","Even without a framework, asking “who decides this?” at the start of a project saves a lot of friction."],"p":"The Library’s RACI entry explains the classic version. WP-03 applies it to household jobs.","go":["/library/teams.html#raci","RACI (Library)"],"see":["team-working-agreements","consensus-and-consent"],"ev":"Frameworks like RACI and DACI are common in organizations. They’re practical tools, not research-backed models, and their value depends on actually using them.","ex":"“For the launch date, who drives it, who approves it, and who needs to be consulted?”","f":"workplace"},{"id":"meeting-norms","t":"Meeting norms","a":["meeting rules","better meetings","meeting etiquette"],"x":["Good meetings tend to share a few habits: a clear purpose, an agenda, starting and ending on time, everyone gets heard, and each action has an owner.","Agreeing these as a team, rather than hoping for them, makes meetings calmer and more useful."],"p":"The coworker road in the Workpaper Suite leans on one owner per job and the Signal Translator.","go":["/library/communication.html#workplace-communication","Workplace communication (Library)"],"see":["team-working-agreements","round-robin-turns"],"ev":"Research on meetings suggests clear purpose, agendas and inclusive participation improve them. Specific rules are practice-based.","ex":"Agenda shared a day before, one person talks at a time, and every action gets an owner and a date.","f":"workplace"},{"id":"round-robin-turns","t":"Round-robin turns","a":["round robin","everyone gets a turn","taking turns in meetings"],"x":["In a round-robin, each person speaks in turn before open discussion begins. It makes sure quieter voices are heard and reduces interruptions.","It’s especially useful in teams where a few people tend to dominate."],"p":"For interruptions at work, the situation advice suggests naming the pattern kindly and proposing a structure like this.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#coworkers","Where coworkers and teams start"],"see":["meeting-norms","being-interrupted-at-work"],"ev":"A simple facilitation technique. Research on group discussion suggests structured turn-taking can widen participation, especially for quieter members.","ex":"“Let’s go around once. Thirty seconds each, no interruptions, then open discussion.”","f":"workplace"},{"id":"being-interrupted-at-work","t":"Being interrupted at work","a":["talked over in meetings","interruptions in meetings","holding the floor"],"x":["Being interrupted repeatedly can leave you feeling unheard and your ideas lost. Often the interrupter is enthusiastic rather than dismissive, but the effect is the same.","Useful moves: a calm line to hold your turn, a private word with the person, and suggesting team norms like round-robins."],"p":"The Signal Translator helps you test how your “let me finish” line might land.","go":["/signal-translator.html","The Signal Translator"],"see":["round-robin-turns","meeting-norms"],"ev":"Research shows interruptions are common in meetings and that some people get interrupted more than others. Practical techniques come from communication training.","ex":"“I’d like to finish this point, and then I really want to hear your view.”","f":"workplace"},{"id":"one-on-ones","t":"One-on-ones","a":["one on one meetings","1:1s","check ins with manager"],"x":["A one-on-one is a regular, private meeting between two people at work, often a manager and a team member. It’s a place to raise small things before they grow, and to build trust.","The best ones are regular, protected from cancellation and led partly by the team member’s agenda."],"p":"It’s the workplace cousin of the daily check-in: small and often beats big and rare.","go":["/workpapers/wp-13-daily-check-in.html","WP-13: The 90-second daily check-in"],"see":["asking-for-feedback","managing-up"],"ev":"Regular one-on-ones are widely recommended in management practice. Research links good manager relationships with engagement, though one-on-ones themselves are less studied.","ex":"Bring three things: what’s going well, what’s stuck, and one thing you need.","f":"workplace"},{"id":"asking-for-feedback","t":"Asking for feedback","a":["requesting feedback","how am I doing","feedback request"],"x":["A general “any feedback?” usually gets “all good!”. A specific question (“what’s one thing I could improve in my reports?”) gets something useful.","Asking regularly makes feedback feel normal, not alarming, for both sides."],"p":"The Library’s feedback entry covers giving and receiving it well.","go":["/library/communication.html#feedback","Feedback (Library)"],"see":["one-on-ones","receiving-criticism-at-work"],"ev":"Research suggests specific feedback requests get more useful answers than general ones. Feedback-seeking is linked with learning, though the relationship is complex.","ex":"“What’s one thing I could do differently in these client calls?”","f":"workplace"},{"id":"receiving-criticism-at-work","t":"Receiving criticism at work","a":["handling criticism at work","taking feedback","defensive at work"],"x":["Criticism at work stings, especially when it’s vague or public. A useful approach: thank them, ask a clarifying question, and take time before responding in full.","You can also ask for criticism to come privately and with specific examples."],"p":"WP-09 helps you weigh what you heard before reacting.","go":["/library/communication.html#feedback","Feedback (Library)"],"see":["asking-for-feedback","defensiveness-as-a-feeling"],"ev":"Research on feedback shows it often triggers defensiveness, and that separating the message from the delivery helps people use it.","ex":"“Thanks. Let me make sure I understand: the main issue is the summary, not the data?”","f":"workplace"},{"id":"managing-up","t":"Managing up","a":["managing up","working with your boss","managing your manager"],"x":["Managing up means helping your working relationship with your manager go well: learning how they like to communicate, keeping them informed and raising problems early.","It isn’t flattery. It’s the same as tuning to anyone’s wiring: finding the signal they receive best."],"p":"Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive, works at work too. A Wiring Card can be shared with a manager.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#coworkers","Where coworkers and teams start"],"see":["one-on-ones","decision-rights-at-work"],"ev":"A common management idea: proactively understanding your manager’s priorities and communication style. Evidence is mostly practical.","ex":"“Would you prefer updates by email or in our one-on-one? And how much detail?”","f":"workplace"},{"id":"handoff-notes-at-work","t":"Handoff notes at work","a":["handoff notes","handover notes","shift handover","project handoff"],"x":["When work passes from one person to another (a shift change, a vacation, a new owner), a short written handoff note prevents things falling through the cracks.","Good notes cover what’s done, what’s in progress, the next step and anything tricky."],"p":"This is the same logic as clear handoffs in WP-03 and in co-parenting: clear owners and a clean handover.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["written-recaps-after-a-talk","team-working-agreements"],"ev":"Structured handoffs are widely used in healthcare, aviation and other fields to reduce errors. Their principles apply to any work handover.","ex":"“Done: X. In progress: Y (next step Z). Watch out for: the client prefers phone calls.”","f":"workplace"},{"id":"disagree-and-commit","t":"Disagree and commit","a":["disagree and commit","commit after disagreeing"],"x":["Disagree and commit means voicing your disagreement openly, and then, once a decision is made, supporting it fully rather than undermining it.","It works when people really are heard first, and when decisions can be revisited if new information appears."],"p":"The Library’s work disagreements entry covers handling disagreements with colleagues.","go":["/library/conflict.html#work-disagreements","Work disagreements (Library)"],"see":["decision-rights-at-work","consensus-and-consent"],"ev":"A management principle popularized by technology companies such as Intel and Amazon. It’s a practice, not a research finding, and works best when disagreement is genuinely heard first.","ex":"“I still think we should wait, but I’ve said my piece. I’m in, and I’ll give it my full effort.”","f":"workplace"},{"id":"status-updates-that-help","t":"Status updates that help","a":["status update","progress update","async update"],"x":["A good status update is short and scannable: what’s done, what’s next, and what’s blocked. It lets others help and prevents surprises.","Regular updates are especially helpful in remote teams and for colleagues who prefer written communication."],"p":"This is the workplace version of the daily check-in: small, regular, and no debate.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#coworkers","Where coworkers and teams start"],"see":["handoff-notes-at-work","response-time-expectations"],"ev":"Practical guidance from project management. Clear, short updates reduce meetings and misunderstandings.","ex":"“Done this week: A, B. Next: C. Blocked on: D (need input from Sam by Thursday).”","f":"workplace"},{"id":"credit-and-recognition-at-work","t":"Credit and recognition at work","a":["giving credit","taking credit","recognition at work"],"x":["Giving specific, public credit for others’ work builds trust and makes invisible contributions visible. When credit is unclear, resentment grows quietly.","Written recaps and clear ownership help everyone see who did what."],"p":"The Library’s appreciation entry and Pillar V (fairness and thanks keep things steady) apply at work too.","go":["/library/fairness.html#appreciation-and-recognition","Appreciation and recognition (Library)"],"see":["status-updates-that-help","thanking-for-invisible-work"],"ev":"Research links recognition at work with engagement and wellbeing. Credit disputes are common in collaborative work.","ex":"In a team update: “Priya did the analysis that made this possible.”","f":"workplace"},{"id":"remote-team-connection","t":"Remote team connection","a":["remote work connection","hybrid teams","working from home team"],"x":["Remote and hybrid teams lose the small, casual moments that build trust in an office. Messages carry less tone, so misunderstandings rise.","Small, deliberate rituals (quick check-ins, video for tricky topics, clear written norms) help replace what’s lost."],"p":"The Signal Translator helps with messages that might be misread. Turning Toward’s small moments work with colleagues too.","go":["/relationships-in-depth.html#coworkers","Where coworkers and teams start"],"see":["status-updates-that-help","texting-norms-that-prevent-fights"],"ev":"Research on remote work suggests people can feel more isolated and misread tone more often in text-heavy teams. Deliberate connection helps.","ex":"Start meetings with a quick round of “one good thing this week”.","f":"workplace"},{"id":"workload-conversations-at-work","t":"Workload conversations at work","a":["too much work","workload talk","at capacity at work"],"x":["When work piles up, a conversation with facts (a list of current tasks and their time) goes better than a general “I’m swamped”. It lets your manager help you prioritize.","Asking “what should move?” rather than “can I say no?” frames it as a shared problem."],"p":"The Lemonade Stand works for teams of two to eight, and shows the split as a plain fact.","go":["/workpapers/wp-03-one-owner-per-job.html","WP-03: One owner per job"],"see":["boundaries-at-work","decision-rights-at-work"],"ev":"Research on burnout links chronic workload imbalance with exhaustion. Raising workload early, with facts, is widely recommended.","ex":"“Here’s what’s on my plate this sprint. If this new project is a priority, which of these should move?”","f":"workplace"}]};
